export const BANK = [{"id":"L02-animals-otters","level":2,"topic":"animals","title":"How Sea Otters Stay Together","text":["Sea otters live in the ocean. They spend most of their day in the water. They even sleep in the water.","When an otter sleeps, the waves can push it away. So otters have a trick. They wrap themselves in kelp. Kelp is a long sea plant that grows from the floor of the ocean. The kelp acts like an anchor and keeps the otter from drifting.","Sometimes otters hold hands while they sleep. Holding hands keeps a family close together. In the morning, they wake up near each other."],"vocab":[{"w":"kelp","pos":"noun","def":"A long plant that grows in the sea.","ex":"The otter wrapped itself in kelp."},{"w":"anchor","pos":"noun","def":"Something heavy that keeps a boat or an animal from moving away.","ex":"The kelp acts like an anchor."},{"w":"drifting","pos":"verb","def":"Moving slowly with the water or the wind.","ex":"The kelp keeps the otter from drifting."},{"w":"wrap","pos":"verb","def":"To wind something around.","ex":"Otters wrap themselves in kelp."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["What sea otters eat","How sea otters stay near each other when they sleep","How kelp grows","Why ocean waves are big"],"a":1,"why":"The text tells how otters use kelp and holding hands so they do not drift apart."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"drifting\" mean?","word":"drifting","c":["Moving slowly with the water","Swimming very fast","Diving deep","Sleeping soundly"],"a":0,"why":"Waves can push an otter, so drifting means being carried along slowly."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why do otters wrap themselves in kelp?","c":["To hide from other animals","To keep warm in the sun","So the waves do not push them away","To find food"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the kelp acts like an anchor and keeps the otter from drifting."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens in the morning?","c":["The otters swim to land","The otters eat the kelp","The kelp floats away","The otters wake up near each other"],"a":3,"why":"The last sentence says they wake up near each other."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks otters sleep on land. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Kelp is a long sea plant.","They even sleep in the water.","Sometimes otters hold hands.","The kelp acts like an anchor."],"a":1,"why":"The text says otters sleep in the water."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["Sleeping Safe in the Sea","A Big Meal of Clams","The Tallest Kelp","Waves at the Beach"],"a":0,"why":"The whole text is about how otters sleep safely in the sea."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"What is \"kelp\"?","word":"kelp","c":["A kind of fish","A small rock","A long plant that grows in the sea","A big wave"],"a":2,"why":"The text says kelp is a long sea plant."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which sentence explains why otters hold hands?","c":["Waves can push an otter away.","Otters spend most of the day in the water.","Kelp grows from the floor of the ocean.","Holding hands keeps a family close together."],"a":3,"why":"The text says holding hands keeps a family close together."}]},{"id":"L04-space-moon-craters","level":4,"topic":"space","title":"Why the Moon Has Craters","text":["The Moon is covered with round holes called craters. Some are as small as a bowl. Others are wider than a city.","Craters form when a rock from space crashes into the Moon. The moment of the crash is called an impact. It blasts dust and stone out in every direction. This leaves a bowl-shaped hole with a raised rim around the edge.","Most of the Moon's craters formed billions of years ago, and they are still there today. That is because the Moon has no wind or rain to wear them away. On Earth, wind and rain slowly erase old craters. Earth also has air that burns up most small rocks before they can hit the ground. The Moon has almost no air, so rocks hit it much more often."],"vocab":[{"w":"craters","pos":"noun","def":"Round holes or pits in the ground made when something hits it hard.","ex":"The Moon is covered with craters."},{"w":"impact","pos":"noun","def":"The moment one thing hits another thing very hard.","ex":"The impact blasts dust and stone in every direction."},{"w":"rim","pos":"noun","def":"The raised edge around something round, like the top edge of a cup.","ex":"The crater has a raised rim."},{"w":"erase","pos":"verb","def":"To remove something so that it is gone.","ex":"Wind and rain slowly erase old craters on Earth."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How craters form on the Moon and why they last","How rockets travel to the Moon","Why the Moon changes shape","How rain forms on Earth"],"a":0,"why":"The text explains how craters form and why they stay."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"impact\" mean?","word":"impact","c":["A kind of dust","The moment one thing hits another hard","A hole in the ground","A raised edge"],"a":1,"why":"The text defines it as the moment of the crash."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why do the Moon's craters last so long?","c":["Rocks keep digging them deeper","The Moon is very cold","It has no wind or rain to wear them away","People take care of them"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the Moon has no wind or rain."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens right after a rock hits the Moon?","c":["Rain falls on the Moon","The rock burns up in the air","The Moon moves closer to Earth","Dust and stone blast out in every direction"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the impact blasts dust and stone out in every direction."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks rocks hit the Moon as rarely as they hit Earth. Which sentence fixes this?","c":["Some craters are as small as a bowl.","The Moon has almost no air, so rocks hit it much more often.","Craters have a raised rim.","Wind and rain wear things away."],"a":1,"why":"With almost no air, far more rocks reach the Moon's surface."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["Holes That Last for Billions of Years","How to Build a Bowl","Rain on the Moon","The Largest City on Earth"],"a":0,"why":"The text is about craters and how long they last."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"What is a \"rim\" in this text?","word":"rim","c":["A deep hole","A kind of rock","The raised edge around a crater","A piece of dust"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the hole has a raised rim around the edge."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which detail best explains why Earth has fewer old craters?","c":["The Moon is covered with round holes.","Some craters are wider than a city.","Rocks come from space.","Wind and rain slowly erase old craters on Earth."],"a":3,"why":"Weather wears Earth's craters away, but the Moon has none."}]},{"id":"L07-games-gravity","level":7,"topic":"games","title":"How Game Designers Bend Gravity","text":["In many video games, a character can jump several times its own height. Real people cannot do that, and designers know it. They bend the rules of physics on purpose.","Real gravity pulls everything down at a steady rate. If a game copied it exactly, jumps would often feel slow and floaty, and the player would have little control. So designers raise the pull of gravity on the way down. The character rises quickly, hangs for a moment, and then drops fast. That shape makes jumps feel snappy and fair. Players may not think about it, but every jump was tuned by hand.","Designers also add small tricks. One is to let a player jump for a few moments after walking off a ledge. Most players never notice this trick, yet they feel that the game responds to them. Designers test many values for gravity before they settle on one. The goal is not to copy the real world. The goal is to make the game feel right."],"vocab":[{"w":"steady","pos":"adjective","def":"Staying the same, without sudden changes.","ex":"Real gravity pulls at a steady rate."},{"w":"responds","pos":"verb","def":"Reacts to something.","ex":"The game responds to the player."},{"w":"values","pos":"noun","def":"Numbers that can be set or changed.","ex":"Designers test many values for gravity."},{"w":"settle","pos":"verb","def":"To decide on one choice after trying others.","ex":"They settle on one value."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is the author's main claim?","c":["Video games are too hard for most people to enjoy","Real physics cannot be used by any computer program","Designers change real physics on purpose so games feel right","Players should avoid jumping because it is not realistic"],"a":2,"why":"The text says designers bend physics on purpose so the game feels right."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"settle on\" mean?","word":"settle","c":["Sit down on","Choose after trying other options","Break apart","Argue about"],"a":1,"why":"Designers test many values and then pick one."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the second paragraph organized?","c":["A problem is described, then a solution","Two games are compared","Events are told in time order","A list of rules is given"],"a":0,"why":"Copying real gravity causes floaty jumps, so designers change it."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens when designers raise gravity on the way down?","c":["The character cannot jump","The character floats for longer","The game gets slower","The character drops fast, which makes jumps feel snappy"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the fast drop makes jumps feel snappy and fair."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders why real gravity would be a poor choice. Which sentence helps most?","c":["A character can jump several times its own height.","If a game copied it exactly, jumps would often feel slow and floaty.","Most players never notice it, yet they feel that the game responds.","The character rises quickly, hangs for a moment, and then drops fast."],"a":1,"why":"This sentence names the problem with exact real gravity."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the claim that designers act on purpose?","c":["Designers test many values for gravity before they settle on one.","A character can jump several times its own height.","Most players never notice this trick, yet they feel that the game responds to them.","Real gravity pulls everything down at a steady rate."],"a":0,"why":"Testing many values shows careful choice, not an accident."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"What does \"steady\" mean in \"a steady rate\"?","word":"steady","c":["Very fast","Too weak","Not changing","Hard to measure"],"a":2,"why":"A steady rate stays the same."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which question would a careful reader ask to check the main idea?","c":["Who made the first video game that used jumping?","How tall is the average player of these games?","How much does a game cost to make and sell?","Why would exact real gravity make a game feel worse?"],"a":3,"why":"The text answers this question, and it tests the main idea."}]},{"id":"L09-people-printing-press","level":9,"topic":"people","title":"How the Printing Press Changed Who Could Read","text":["Before the 1400s, every book in Europe was copied by hand. A scribe might spend months on a single copy, so books were rare and costly. Most of the people who owned them belonged to churches, universities or wealthy families.","Around 1450, a German metalworker named Johannes Gutenberg built a press that used movable metal type. Each letter was a separate piece that could be arranged into words, inked, and pressed onto paper. When a page was finished, the letters could be rearranged to make a new page. This was far faster than copying by hand.","Within a few decades, printers had opened shops in cities across Europe, and millions of books had been produced. As the supply grew, prices fell. Printed copies of a book were all alike, which made it easier for readers in different places to share the same text. Ordinary merchants and craftspeople could begin to afford books, and a growing number of them learned to read.","The change was gradual, not sudden. Many people still could not read for centuries, and the first printed books copied the style of handwritten ones. Even so, ideas now traveled far more quickly than before. Historians often point to the press as one reason new ideas in religion and science spread so widely."],"vocab":[{"w":"scribe","pos":"noun","def":"A person whose job was to copy books and papers by hand.","ex":"A scribe might spend months on one book."},{"w":"movable","pos":"adjective","def":"Able to be moved or rearranged.","ex":"The press used movable metal type."},{"w":"supply","pos":"noun","def":"The amount of something that is available.","ex":"As the supply grew, prices fell."},{"w":"gradual","pos":"adjective","def":"Happening slowly, in small steps.","ex":"The change was gradual, not sudden."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["Gutenberg became the richest man in Europe because of books","Only wealthy families ever learned to read in those centuries","Handwritten books stayed better than printed ones for a very long time in Europe","The press made books cheaper and helped ideas spread, though change took time"],"a":3,"why":"The text covers the invention, lower prices, wider reading and the slow pace of change."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"gradual\" mean?","word":"gradual","c":["Happening slowly, in small steps","Happening by accident","Happening in one day","Happening only in cities"],"a":0,"why":"The text contrasts it with \"sudden\"."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How does the author organize the text?","c":["Two inventors are compared, and one is said to be better","It moves from life before the press to the press and its effects","Ten facts about printing are listed in order of how important they are","One day in a busy print shop is told hour by hour"],"a":1,"why":"The paragraphs move from before, to the invention, to the results."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, what caused book prices to fall?","c":["Scribes worked harder","Churches gave books away","The supply of books grew","Paper became free"],"a":2,"why":"The text says that as the supply grew, prices fell."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader concludes that everyone could read soon after 1450. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Many people still could not read for centuries.","Printers opened shops in many cities.","Millions of books had been produced.","Gutenberg built a press around 1450."],"a":0,"why":"The final paragraph says many people could not read for centuries."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that books became easier to get?","c":["A scribe might spend months on a single copy.","Each letter was a separate piece that could be arranged into words, inked, and pressed onto paper.","Printers had opened shops in cities across Europe, and millions of books had been produced.","Gutenberg was a German metalworker."],"a":2,"why":"Many shops and millions of books show that books were far easier to get."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In \"movable metal type,\" what does \"movable\" suggest about the letters?","word":"movable","c":["They were very heavy","They were made of paper","They could not be changed","They could be rearranged"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the letters could be rearranged to make a new page."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which inference is best supported by the text?","c":["Ordinary people could get books mainly because books became cheaper.","Scribes lost all of their work on the very day that the press was built.","Every printed book contained a brand new kind of story for readers.","Only churches and universities wanted to buy the printed books."],"a":0,"why":"Prices fell, so merchants and craftspeople could afford books."}]},{"id":"L01-vehicles-train-wheels","level":1,"topic":"vehicles","title":"Wheels on the Rails","text":["A train rides on a track. The track has two long rails. The rails are made of steel.","Each train wheel has a lip on its inside edge. The lip is called a flange. It hugs the rail. It keeps the wheel from sliding off.","First, the engine starts to pull. Then the cars roll behind it. The flanges keep every wheel on the track."],"vocab":[{"w":"rails","pos":"noun","def":"Long steel bars that a train rolls on.","ex":"The track has two long rails."},{"w":"flange","pos":"noun","def":"A lip on the inside edge of a train wheel.","ex":"The lip is called a flange."},{"w":"hugs","pos":"verb","def":"Stays close against something.","ex":"The flange hugs the rail."},{"w":"engine","pos":"noun","def":"The machine that pulls a train.","ex":"First, the engine starts to pull."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How trains get painted","How train wheels stay on the track","Where trains go at night","How fast trains can go"],"a":1,"why":"The text tells how the flange keeps each wheel on the rail."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"flange\"?","word":"flange","c":["A lip on the edge of a wheel","A kind of train car","A long steel bar","A loud train horn"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the lip on the wheel is called a flange."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens first when the train starts to move?","c":["The cars roll","The wheels slide off","The engine starts to pull","The rails move"],"a":2,"why":"The text says first, the engine starts to pull."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens right after the engine starts to pull?","c":["The rails turn to steel","The wheels slide off","The engine stops","The cars roll behind it"],"a":3,"why":"The text says then the cars roll behind it."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a track has only one rail. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["The lip is called a flange.","The track has two long rails.","It hugs the rail.","Then the cars roll behind it."],"a":1,"why":"The text says the track has two long rails."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["Staying on the Track","Trains at the Station","A Trip to the Sea","Building a Car"],"a":0,"why":"The whole text is about how wheels stay on the track."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"The flange \"hugs\" the rail. What does \"hugs\" mean here?","word":"hugs","c":["Pushes away from","Jumps over","Stays close against","Breaks off"],"a":2,"why":"The flange stays close to the rail so the wheel does not slide off."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which sentence explains why the wheels do not slide off?","c":["The rails are made of steel.","A train rides on a track.","Then the cars roll behind it.","The flanges keep every wheel on the track."],"a":3,"why":"The text says the flanges keep every wheel on the track."}]},{"id":"L01-dinosaurs-eggs","level":1,"topic":"dinosaurs","title":"Inside a Dinosaur Egg","text":["Baby dinosaurs came from eggs. A mother dinosaur laid her eggs in a nest. Some nests were holes dug in the dirt.","A tiny dinosaur grew inside each egg. Then it broke out of the shell. Breaking out is called hatching.","Some dinosaur eggs were round like a ball. Others were long and thin. Today, scientists dig up eggs that turned to stone. Stone eggs like these are called fossils."],"vocab":[{"w":"nest","pos":"noun","def":"A place where an animal lays its eggs.","ex":"A mother dinosaur laid her eggs in a nest."},{"w":"hatching","pos":"noun","def":"Breaking out of an egg.","ex":"Breaking out is called hatching."},{"w":"shell","pos":"noun","def":"The outside cover of an egg.","ex":"Then it broke out of the shell."},{"w":"fossils","pos":"noun","def":"Parts of plants or animals from long ago that turned to stone.","ex":"Stone eggs like these are called fossils."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How dinosaurs ran fast","What dinosaurs liked to eat","Dinosaur eggs and baby dinosaurs","Why dinosaurs were so big"],"a":2,"why":"The text tells about nests, eggs, hatching and egg fossils."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"hatching\" mean?","word":"hatching","c":["Laying an egg in a nest","Breaking out of an egg","Digging a hole in dirt","Turning into stone"],"a":1,"why":"The text says breaking out is called hatching."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happened after a tiny dinosaur grew inside the egg?","c":["It broke out of the shell","It dug a new nest","It turned to stone","It laid more eggs"],"a":0,"why":"The text says then it broke out of the shell."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why are some very old eggs called fossils?","c":["They are very small","They are round","They hatched long ago","They turned to stone"],"a":3,"why":"The text says eggs that turned to stone are called fossils."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks all dinosaur eggs were round. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Baby dinosaurs came from eggs.","A tiny dinosaur grew inside each egg.","Breaking out is called hatching.","Others were long and thin."],"a":3,"why":"The text says some eggs were round and others were long and thin."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["A Dinosaur Lunch","The Biggest Dinosaur","Starting Life in a Shell","Dinosaurs That Could Fly"],"a":2,"why":"The text is about baby dinosaurs growing in eggs and hatching."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"nest\"?","word":"nest","c":["A kind of stone","A place where eggs are laid","A baby dinosaur","A hard shell"],"a":1,"why":"The text says a mother dinosaur laid her eggs in a nest."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure what a fossil is. Which sentence helps most?","c":["Stone eggs like these are called fossils.","Some nests were holes dug in the dirt.","Then it broke out of the shell.","Baby dinosaurs came from eggs."],"a":0,"why":"This sentence tells that eggs turned to stone are called fossils."}]},{"id":"L01-food-bread-dough","level":1,"topic":"food","title":"From Flour to Bread","text":["First, a baker mixes flour, water and yeast. This soft mix is called dough.","Yeast is a tiny living thing. It makes little bubbles of gas. The bubbles make the dough rise. The dough grows big and puffy.","Then the baker puts the dough in a hot oven. The heat bakes it into bread. Last, the bread cools. Now it is ready to eat."],"vocab":[{"w":"dough","pos":"noun","def":"A soft mix of flour and water that is baked into bread.","ex":"This soft mix is called dough."},{"w":"yeast","pos":"noun","def":"A tiny living thing that makes bubbles in dough.","ex":"Yeast is a tiny living thing."},{"w":"rise","pos":"verb","def":"To go up and get bigger.","ex":"The bubbles make the dough rise."},{"w":"puffy","pos":"adjective","def":"Soft and full of air.","ex":"The dough grows big and puffy."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["Where flour comes from","Why bread is good for you","How to slice bread","How bread is made"],"a":3,"why":"The text tells the steps a baker follows to make bread."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is \"yeast\"?","word":"yeast","c":["A kind of flour","A hot oven","A tiny living thing","A big bubble"],"a":2,"why":"The text says yeast is a tiny living thing."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does the dough rise?","c":["The oven is cold","Bubbles of gas from the yeast","The baker presses on it","The bread cools down"],"a":1,"why":"The text says the yeast makes bubbles and the bubbles make the dough rise."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens after the dough goes in the oven?","c":["The heat bakes it into bread","The baker adds more water","The yeast is mixed in","The dough turns into flour"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the heat bakes it into bread."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks yeast is a kind of rock. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Yeast is a tiny living thing.","The heat bakes it into bread.","Last, the bread cools.","This soft mix is called dough."],"a":0,"why":"The text says yeast is a living thing, not a rock."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["A Trip to the Farm","Cold Bread for Lunch","Fun with Fresh Fruit","Mix, Rise, Bake"],"a":3,"why":"The text tells how dough is mixed, rises and is baked."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"puffy\" mean?","word":"puffy","c":["Hard and flat","Wet and cold","Soft and full of air","Small and dry"],"a":2,"why":"Bubbles of gas fill the dough, so it gets soft and full of air."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders what the baker mixes first. Which sentence helps?","c":["The dough grows big and puffy.","First, a baker mixes flour, water and yeast.","The heat bakes it into bread.","It makes little bubbles of gas."],"a":1,"why":"The first sentence names what the baker mixes."}]},{"id":"L02-ocean-tide-pools","level":2,"topic":"ocean","title":"Little Pools by the Sea","text":["The ocean rises and falls each day. This rise and fall is called the tide. At high tide, water covers the rocks. At low tide, the water moves back.","When the water moves back, some stays behind. It fills little holes in the rocks. These small pools are called tide pools.","Many animals live in tide pools. Crabs hide under rocks. Sea stars hold on tight with tiny tube feet. Sea anemones close up like a fist. This keeps water inside their soft bodies. Soon the tide comes back in."],"vocab":[{"w":"tide","pos":"noun","def":"The rise and fall of ocean water each day.","ex":"This rise and fall is called the tide."},{"w":"rises","pos":"verb","def":"Goes up.","ex":"The ocean rises and falls each day."},{"w":"anemones","pos":"noun","def":"Soft sea animals that can close up like a fist.","ex":"Sea anemones close up like a fist."},{"w":"tight","pos":"adverb","def":"Firmly, without letting go.","ex":"Sea stars hold on tight with tiny tube feet."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["Tide pools and the animals in them","How to swim safely in the ocean","Why sand at the beach is soft","How big ocean waves are made"],"a":0,"why":"The text tells how tide pools form and which animals live there."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is the \"tide\"?","word":"tide","c":["A kind of crab","A small hole in a rock","A sea animal with feet","The rise and fall of ocean water"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the rise and fall of the ocean is called the tide."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How does a tide pool form?","c":["Crabs dig deep holes in the sand","Water stays in holes in the rocks","Rain fills up holes on the beach","Sea stars build a home"],"a":1,"why":"The text says some water stays behind and fills little holes in the rocks."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why do sea anemones close up?","c":["To catch a crab","To hide from the sun","To keep water inside their bodies","To swim away fast"],"a":2,"why":"The text says closing up keeps water inside their soft bodies."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the ocean always stays at the same height. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Crabs hide under rocks.","Many animals live in tide pools.","The ocean rises and falls each day.","Sea anemones close up like a fist."],"a":2,"why":"The text says the ocean rises and falls each day."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that tide pools are full of life?","c":["Many animals live in tide pools.","At high tide, water covers the rocks.","It fills little holes in the rocks.","Soon the tide comes back in."],"a":0,"why":"This sentence says many animals live in tide pools."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"Sea stars \"hold on tight.\" What does \"tight\" mean here?","word":"tight","c":["Very loosely","Only for a moment","With a soft splash","Firmly, without letting go"],"a":3,"why":"Holding on tight means holding firmly so the sea star stays in place."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders when tide pools appear. Which sentence helps most?","c":["Crabs hide under rocks.","When the water moves back, some stays behind.","Sea stars hold on tight with tiny tube feet.","This keeps water inside their soft bodies."],"a":1,"why":"Tide pools appear when the water moves back and some stays behind."}]},{"id":"L02-music-drums","level":2,"topic":"music","title":"The Beat Inside a Drum","text":["A drum has a thin sheet stretched over the top. This top part is called the drumhead. A drummer hits the drumhead with a stick. Then the drumhead shakes back and forth. It shakes very fast.","This fast shaking is called vibration. The vibration pushes on the air around the drum. The air carries the sound to our ears.","Different drums make different sounds. A big drum makes a low, deep boom. A small drum makes a higher sound. A drummer can also pull the drumhead tighter. A tighter drumhead makes a higher sound too."],"vocab":[{"w":"drumhead","pos":"noun","def":"The thin sheet on top of a drum that you hit.","ex":"A drummer hits the drumhead with a stick."},{"w":"vibration","pos":"noun","def":"Very fast shaking back and forth.","ex":"This fast shaking is called vibration."},{"w":"stretched","pos":"verb","def":"Pulled out flat and tight over something.","ex":"A drum has a thin sheet stretched over the top."},{"w":"deep","pos":"adjective","def":"Low in sound.","ex":"A big drum makes a low, deep boom."},{"w":"tighter","pos":"adjective","def":"Pulled more firmly, with less give.","ex":"A drummer can pull the drumhead tighter."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to buy a new drum","How drums make sound","Who made the first drum","Why bands march in parades"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains how a drum shakes, moves the air and makes sound."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"vibration\" mean?","word":"vibration","c":["A wooden drumstick","A low boom","Very fast shaking","A big drum"],"a":2,"why":"The text says fast shaking is called vibration."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens right after a drummer hits the drumhead?","c":["The drum gets bigger","The air stops moving","The drummer tightens it","The drumhead shakes back and forth"],"a":3,"why":"The text says then the drumhead shakes back and forth."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How does the sound of a drum get to our ears?","c":["The air carries it to our ears","The paint on the drum shines","The drumstick is made of wood","The drumhead stays very still"],"a":0,"why":"The vibration pushes the air, and the air carries the sound to our ears."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a small drum makes a lower sound than a big drum. Which sentence fixes this?","c":["Different drums make different sounds.","It shakes very fast.","This fast shaking is called vibration.","A small drum makes a higher sound."],"a":3,"why":"The text says a big drum makes a low boom and a small drum makes a higher sound."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["A Drum in a Parade","Shake, Push, Boom","The Loudest Band","How to Hold a Stick"],"a":1,"why":"The drumhead shakes, pushes the air, and makes a sound."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"tighter\" mean?","word":"tighter","c":["Pulled more firmly","Painted a new color","Made much bigger","Hit more softly"],"a":0,"why":"The text says a drummer can pull the drumhead tighter, so it is pulled more firmly."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure what a drumhead is. Which sentence helps most?","c":["A big drum makes a low, deep boom.","The air carries the sound to our ears.","This top part is called the drumhead.","It shakes very fast."],"a":2,"why":"This sentence tells that the top part of the drum is the drumhead."}]},{"id":"L03-nature-fireflies","level":3,"topic":"nature","title":"Lights in the Summer Grass","text":["On warm summer nights, tiny lights blink on and off over the grass. These lights come from fireflies. Even though they are called flies, fireflies are really beetles. A beetle is an insect with hard covers over its wings.","A firefly makes light in the end of its body. Special chemicals there mix with air and start to glow. To glow means to give off a soft light. This light stays cool, so it does not burn the firefly.","Many fireflies flash their lights to send a signal. A signal is a message sent without words. Each kind of firefly has its own pattern of flashes. A pattern is a set of flashes that repeats the same way. When a firefly sees the right pattern, it flashes back. That is how fireflies find each other in the dark."],"vocab":[{"w":"beetle","pos":"noun","def":"An insect with hard covers over its wings.","ex":"A firefly is really a kind of beetle."},{"w":"glow","pos":"verb","def":"To give off a soft light.","ex":"Special chemicals mix with air and start to glow."},{"w":"signal","pos":"noun","def":"A message that is sent without words.","ex":"Fireflies flash their lights to send a signal."},{"w":"pattern","pos":"noun","def":"Something that repeats the same way each time.","ex":"Each kind of firefly has its own pattern of flashes."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["What fireflies eat on summer nights","How fireflies make light and why they flash","Why beetles have hard covers on their wings","Where fireflies rest during the day"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains how fireflies glow and how they use flashes to find each other."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"signal\" mean?","word":"signal","c":["A message sent without words","A soft light that stays cool","A set of flashes that repeats","A hard cover over a wing"],"a":0,"why":"The text says a signal is a message sent without words."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does a firefly flash back?","c":["It feels too warm in the grass","Its light is about to run out","It sees the right pattern","It wants to scare away birds"],"a":2,"why":"The text says that when a firefly sees the right pattern, it flashes back."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens when the chemicals in a firefly mix with air?","c":["The firefly gets very hot","The firefly flies away","The firefly changes color","The chemicals start to glow"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the chemicals mix with air and start to glow."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a firefly's light is hot. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["This light stays cool, so it does not burn the firefly.","These lights come from fireflies.","Many fireflies flash their lights to send a signal.","A firefly makes light in the end of its body."],"a":0,"why":"The text says the light stays cool."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that fireflies use light to send messages?","c":["A beetle is an insect with hard covers over its wings.","On warm summer nights, tiny lights blink over the grass.","This light stays cool, so it does not burn the firefly.","When a firefly sees the right pattern, it flashes back."],"a":3,"why":"Flashing back when it sees a pattern shows that fireflies answer each other with light."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"What is a \"pattern\" in this text?","word":"pattern","c":["A kind of beetle that glows at night","A set of flashes that repeats","A message sent without words","A cool light in the grass"],"a":1,"why":"The text says a pattern is a set of flashes that repeats the same way."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is confused because fireflies are called flies. Which sentence helps clear this up?","c":["These lights come from fireflies.","Many fireflies flash their lights.","Fireflies are really beetles.","A firefly makes light in its body."],"a":2,"why":"The first paragraph says that fireflies are really beetles, not flies."}]},{"id":"L03-sports-golf-dimples","level":3,"topic":"sports","title":"A Closer Look at a Golf Ball","text":["Look closely at a golf ball. It is covered with small round dents called dimples. Most golf balls have between 300 and 500 of them.","Long ago, golf balls were smooth. Then players noticed something odd, which means strange. Old balls covered in nicks, or tiny cuts, flew farther than new smooth balls. So ball makers began adding dents on purpose.","Why would dents help a ball fly? As a ball zooms through the air, the air pushes back against it. This push is called drag, and it slows the ball down. Dimples help a thin layer of air cling to the ball. To cling means to hold on tightly. With air clinging to it, the ball has less drag. A dimpled ball can fly about twice as far as a smooth one."],"vocab":[{"w":"dimples","pos":"noun","def":"Small round dents in a surface.","ex":"A golf ball is covered with small round dents called dimples."},{"w":"odd","pos":"adjective","def":"Strange or not what you expect.","ex":"Players noticed something odd about old golf balls."},{"w":"nicks","pos":"noun","def":"Tiny cuts or marks on the outside of something.","ex":"Old balls covered in nicks flew farther."},{"w":"drag","pos":"noun","def":"The push of air against a moving thing that slows it down.","ex":"Drag slows the ball down."},{"w":"cling","pos":"verb","def":"To hold on tightly.","ex":"Dimples help a thin layer of air cling to the ball."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to hit a golf ball very far","Where the first golf balls were made","Why golf balls have dimples","How fast a golf ball can spin"],"a":2,"why":"The text explains what dimples are and how they help a golf ball fly."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"drag\" mean?","word":"drag","c":["A small round dent on a ball","Air pushing back on a ball","A long swing of a golf club","A thin coat of paint"],"a":1,"why":"The text says the air pushes back against the ball, and this push is called drag."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why did ball makers begin adding dents on purpose?","c":["Old balls with nicks flew farther","Smooth balls were hard to find","Dents made the balls look nicer","Players asked for softer balls"],"a":0,"why":"Players noticed that old balls with nicks flew farther, so makers added dents."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens when air clings to a golf ball?","c":["The ball gets heavier","The ball spins faster","The ball gets new dents","The ball has less drag"],"a":3,"why":"The text says that with air clinging to it, the ball has less drag."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks golf balls have always had dimples. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Most golf balls have between 300 and 500 of them.","This push is called drag.","Long ago, golf balls were smooth.","To cling means to hold on tightly."],"a":2,"why":"The second paragraph says golf balls were smooth long ago."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["How Dents Help a Ball Fly","Choosing the Right Golf Club","Keeping a Golf Ball Clean","The First Golf Course"],"a":0,"why":"The whole text is about how dimples help a golf ball fly farther."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what are \"nicks\"?","word":"nicks","c":["Small round dents made on purpose","Strong pushes from the air","Smooth spots on a new ball","Tiny cuts on a ball"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the old balls were covered in nicks, or tiny cuts."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders how much farther a dimpled ball can go. Which sentence answers this?","c":["Then players noticed something odd, which means strange.","A dimpled ball can fly about twice as far as a smooth one.","As a ball zooms through the air, the air pushes back against it.","Old balls covered in nicks flew farther than new smooth balls."],"a":1,"why":"Only this sentence tells how much farther: about twice as far."}]},{"id":"L03-art-mixing-colors","level":3,"topic":"art","title":"Making New Colors","text":["Painters can make hundreds of colors from just a few tubes of paint. They often start with three primary colors: red, yellow and blue. A primary color is one you cannot make by mixing other paints.","When you mix two primary colors, you get a secondary color. Blue and yellow make green. Red and yellow make orange. Red and blue make purple. Adding white to any color makes it lighter. This lighter color is called a tint.","Many artists use a color wheel to plan their mixes. A color wheel is a circle that shows colors in rainbow order. Colors across from each other on the wheel are called complementary colors. When they sit side by side, each one looks brighter. Artists use this trick to make part of a painting stand out."],"vocab":[{"w":"primary","pos":"adjective","def":"A primary color is one you cannot make by mixing other colors.","ex":"Red, yellow and blue are the three primary colors."},{"w":"secondary","pos":"adjective","def":"A secondary color is made by mixing two primary colors.","ex":"Green is a secondary color made from blue and yellow."},{"w":"tint","pos":"noun","def":"A lighter color made by adding white.","ex":"Adding white to red makes a tint."},{"w":"complementary","pos":"adjective","def":"Complementary colors sit across from each other on a color wheel.","ex":"Colors across from each other on the wheel are called complementary colors."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["Where tubes of paint are made","Why rainbows have so many colors","How to clean a paintbrush","How painters mix and use colors"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains how painters mix new colors and use a color wheel."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"primary\" color?","word":"primary","c":["A color made by mixing two others","A color that looks very bright","A color you cannot get by mixing","A color with some white added"],"a":2,"why":"The text says a primary color is one you cannot make by mixing other paints."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens when a painter mixes blue and yellow?","c":["They get purple","They get green","They get orange","They get a tint"],"a":1,"why":"The text says blue and yellow make green."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why do artists put complementary colors side by side?","c":["To make each color look brighter","To make the paint last longer","To make the paint dry faster","To make a new primary color"],"a":0,"why":"The text says that side by side, each one looks brighter."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks green is a primary color. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Painters can make hundreds of colors.","Blue and yellow make green.","A color wheel is a circle.","Each one looks brighter."],"a":1,"why":"Green can be made by mixing, so it cannot be a primary color."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that a few paints can make many colors?","c":["A color wheel is a circle that shows colors in order.","Artists use this trick to make part of a painting stand out.","When you mix two primary colors, you get a secondary color.","Colors across from each other are called complementary colors."],"a":2,"why":"Mixing two colors to get a new one shows how a few paints become many."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"tint\"?","word":"tint","c":["A color made lighter with white","A color across the wheel","A color you cannot mix","A color made darker with black"],"a":0,"why":"The text says adding white makes a color lighter, and this is called a tint."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure which colors are complementary. Which sentence helps?","c":["Red and blue mixed together make purple.","Adding white to any color makes it lighter.","Painters start with three primary colors.","Colors across the wheel are complementary."],"a":3,"why":"The last paragraph says colors across from each other on the wheel are complementary."}]},{"id":"L04-stories-comic-panels","level":4,"topic":"stories","title":"Reading the Spaces in a Comic","text":["A comic tells a story with pictures set in boxes. Each box is called a panel. Readers usually go through the panels from left to right and top to bottom.","The thin space between two panels is called the gutter. Nothing is drawn there, yet it does an important job. Your mind fills in what happens in that gap. Imagine one panel shows a player kicking a soccer ball. The next panel shows the ball resting in the net. You never see the ball fly, but your mind knows it did.","Artists also use panel size to control time. A wide panel can make a quiet moment feel long. A row of small panels can make action feel fast. Speech balloons, the shapes that hold what characters say, matter too. The more words a panel holds, the longer it takes to read. So the moment seems to last longer."],"vocab":[{"w":"panel","pos":"noun","def":"One box with a picture in a comic.","ex":"Each box in a comic is called a panel."},{"w":"gutter","pos":"noun","def":"The thin space between two panels in a comic.","ex":"Nothing is drawn in the gutter."},{"w":"gap","pos":"noun","def":"An empty space between two things.","ex":"Your mind fills in what happens in that gap."},{"w":"control","pos":"verb","def":"To decide how something goes.","ex":"Artists use panel size to control time."},{"w":"balloons","pos":"noun","def":"In comics, the shapes that hold what characters say.","ex":"Speech balloons hold what characters say."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How comic panels show the passing of time","How artists draw players in a soccer game","Why many comics are printed in bright color","Where the very first comic book was made"],"a":0,"why":"The text explains how panels, gutters and speech balloons shape time in a comic."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is the \"gutter\"?","word":"gutter","c":["A box that holds a picture","A shape that holds words","The last page of a comic","The space between two panels"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the thin space between two panels is called the gutter."},{"skill":"structure","q":"In the soccer example, what happens between the two panels?","c":["The game ends in a tie","The player walks off the field","The ball flies into the net","The player picks up the ball"],"a":2,"why":"One panel shows the kick and the next shows the ball in the net, so the ball flew in between."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does a panel with many words seem to last longer?","c":["It is printed in larger letters","It takes longer to read","It is always the last panel","It has no gutter around it"],"a":1,"why":"The text says the more words a panel holds, the longer it takes to read."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the gutter does not matter because nothing is drawn there. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Each box is called a panel.","A wide panel can make a moment feel long.","Speech balloons hold what characters say.","Your mind fills in what happens in that gap."],"a":3,"why":"The gutter matters because the reader's mind fills in what happens there."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that panel size can change how time feels?","c":["Readers go through the panels in order.","A row of small panels can make action feel fast.","The thin space between two panels is called the gutter.","Nothing is drawn in the gutter."],"a":1,"why":"Small panels making action feel fast shows that size changes how time feels."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what are speech \"balloons\"?","word":"balloons","c":["Toys that float up in the air","Large panels for quiet moments","Shapes that hold spoken words","Thin spaces between two panels"],"a":2,"why":"The text says speech balloons are the shapes that hold what characters say."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders which way to read the panels. Which sentence helps?","c":["Readers usually go left to right and top to bottom.","Nothing is drawn there, yet it does an important job.","Artists also use panel size to control time.","The next panel shows the ball resting in the net."],"a":0,"why":"The first paragraph says readers usually go from left to right and top to bottom."}]},{"id":"L04-mystery-caesar-code","level":4,"topic":"mystery","title":"A Code From Ancient Rome","text":["People have hidden messages in secret codes for more than 2,000 years. One famous code is named after Julius Caesar, a leader in ancient Rome. He used it to keep his private letters safe from curious eyes.","His method, or way of doing it, was simple. He shifted each letter three places down the alphabet. A became D, B became E, and C became F. The word CAT turned into FDW. To decode the message, a reader shifted each letter back three places.","This kind of code is called a cipher. A cipher changes each letter by following a rule. The Caesar cipher is easy to crack, though. A clever reader can try every shift until the words make sense. There are only 25 shifts to test. Today, computers use much harder ciphers to protect messages."],"vocab":[{"w":"method","pos":"noun","def":"A way of doing something.","ex":"His method was to shift each letter three places."},{"w":"shifted","pos":"verb","def":"Moved something over to a new place.","ex":"He shifted each letter three places down the alphabet."},{"w":"decode","pos":"verb","def":"To turn a secret code back into the real message.","ex":"To decode the message, a reader shifted each letter back."},{"w":"cipher","pos":"noun","def":"A secret code that changes each letter by following a rule.","ex":"This kind of code is called a cipher."},{"w":"crack","pos":"verb","def":"To figure out a secret code without being told the rule.","ex":"The Caesar cipher is easy to crack."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How Rome built its long roads","How a famous old code works","Why letters were sent by boat","How the alphabet was invented"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains how the Caesar code works and why it is easy to crack."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"decode\" mean?","word":"decode","c":["To turn a code back into words","To mail a letter to a friend","To write a letter in capitals","To make up a whole new code"],"a":0,"why":"To decode, a reader shifts each letter back to get the real message."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens to each letter in the Caesar cipher?","c":["It is written backward","It turns into a number","It becomes a small picture","It shifts three places"],"a":3,"why":"The text says he shifted each letter three places down the alphabet."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why is the Caesar cipher easy to crack?","c":["The letters are written too big","Caesar told everyone the rule","There are only 25 shifts to test","It uses numbers instead of letters"],"a":2,"why":"The text says a reader can try every shift, and there are only 25."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks CAT would turn into DBU. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["He shifted each letter three places down the alphabet.","The Caesar cipher is easy to crack, though.","A cipher changes each letter by following a rule.","Today, computers use much harder ciphers to protect messages."],"a":0,"why":"Each letter moves three places, not one, so CAT becomes FDW."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["Letters From a Roman Farm","How Computers Send Messages","Writing With a Feather Pen","Hiding Words With a Simple Rule"],"a":3,"why":"The text is about a code that hides words by following one simple rule."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does it mean to \"crack\" a code?","word":"crack","c":["To break the paper it is on","To figure it out without the rule","To write it down three times","To send it to a friend"],"a":1,"why":"The text says a clever reader can try every shift until the words make sense."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which question would a careful reader ask to check the main idea?","c":["What color ink did Caesar use?","How many letters did Caesar write?","How does shifting letters hide words?","Where did Caesar keep his letters?"],"a":2,"why":"The text answers this question, and it is the main idea."}]},{"id":"L05-tech-stone-arches","level":5,"topic":"tech","title":"How a Stone Arch Stands Up","text":["Some stone bridges built by the ancient Romans are still standing after about 2,000 years. A few of them have no mortar, the paste that builders use to stick stones together. Their secret is a curved shape called an arch.","An arch works by pushing, not pulling. When a heavy load presses down on the top, each stone presses hard against the stones beside it. The weight travels sideways and down along the curve. It ends at the thick supports at each end of the arch, called abutments. The abutments push back and hold the whole arch in place.","Building an arch has one tricky problem. Until the last stone is in place, the arch cannot hold itself up. To solve this, builders first make a temporary wooden frame shaped like the curve. They stack the stones on top of the frame, working up from both sides. Last of all, they drop the center stone, called the keystone, into the gap at the top. The keystone locks the other stones together. Then the builders take the wooden frame away, and the arch stands on its own."],"vocab":[{"w":"load","pos":"noun","def":"The weight that something has to hold up.","ex":"A heavy load presses down on the top of the arch."},{"w":"abutments","pos":"noun","def":"The thick supports at each end of an arch or bridge.","ex":"The abutments push back and hold the arch in place."},{"w":"temporary","pos":"adjective","def":"Used or kept for a short time and then taken away.","ex":"Builders make a temporary wooden frame and take it away later."},{"w":"keystone","pos":"noun","def":"The center stone at the top of an arch that locks the other stones together.","ex":"They drop the keystone into the gap at the top."},{"w":"mortar","pos":"noun","def":"A paste that builders spread between stones or bricks to stick them together.","ex":"A few old bridges have no mortar between their stones."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["Why steel bridges are stronger than old stone ones","How arches hold weight and how they are built","How Roman workers cut big stones out of hills","Why wooden bridges need to be painted often"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains how an arch carries weight and how builders put one together."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what are \"abutments\"?","word":"abutments","c":["The thick supports at each end of an arch","The stones at the very top of an arch","The wooden frames that hold up the stones","The heavy loads that travel across a bridge"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the thick supports at each end of the arch are called abutments."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why do builders use a wooden frame?","c":["Wood makes the finished arch look nicer","The frame keeps rain off the new stones","The arch cannot hold itself up until the last stone is in","The frame becomes part of the finished bridge"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the arch cannot hold itself up until the last stone is in place, so builders use a frame."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What do the builders do right after the keystone locks the stones together?","c":["They stack more stones on both sides","They build a new wooden frame","They add mortar between the stones","They take the wooden frame away"],"a":3,"why":"The text says that after the keystone locks the stones, the builders take the frame away."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the stones in an arch pull on each other. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["An arch works by pushing, not pulling.","Their secret is a curved shape called an arch.","They stack the stones on top of the frame.","Some stone bridges are still standing after about 2,000 years."],"a":0,"why":"The second paragraph begins by saying an arch works by pushing, not pulling."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that arches are very strong?","c":["Builders first make a temporary wooden frame shaped like the curve.","The keystone is the center stone at the top.","Builders stack the stones from both sides.","Some Roman bridges are still standing after about 2,000 years."],"a":3,"why":"Bridges that last about 2,000 years show how strong the arch shape is."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"The wooden frame is \"temporary.\" What does that tell you about it?","word":"temporary","c":["It is very heavy and strong","It is made from many small stones","It is used for a while and then removed","It is shaped like a straight line"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the builders take the frame away once the arch can stand."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is confused about where the weight on an arch goes. Which part should they reread?","c":["The first paragraph, about old Roman bridges","The second paragraph, about how weight travels","The third paragraph, about the wooden frame","The last sentence, about taking the frame away"],"a":1,"why":"The second paragraph explains that the weight travels along the curve to the abutments."}]},{"id":"L05-dinosaurs-footprints","level":5,"topic":"dinosaurs","title":"Clues Left in the Mud","text":["Most dinosaur fossils are bones or teeth. But some of the most useful fossils are footprints. A footprint fossil forms when an animal steps in soft mud or sand. Later the print fills with more mud and slowly hardens into rock. Scientists call this a trace fossil. It is a sign that an animal passed by, not part of its body.","Footprints can show things that bones cannot. A skeleton shows how a dinosaur was built, but footprints show how it moved. A trackway is a line of footprints made by one animal as it walked. By measuring the distance between steps, scientists can estimate how fast the dinosaur was going. For feet of the same size, longer steps usually mean a faster animal.","Some trackways hold the prints of many dinosaurs walking side by side in the same direction. This suggests that some kinds of dinosaurs traveled in groups. Bones alone could never tell us that. A set of footprints is like a photo of one moment, frozen in stone."],"vocab":[{"w":"trace","pos":"noun","def":"A sign or mark that shows something was there.","ex":"A footprint is a trace fossil, a sign that an animal passed by."},{"w":"trackway","pos":"noun","def":"A line of footprints made by one animal as it walked.","ex":"The trackway showed how the dinosaur moved."},{"w":"estimate","pos":"verb","def":"To make a careful guess using facts or measurements.","ex":"Scientists can estimate how fast the dinosaur was going."},{"w":"hardens","pos":"verb","def":"Becomes hard and stiff.","ex":"The print slowly hardens into rock."},{"w":"skeleton","pos":"noun","def":"All the bones of an animal's body, joined together.","ex":"A skeleton shows how a dinosaur was built."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How dinosaurs found food in soft mud","Why dinosaur bones are hard to dig up","What dinosaur footprints teach scientists","How mud turns into sand near the sea"],"a":2,"why":"Each paragraph tells something that footprints show about dinosaurs."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"trackway\"?","word":"trackway","c":["A line of prints made by one animal walking","A road that people built across a field","A bone from the foot of a dinosaur","A tool used to measure long steps"],"a":0,"why":"The text says a trackway is a line of footprints made by one animal as it walked."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How does a footprint fossil form?","c":["A bone breaks apart and turns into sand","An animal steps in mud, then the print fills and hardens","Water washes over a rock and leaves a mark","Scientists press a foot into wet rock"],"a":1,"why":"The first paragraph tells the steps: an animal steps in mud, the print fills, and it hardens into rock."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, what do longer steps usually mean?","c":["The dinosaur was very old","The dinosaur was walking in a group","The mud was very soft that day","The dinosaur was moving faster"],"a":3,"why":"The text says that for feet of the same size, longer steps usually mean a faster animal."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks fossils are always bones or teeth. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Most dinosaur fossils are bones or teeth.","But some of the most useful fossils are footprints.","A skeleton shows how a dinosaur was built.","Longer steps usually mean a faster animal."],"a":1,"why":"The text says some of the most useful fossils are footprints, not bones."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that footprints show things bones cannot?","c":["Most dinosaur fossils are bones or teeth.","A footprint forms when an animal steps in soft mud.","The print slowly hardens into rock.","Some trackways show many dinosaurs walking side by side."],"a":3,"why":"Prints of many dinosaurs together suggest group travel, which bones alone could never show."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"estimate\" mean?","word":"estimate","c":["Make a careful guess using measurements","Know the exact answer for sure","Draw a picture of something","Dig something out of the ground"],"a":0,"why":"Scientists measure the steps and use them to make a careful guess about speed."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure how scientists figure out a dinosaur's speed. Which part should they reread?","c":["The part about how footprints turn into rock","The part about dinosaurs walking in groups","The part about measuring the distance between steps","The part about bones and teeth"],"a":2,"why":"The second paragraph explains that measuring the distance between steps helps estimate speed."}]},{"id":"L05-sports-golf-dimples","level":5,"topic":"sports","title":"A Ball Covered in Tiny Pits","text":["A golf ball is covered with hundreds of small, shallow pits called dimples. The pits are not very deep, and they cover the whole ball. You might think a smooth ball would fly farther. In fact, a dimpled ball flies much farther than a smooth one.","Golfers learned this by accident in the 1800s. Back then, many golf balls were smooth when they were new. Players noticed that old, scuffed balls covered in nicks and scrapes seemed to travel farther. Soon ball makers began adding bumps and patterns on purpose.","The reason has to do with air. As a ball flies, air flows around it. Behind a smooth ball, the air breaks away early and leaves a wide pocket of swirling air. That pocket slows the ball down. Scientists call this slowing force drag. Dimples stir up a thin layer of air right next to the ball's surface. This layer hugs the ball longer, so the pocket behind it is smaller. Less drag means the ball keeps its speed and travels farther."],"vocab":[{"w":"dimples","pos":"noun","def":"Small, shallow dents in a surface.","ex":"A golf ball is covered with hundreds of dimples."},{"w":"shallow","pos":"adjective","def":"Not very deep.","ex":"The pits are shallow, so they are not very deep."},{"w":"scuffed","pos":"adjective","def":"Marked with small scrapes and dents from being used.","ex":"Old, scuffed balls seemed to travel farther."},{"w":"swirling","pos":"adjective","def":"Moving around and around in circles.","ex":"A wide pocket of swirling air forms behind a smooth ball."},{"w":"drag","pos":"noun","def":"A force from air or water that slows a moving object down.","ex":"Less drag means the ball keeps its speed."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to hit a golf ball very straight","How golf balls are painted white","Who won the first golf contest","Why dimples help a golf ball fly farther"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains how people found out about dimples and why they help the ball fly."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"scuffed\" mean?","word":"scuffed","c":["Brand new and shiny","Marked with small scrapes and dents","Painted a bright color","Filled up with air"],"a":1,"why":"The text describes the scuffed balls as covered in nicks and scrapes."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why did ball makers start adding patterns on purpose?","c":["Players noticed that old, scraped balls went farther","Smooth balls were too hard to find in the grass","Players wanted balls that looked different","Patterns made the balls cheaper to make"],"a":0,"why":"The second paragraph says players noticed scuffed balls traveled farther, so makers added patterns."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens behind a smooth ball as it flies?","c":["The air hugs the ball for a long time","The ball spins faster and faster","The air breaks away early and leaves a wide pocket","A thin layer of air pushes it forward"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the air breaks away early behind a smooth ball and leaves a wide pocket."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a smooth ball would fly the farthest. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Dimples stir up a thin layer of air right next to the ball's surface.","As a ball flies, air flows around it.","In fact, a dimpled ball flies much farther than a smooth one.","Back then, many golf balls were smooth when they were new."],"a":2,"why":"The first paragraph says a dimpled ball flies much farther than a smooth one."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["How Tiny Pits Help a Ball Fly","The Very First Golf Course","How to Win at Golf","Making a Ball Bounce Higher"],"a":0,"why":"The whole text is about how dimples help a golf ball travel farther."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is \"drag\"?","word":"drag","c":["A pattern of bumps on a ball","A thin layer of air on a ball","A kind of golf club","A force that slows a moving ball"],"a":3,"why":"The text calls the force that slows the ball down drag."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which sentence best explains why less drag helps a golf ball?","c":["Golfers learned this by accident in the 1800s.","Less drag means the ball keeps its speed and travels farther.","Dimples stir up a thin layer of air right next to the ball's surface.","Players noticed old balls seemed to travel farther."],"a":1,"why":"The last sentence says that with less drag, the ball keeps its speed and goes farther."}]},{"id":"L06-ocean-coral-builders","level":6,"topic":"ocean","title":"The Tiny Builders of a Reef","text":["A coral reef can look like a garden of colorful rocks. But a reef is actually built by animals. Each coral is made of many tiny animals called polyps. A polyp has a soft, tube-shaped body with a ring of tentacles around its mouth. The tentacles work like small arms that catch bits of food floating by.","Polyps build reefs very slowly. Each polyp takes minerals from seawater. It uses them to make a hard cup of limestone, a kind of stone, around its body. New polyps grow on top of the cups left by older ones. Layer by layer, over thousands of years, the stone builds up into a reef. The largest reef system on Earth, the Great Barrier Reef near Australia, is about 1,400 miles long.","Building all that stone takes a lot of energy, and the bits of food that tentacles catch are not enough. The solution is a partnership. Tiny algae live inside the polyps' bodies. Like plants, the algae use sunlight to make food, and they share most of it with the coral. In return, the coral gives the algae a safe home. Because the algae need sunlight, most reef corals grow in clear, shallow water."],"vocab":[{"w":"polyps","pos":"noun","def":"Tiny soft animals that live together to make up a coral.","ex":"Each coral is made of many tiny polyps."},{"w":"tentacles","pos":"noun","def":"Long, bendy parts of some animals used to feel and catch things.","ex":"The tentacles catch bits of food floating by."},{"w":"limestone","pos":"noun","def":"A kind of hard stone that corals and some sea animals help make.","ex":"Each polyp makes a hard cup of limestone around its body."},{"w":"partnership","pos":"noun","def":"Two or more living things working together so each one gains something.","ex":"The coral and the algae have a partnership."},{"w":"algae","pos":"noun","def":"Simple living things that use sunlight to make food, like plants do.","ex":"Tiny algae live inside the polyps' bodies."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How tiny animals build reefs and get their food","Why many fish like to hide in coral reefs","How divers explore a reef without harming it","How rocks form on the floor of the deep sea"],"a":0,"why":"The text explains how polyps build reefs and how algae help feed them."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what are \"polyps\"?","word":"polyps","c":["Small plants that use sunlight","Hard cups made of stone","Tiny animals that make up a coral","Rocks that grow in shallow water"],"a":2,"why":"The text says each coral is made of many tiny animals called polyps."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How does a reef get bigger over time?","c":["Waves push loose rocks into big piles","The algae slowly turn into hard stone","Polyps catch rocks with their tentacles","New polyps grow on cups left by older ones"],"a":3,"why":"The second paragraph says new polyps grow on top of older cups, layer by layer."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why do most reef corals grow in clear, shallow water?","c":["The polyps need to come up for air","The algae inside them need sunlight","The water there is much colder","Fish bring them more food there"],"a":1,"why":"The last sentence says the algae need sunlight, so corals grow in clear, shallow water."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a coral reef is just a pile of rocks. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["A coral reef can look like a garden of colorful rocks.","Building all that stone takes a lot of energy.","Polyps build reefs very slowly.","But a reef is actually built by animals."],"a":3,"why":"The text says a reef is built by animals, not just made of rocks."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that coral and algae help each other?","c":["Polyps catch bits of food with their tentacles.","In return, the coral gives the algae a safe home.","Each polyp makes a hard cup of limestone.","The Great Barrier Reef is about 1,400 miles long."],"a":1,"why":"The algae share food, and in return the coral gives them a home, so both are helped."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"partnership\" mean?","word":"partnership","c":["Two living things helping each other","One living thing taking food from another","A group of animals that all look alike","A place where two kinds of water meet"],"a":0,"why":"The algae give food and the coral gives a home, so they help each other."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure where corals get most of their food. Which part should they reread?","c":["The first paragraph, about the shape of a polyp","The second paragraph, about limestone cups","The third paragraph, about the algae","The sentence about the Great Barrier Reef"],"a":2,"why":"The third paragraph says the algae make food and share most of it with the coral."}]},{"id":"L06-money-lemonade-profit","level":6,"topic":"money","title":"Counting Cups at a Lemonade Stand","text":["Nadia wanted to run a lemonade stand on a hot Saturday. She bought lemons, sugar and paper cups for $12. Those supplies were enough to make 40 cups of lemonade. She decided to charge 25 cents a cup, because she wanted a low price that anyone could pay.","By the afternoon, Nadia had sold all 40 cups. She counted her money and found $10. That was her revenue, the total amount of money that came in from sales. But she had spent $12 on supplies, and those were her costs. Since her costs were bigger than her revenue, she had a loss of $2.","Nadia's aunt showed her a way to fix the problem. First, divide the cost of the supplies by the number of cups. Twelve dollars divided by 40 cups is 30 cents per cup. Any price below 30 cents would lose money. The next Saturday, Nadia charged 50 cents a cup and sold all 40 cups again. Her revenue was $20. After she subtracted her $12 in costs, $8 was left over. That $8 was her profit, the money a business keeps after paying for what it used."],"vocab":[{"w":"supplies","pos":"noun","def":"Things you buy and use up to make or do something.","ex":"Lemons, sugar and cups were Nadia's supplies."},{"w":"revenue","pos":"noun","def":"All the money a business takes in from sales.","ex":"Her revenue was $20."},{"w":"costs","pos":"noun","def":"The money a business spends to make what it sells.","ex":"She had spent $12 on supplies, and those were her costs."},{"w":"loss","pos":"noun","def":"Money a business is short when it spends more than it takes in.","ex":"Her costs were bigger than her revenue, so she had a loss of $2."},{"w":"profit","pos":"noun","def":"The money a business keeps after paying all its costs.","ex":"That $8 was her profit."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to choose ripe lemons at a store","How Nadia learned to set a price that earns money","Why lemonade sells best on a very hot day","How Nadia's aunt started her own business"],"a":1,"why":"The text shows how Nadia lost money at first and then found a price that made a profit."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"revenue\" mean?","word":"revenue","c":["The money left after paying all costs","The money spent on lemons and cups","The price of one cup of lemonade","All the money that comes in from sales"],"a":3,"why":"The text says revenue is the total amount of money that came in from sales."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why did Nadia lose money on the first Saturday?","c":["Her costs were bigger than her revenue","She did not sell all of her cups","It rained, so very few people came","Her aunt kept some of the money"],"a":0,"why":"The text says her costs were bigger than her revenue, so she had a loss."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What did Nadia change on the next Saturday?","c":["She bought cheaper lemons and sugar","She made 80 cups instead of 40","She charged 50 cents a cup instead of 25","She moved her stand to a busier street"],"a":2,"why":"The third paragraph says she charged 50 cents a cup the next Saturday."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks Nadia made money on the first Saturday because she sold every cup. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Since her costs were bigger than her revenue, she had a loss of $2.","By the afternoon, Nadia had sold all 40 cups.","She counted her money and found $10.","She decided to charge 25 cents a cup, because she wanted a low price that anyone could pay."],"a":0,"why":"Selling every cup still brought in less than she spent, so she lost $2."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["The Best Lemonade Recipe","A Trip to the Grocery Store","Finding a Price That Pays","Nadia's Long Summer Break"],"a":2,"why":"The text is about finding a price that covers costs and leaves a profit."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is \"profit\"?","word":"profit","c":["All the money that comes in from sales","The money kept after paying all costs","The money spent on lemons and cups","The price charged for one cup"],"a":1,"why":"The text says profit is the money a business keeps after paying for what it used."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders why the number 30 cents matters. Which sentence explains it?","c":["She decided to charge 25 cents a cup.","Her revenue was $20.","She bought lemons, sugar and paper cups for $12.","Any price below 30 cents would lose money."],"a":3,"why":"Each cup cost 30 cents to make, so any lower price would lose money."}]},{"id":"L06-music-string-pitch","level":6,"topic":"music","title":"Inside a Vibrating String","text":["When you pluck a guitar string, it moves back and forth very quickly. This fast shaking is called a vibration. The string pushes on the air around it and sends waves of sound to your ears. How fast the string vibrates decides its pitch, which is how high or low a note sounds.","Faster vibrations make higher notes, and slower vibrations make lower notes. Scientists measure vibrations in hertz, the number of back and forth movements in one second. Many orchestras tune to a note called A, which vibrates 440 times each second.","Three things change how fast a string vibrates. A shorter string vibrates faster than a longer one. A tighter string vibrates faster than a looser one. A thinner, lighter string vibrates faster than a thick, heavy one. This is why a violin, with its short, thin strings, plays high notes. A double bass has long, thick strings, so it plays low notes.","Guitar players use these rules every time they play. Turning a tuning peg tightens or loosens a string to raise or lower its pitch. Pressing a finger on a string makes the part that vibrates shorter, so the note goes up."],"vocab":[{"w":"vibration","pos":"noun","def":"A fast shaking movement back and forth.","ex":"This fast shaking is called a vibration."},{"w":"pitch","pos":"noun","def":"How high or low a sound is.","ex":"How fast the string vibrates decides its pitch."},{"w":"hertz","pos":"noun","def":"A unit that counts how many times something vibrates in one second.","ex":"Scientists measure vibrations in hertz."},{"w":"loosens","pos":"verb","def":"Makes something less tight.","ex":"Turning a tuning peg loosens a string and lowers its pitch."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to choose your first guitar","Why orchestras play in large halls","What makes a string play high or low notes","How sound travels through deep water"],"a":2,"why":"The text explains how the speed of a string's vibration sets how high or low it sounds."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"pitch\" mean?","word":"pitch","c":["How loud a note sounds","How high or low a note sounds","How long a string is","How fast a song is played"],"a":1,"why":"The first paragraph says pitch is how high or low a note sounds."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the third paragraph organized?","c":["It tells events in the order they happened","It describes a problem and then a solution","It lists the steps for building a violin","It compares strings and gives examples"],"a":3,"why":"The paragraph compares short and long, tight and loose, thin and thick strings, then names two instruments."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens when a guitar player presses a finger on a string?","c":["The vibrating part gets shorter, so the note goes up","The string gets looser, so the note goes down","The string stops making any sound at all","The string gets thicker, so it vibrates slower"],"a":0,"why":"The last sentence says pressing a finger makes the vibrating part shorter, so the note goes up."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a double bass plays low notes because it is big and loud. Which sentence fixes this?","c":["This fast shaking is called a vibration.","The string pushes on the air around it and sends waves of sound to your ears.","A double bass has long, thick strings, so it plays low notes.","Many orchestras tune to a note called A."],"a":2,"why":"The text says the long, thick strings are the reason a double bass plays low notes."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that the size of a string changes its pitch?","c":["A violin, with its short, thin strings, plays high notes.","Scientists measure vibrations in hertz.","The string pushes on the air around it.","Many orchestras tune to a note called A."],"a":0,"why":"Short, thin violin strings play high notes, which shows that string size changes pitch."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what are \"hertz\"?","word":"hertz","c":["The pegs used to tune a guitar","The strings on a double bass","The notes that an orchestra plays","A count of vibrations in one second"],"a":3,"why":"The text says hertz is the number of back and forth movements in one second."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders how a player can raise a note without pressing a finger down. Which sentence answers this?","c":["Pressing a finger on a string makes the part that vibrates shorter, so the note goes up.","Turning a tuning peg tightens or loosens a string to raise or lower its pitch.","A shorter string vibrates faster than a longer one.","Guitar players use these rules every time they play."],"a":1,"why":"The text says turning a tuning peg can tighten a string and raise its pitch."}]},{"id":"L07-art-camera-obscura","level":7,"topic":"art","title":"A Picture on the Wall of a Dark Room","text":["Long before photographs, people noticed something surprising. When sunlight passed through a tiny hole into a dark room, a picture of the outside world appeared. It showed up on the opposite wall, the one facing the hole. This effect is called a camera obscura, from Latin words that mean dark room.","The picture forms because light travels in straight lines. Light from the top of a tree passes through the hole and keeps heading down, so it lands low on the wall. Light from the bottom of the tree heads up and lands high. As a result, the image is upside down. It is also reversed, so the left side of the scene shows up on the right.","The size of the hole matters too. A small hole makes a sharp but dim image, while a bigger hole lets in more light and makes the image blurry. Later, people placed a glass lens in the hole, which made the image both bright and sharp. They also built the dark room into a box small enough to carry.","Artists used these boxes to trace landscapes and buildings, drawing over the lines of the image on paper. Historians have found evidence that some famous painters used one. Still, a camera obscura could only show a picture, not save it. In the 1800s, inventors found materials that change when light strikes them, and the first photographs were made inside these same boxes."],"vocab":[{"w":"opposite","pos":"adjective","def":"Across from something and facing it.","ex":"A picture appeared on the opposite wall, the wall facing the hole."},{"w":"image","pos":"noun","def":"A picture of something made by light, a lens or a mirror.","ex":"The image on the wall is upside down."},{"w":"reversed","pos":"adjective","def":"Turned the other way, so left and right switch places.","ex":"The image is reversed, so the left side shows up on the right."},{"w":"trace","pos":"verb","def":"To copy a picture by drawing over its lines.","ex":"Artists used the boxes to trace landscapes and buildings."},{"w":"landscapes","pos":"noun","def":"Views of land, such as hills, fields and trees.","ex":"Artists traced landscapes by drawing over the image."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is the author's main point?","c":["Painters in the past could not draw anything well without a camera","Light through a small hole forms a picture, an idea behind cameras","The first photographs were taken by people who lived in dark rooms","Glass lenses are the reason every picture is upside down and blurry"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains how a hole makes a picture and ends by showing how this led to photographs."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the first paragraph, what does \"opposite\" mean?","word":"opposite","c":["Across from and facing","Very dark and quiet","Close to the floor","Painted bright white"],"a":0,"why":"The text explains that the opposite wall is the wall facing the hole."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why is the image on the wall upside down?","c":["The glass lens bends the light the wrong way","The room is too dark to show it clearly","The hole is too small to fit the whole tree","Light travels in straight lines through the hole"],"a":3,"why":"Light from the top heads down and light from the bottom heads up because light travels in straight lines."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the third paragraph organized?","c":["Events from one day are told in time order","Two kinds of artists are compared","A problem with the hole is described, then a fix","A list of famous painters is given"],"a":2,"why":"A small hole is dim and a big hole is blurry, and then a lens solves the problem."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a bigger hole always makes a better picture. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["The picture forms because light travels in straight lines.","It is also reversed, so the left side shows up on the right.","A bigger hole lets in more light and makes the image blurry.","Artists used these boxes to trace landscapes and buildings."],"a":2,"why":"The text says a bigger hole makes the image blurry."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that the camera obscura helped artists?","c":["Light from the bottom of the tree heads up and lands high on the wall.","This effect is called a camera obscura.","A small hole makes a sharp but dim image.","Historians have found evidence that some famous painters used one."],"a":3,"why":"Evidence that painters used one shows that it was a tool for artists."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"trace\" mean?","word":"trace","c":["Copy by drawing over lines","Find a very small amount","Follow a set of footprints","Paint with bright colors"],"a":0,"why":"The text says artists traced by drawing over the lines of the image."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a camera obscura could save pictures. Which sentence fixes this?","c":["Artists used these boxes to trace landscapes.","A camera obscura could only show a picture, not save it.","Later, people placed a glass lens in the hole.","The picture forms because light travels in straight lines."],"a":1,"why":"The last paragraph says it could only show a picture, not save it."}]},{"id":"L07-nature-fall-leaves","level":7,"topic":"nature","title":"What Happens to Leaves in the Fall","text":["Every fall, the leaves on many trees turn yellow, orange and red. It might seem that trees paint new colors onto their leaves. For many of these colors, the truth is quite different: the yellow and orange were inside the leaf all summer long.","A green leaf is full of chlorophyll, a substance that helps the tree use sunlight to make its food. A leaf holds so much chlorophyll that its strong green color covers up the other colors. Those other colors come from pigments, natural substances that give things their color. The yellow and orange pigments in leaves are called carotenoids, and they also give carrots and corn their color.","In the fall, the nights grow longer and the air turns cooler. The tree begins to shut down its leaves for winter, and it stops making new chlorophyll. The green slowly breaks down and fades away. As it disappears, the yellow and orange that were hidden underneath can finally be seen.","Red is different. In some trees, such as many maples, the leaves make new red pigments in late summer and fall. Bright sunny days and cool nights help produce more of them. That is why a fall with many sunny days and cool, but not freezing, nights often brings the brightest reds."],"vocab":[{"w":"chlorophyll","pos":"noun","def":"The green substance in leaves that helps a plant use sunlight to make food.","ex":"A green leaf is full of chlorophyll."},{"w":"pigments","pos":"noun","def":"Natural substances that give things their color.","ex":"The yellow and orange pigments in leaves are called carotenoids."},{"w":"fades","pos":"verb","def":"Slowly becomes weaker or harder to see.","ex":"The green slowly breaks down and fades away."},{"w":"hidden","pos":"adjective","def":"Covered up so it cannot be seen.","ex":"The yellow and orange were hidden underneath the green."},{"w":"produce","pos":"verb","def":"To make or create something.","ex":"Sunny days and cool nights help produce more red pigments."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is the author's main claim?","c":["Trees paint brand new colors onto all of their leaves each fall","Leaves drop from trees because the air gets too cold for them","Many fall colors were in the leaf all along, hidden by green","Carrots and corn get their bright color from the leaves of trees"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the yellow and orange were inside the leaf all summer, covered by green."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what are \"pigments\"?","word":"pigments","c":["Tiny holes on the side of a leaf","Different kinds of maple trees","Drops of rain that sit on a leaf","Natural substances that give color"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains that pigments are natural substances that give things their color."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does the green fade from leaves in the fall?","c":["The tree stops making new chlorophyll","The leaves get too much sunlight","The carotenoids eat up the green","The red pigments cover it up"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the tree stops making new chlorophyll, so the green breaks down."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens as the green disappears from a leaf?","c":["The tree makes new chlorophyll","The hidden yellow and orange show","The nights grow shorter","The leaf turns green again"],"a":1,"why":"The third paragraph says the hidden yellow and orange can finally be seen."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks every fall color was hiding in the leaf all summer. Which sentence shows this is not quite right?","c":["The green slowly breaks down and fades away.","Some leaves make new red pigments in the fall.","Carotenoids also give carrots and corn their color.","Many leaves turn yellow, orange and red."],"a":1,"why":"The last paragraph says red is different because leaves make new red pigments."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the claim that yellow was in the leaf all summer?","c":["The strong green color covers up the other colors.","The nights grow longer and the air turns cooler.","Bright sunny days help produce more red.","Many maples make new red pigments."],"a":0,"why":"The green covered the other colors, so they were there but could not be seen."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In \"help produce more of them,\" what does \"produce\" mean?","word":"produce","c":["Fruits and vegetables for sale","Hide away from sight","Break down slowly","Make or create"],"a":3,"why":"Sunny days and cool nights help leaves make more red pigments."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders why some falls have brighter reds than others. Which sentence helps most?","c":["The tree begins to shut down its leaves for winter.","A leaf holds so much chlorophyll that its green covers other colors.","Bright sunny days and cool nights help produce more of them.","Carotenoids also give carrots and corn their color."],"a":2,"why":"The text says sunny days and cool nights help leaves make more red."}]},{"id":"L08-vehicles-train-wheels","level":8,"topic":"vehicles","title":"How a Train Turns Without a Steering Wheel","text":["A car turns when the driver moves the steering wheel. A train has no steering wheel at all, yet it follows every curve in the track. The secret is hidden in the shape of its wheels.","On most trains, two wheels are joined by a solid steel bar called an axle. Because they are locked together, both wheels must spin at exactly the same speed. That creates a tricky engineering puzzle whenever the track curves. The outside rail of a curve is longer than the inside rail. So the outer wheel has farther to travel in the same amount of time.","The answer is that train wheels are not flat like the side of a can. Each wheel is tapered, which means it is bigger around on one side than the other. The bigger side is closest to the middle of the track. On a curve, the pair of wheels naturally shifts a little toward the outside. The outer wheel then rolls on its bigger part, which covers more ground with each turn. The inner wheel rolls on its smaller part. Together, the pair automatically steers itself around the bend.","Each wheel also has a raised lip on its inner edge called a flange. Many people believe the flanges are what keep a train on a curve. In fact, they work more like a backup, a second plan for when the first one is not enough. On a gentle curve, the tapered shape does most of the steering, and the flanges rarely press hard against the rails."],"vocab":[{"w":"axle","pos":"noun","def":"A bar that joins two wheels so they turn together.","ex":"Two wheels are joined by a solid steel bar called an axle."},{"w":"tapered","pos":"adjective","def":"Bigger at one end and slowly getting smaller toward the other end.","ex":"Each wheel is tapered, so it is bigger on one side."},{"w":"bend","pos":"noun","def":"A curve in a road, river or track.","ex":"The pair steers itself around the bend."},{"w":"flange","pos":"noun","def":"A raised lip or edge that helps hold something in place.","ex":"Each wheel has a raised lip on its inner edge called a flange."},{"w":"backup","pos":"noun","def":"Something ready to help if the first plan is not enough.","ex":"The flanges work more like a backup."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["Why trains are faster than cars on long trips","How engineers build rails that never curve","Why a train driver needs special controls","How the shape of train wheels steers a train"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains how tapered wheels let a train steer itself around curves."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is an \"axle\"?","word":"axle","c":["A curved piece of rail","A solid bar that joins two wheels","A raised lip on a wheel","A tool the driver uses to steer"],"a":1,"why":"The text says two wheels are joined by a solid steel bar called an axle."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why is a curve a puzzle for two wheels joined by an axle?","c":["The inside rail is longer than the outside rail","The wheels are too heavy to spin around a curve","They spin together, but the outer wheel goes farther","The flanges keep the wheels from spinning freely"],"a":2,"why":"Both wheels spin at the same speed, but the outside rail is longer."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens when the wheels shift toward the outside of a curve?","c":["The outer wheel rolls on its bigger part","Both wheels stop spinning for a moment","The inner wheel rolls on its bigger part","The flanges lift the wheels off the rail"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the outer wheel then rolls on its bigger part."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the flanges do most of the work on a curve. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["The tapered shape does most of the steering.","Each wheel has a raised lip called a flange.","A car turns when the driver moves the wheel.","The outside rail of a curve is longer."],"a":0,"why":"The last paragraph says the tapered shape does most of the steering on a gentle curve."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that the wheels steer the train?","c":["A train has no steering wheel at all, yet it follows every curve in the track.","Each wheel also has a raised lip on its inner edge.","Each wheel is tapered, so the pair steers itself around the bend.","The outside rail of a curve is longer than the inside rail."],"a":2,"why":"The tapered shape is what lets the pair of wheels steer itself."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"tapered\" mean?","word":"tapered","c":["Wrapped tightly in sticky tape","Bigger on one side than the other","Flat on both sides like a can","Made of one solid piece of steel"],"a":1,"why":"The text says a tapered wheel is bigger around on one side than the other."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is unsure how the outer wheel keeps up on a curve. Which sentence should they reread?","c":["A car turns when the driver moves the steering wheel.","On most trains, two wheels are joined by an axle.","Many people believe the flanges keep a train on a curve.","The outer wheel rolls on its bigger part."],"a":3,"why":"The bigger part of the wheel covers more ground with each turn."}]},{"id":"L08-mystery-rosetta-stone","level":8,"topic":"mystery","title":"Cracking the Code of a Broken Stone","text":["For more than a thousand years, no one could read Egyptian hieroglyphs, the picture signs carved on temple walls and statues. The knowledge of how to read them had been lost. Scholars, people who study a subject deeply, guessed at their meaning, and many of their guesses were wrong. Most believed each sign stood only for an idea.","In 1799, people digging near a fort by the Egyptian town of Rashid, which Europeans called Rosetta, found a broken slab of dark stone. It was carved with writing in three scripts. Hieroglyphs filled the top, a later Egyptian script called Demotic filled the middle, and ancient Greek filled the bottom. Scholars could still read Greek, and the Greek part said that the same message was written in all three scripts.","That made the stone a key, something that helps open up a puzzle. Readers could look for a name in the Greek and then find the same name in the hieroglyphs. Royal names in hieroglyphs were circled by an oval ring called a cartouche, so they were easy to spot. One cartouche held the name of a king, Ptolemy. By comparing it with other royal names, scholars began to work out which signs stood for which sounds.","In 1822, a French scholar named Champollion announced that he had solved the puzzle. Many signs stood for sounds, like letters, while others stood for whole words or ideas. Other scholars, such as Thomas Young in England, had made important steps first. Today the stone sits in the British Museum in London."],"vocab":[{"w":"hieroglyphs","pos":"noun","def":"Picture signs used for writing in ancient Egypt.","ex":"No one could read Egyptian hieroglyphs for more than a thousand years."},{"w":"scholars","pos":"noun","def":"People who study a subject very deeply.","ex":"Scholars guessed at the meaning of the signs."},{"w":"script","pos":"noun","def":"A system of signs or letters used for writing.","ex":"A later Egyptian script called Demotic filled the middle."},{"w":"key","pos":"noun","def":"Something that helps you solve or understand a puzzle.","ex":"That made the stone a key to reading hieroglyphs."},{"w":"cartouche","pos":"noun","def":"An oval ring drawn around a royal name in hieroglyphs.","ex":"One cartouche held the name of a king, Ptolemy."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How the Rosetta Stone helped people read hieroglyphs","How ancient Egyptians built their temples and statues","Why Greek was the hardest language for scholars to learn","How a fort near the town of Rashid was rebuilt in 1799"],"a":0,"why":"The text tells how the stone gave scholars what they needed to read hieroglyphs."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In \"That made the stone a key,\" what does \"key\" mean?","word":"key","c":["A tool that opens a door lock","A button on a computer keyboard","Something that helps solve a puzzle","A low island near a coast"],"a":2,"why":"The text says a key here is something that helps open up a puzzle."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why could the Greek part help scholars read the hieroglyphs?","c":["Greek uses the very same signs as hieroglyphs","The same message was written in all three scripts","The Greek part was carved first, before the rest","Champollion carved the Greek part by himself"],"a":1,"why":"Because the message was the same, scholars could match the Greek they knew to the hieroglyphs."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the text mostly organized?","c":["Two scholars are compared to show who was smarter","A list of Egyptian kings is given in order","The steps for carving a stone are explained","A problem is described, then how it was solved"],"a":3,"why":"It starts with a lost way of reading and ends with the puzzle being solved."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks each hieroglyph stands only for an idea. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Hieroglyphs filled the top of the stone.","Scholars could still read Greek.","One cartouche held the name of a king.","Many signs stood for sounds, like letters."],"a":3,"why":"Champollion showed that many signs stood for sounds."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["Temples Along the Nile","A Puzzle Carved in Stone","The Old Fort at Rashid","The Life of King Ptolemy"],"a":1,"why":"The whole text is about how the writing on the stone was worked out."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"What is a \"cartouche\" in this text?","word":"cartouche","c":["A broken slab of dark stone","A kind of Greek letter","An oval ring around a royal name","A museum in London"],"a":2,"why":"The text says royal names were circled by an oval ring called a cartouche."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders how scholars found the same name in two scripts. Which sentence helps most?","c":["Royal names were circled, so they were easy to spot.","Scholars guessed, and many of their guesses were wrong.","Today the stone sits in a museum in London.","People found a broken slab of dark stone."],"a":0,"why":"The oval rings made royal names easy to find in the hieroglyphs."}]},{"id":"L08-space-neptune","level":8,"topic":"space","title":"The Eighth Planet","text":["In 1781, the astronomer William Herschel spotted a new planet, later named Uranus. Over the next sixty years, astronomers tracked its path and noticed a problem. Uranus did not move the way their math said it should. Sometimes it was a little ahead of its predicted place, the spot the math said it would be, and sometimes a little behind.","Some astronomers made a bold claim. They argued that the gravity of an unseen planet, even farther from the Sun, was tugging on Uranus. It was pulling Uranus off course, away from its expected path. If that was true, the tugs held clues. By measuring exactly how Uranus strayed, or wandered from that path, a person could work backward to find the hidden planet.","A French mathematician named Urbain Le Verrier took on this difficult problem. Using only pen, paper and numbers, he calculated, or worked out with math, where the unknown planet should be. In England, John Couch Adams had worked on the same question on his own. Le Verrier sent his prediction to Johann Galle, an astronomer at the Berlin Observatory in Germany.","On September 23, 1846, Galle pointed his telescope at the spot Le Verrier had named. Comparing the sky with a star map, he found a faint object that the map did not show. It was too dim to see without a telescope, and it sat about one degree from the predicted position. The planet, later named Neptune, was strong evidence that math about gravity could reveal an object no one had seen."],"vocab":[{"w":"predicted","pos":"adjective","def":"Said ahead of time, based on facts or math.","ex":"Uranus was sometimes ahead of its predicted place."},{"w":"course","pos":"noun","def":"The path that something is expected to follow.","ex":"Gravity was pulling Uranus off course."},{"w":"strayed","pos":"verb","def":"Wandered away from the expected path.","ex":"Astronomers measured how Uranus strayed."},{"w":"calculated","pos":"verb","def":"Worked something out using math.","ex":"Le Verrier calculated where the planet should appear."},{"w":"faint","pos":"adjective","def":"Dim and hard to see.","ex":"Galle found a faint object that was not on the map."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is the author's main point?","c":["Uranus is the most important planet to study","Telescopes in the 1800s could not see planets","Math helped astronomers find a hidden planet","Herschel found every planet beyond Saturn"],"a":2,"why":"The text shows how calculations about gravity led Galle to Neptune."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In \"pulling it off course,\" what does \"course\" mean?","word":"course","c":["The path something is expected to follow","A class that students take at school","One part of a big meal","A field for running races"],"a":0,"why":"The text explains that off course means away from its expected path."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why did some astronomers think there was an unseen planet?","c":["Herschel saw it through his telescope in 1781","It was already marked on every star map","The Sun was pulling too hard on Uranus","Uranus kept moving away from its predicted place"],"a":3,"why":"Uranus did not move the way the math said, so something else had to be pulling on it."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happened after Le Verrier finished his calculations?","c":["Herschel spotted Uranus","He sent his prediction to Galle","Uranus was given its name","Adams began to study the sky"],"a":1,"why":"The third paragraph says Le Verrier sent his prediction to Johann Galle."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks Le Verrier found Neptune with his own telescope. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Uranus did not move the way their math said it should.","Galle pointed his telescope at the spot Le Verrier named.","In England, John Couch Adams worked on the same question.","Over sixty years, astronomers tracked the path of Uranus."],"a":1,"why":"Galle, not Le Verrier, used the telescope to find the planet."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that the prediction was accurate?","c":["Uranus was sometimes a little ahead of its place.","Le Verrier was a French mathematician.","Adams worked on the same question on his own.","It sat about one degree from the predicted spot."],"a":3,"why":"The planet was found very close to where Le Verrier said it would be."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"faint\" mean?","word":"faint","c":["Dim and hard to see","Very large and bright","Moving very quickly","Feeling dizzy and weak"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the object was too dim to see without a telescope."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is unsure how a planet could be found before anyone saw it. Which sentence should they reread?","c":["In 1781, the astronomer William Herschel spotted a new planet.","Le Verrier sent his prediction to an astronomer in Germany.","By measuring how Uranus strayed, a person could work backward.","The planet was later given the name Neptune by astronomers."],"a":2,"why":"This sentence explains how the pulls on Uranus pointed to the hidden planet."}]},{"id":"L09-tech-machine-learning","level":9,"topic":"tech","title":"Teaching a Computer to Sort Photos","text":["For most of computing history, a program did exactly what its instructions said. A programmer wrote every rule by hand, and the computer followed those rules one step at a time, never changing them on its own.","Some tasks, however, are very hard to describe with rules. Imagine writing instructions that explain how to recognize a cat in any photo, from any angle and in any light. Machine learning takes a different approach, a new way of handling the problem. Instead of writing the rules, people give a program thousands of examples, such as photos labeled \"cat\" or \"not cat,\" so each photo carries a word that names what it shows. This collection of examples is called training data.","During training, the program makes a guess about each example and then checks the label. When a guess is wrong, it adjusts its internal numbers, the settings stored inside the program, changing them by a tiny amount so the next guess is a little better. After many rounds, the program can often find patterns that no person ever wrote down.","These systems can be remarkably useful, but they have real limits. A program that sorts photos does not understand cats the way a person does; it has only learned patterns in its examples. If the training data leaves something out, such as cats photographed outdoors in snow, the program may make surprising errors. For this reason, engineers usually test a system carefully on new examples before anyone relies on it."],"vocab":[{"w":"approach","pos":"noun","def":"A way of dealing with a problem or a task.","ex":"Machine learning takes a different approach."},{"w":"labeled","pos":"verb","def":"Marked with a word or name that tells what something is.","ex":"People give a program photos labeled \"cat\" or \"not cat.\""},{"w":"adjusts","pos":"verb","def":"Changes something a little to make it work better.","ex":"When a guess is wrong, the program adjusts its numbers."},{"w":"internal","pos":"adjective","def":"On the inside of something.","ex":"It changes its internal numbers, the settings stored inside the program."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["Computers now understand photos in the same way that people do","Machine learning finds patterns in examples but has real limits","Programmers no longer need to write any rules for computers at all","Photos of cats are the hardest thing for a computer to sort"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains how programs learn from examples and then describes their limits."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the second paragraph, what does \"approach\" mean?","word":"approach","c":["A way of dealing with a problem","A step toward a place","A kind of computer screen","A rule written by hand"],"a":0,"why":"The text calls it \"a new way of handling the problem.\""},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the second paragraph organized?","c":["Events are told in the order they happened","Two computers are compared side by side","A list of facts about cats is given","A problem is named, then a new method is offered"],"a":3,"why":"Rules are hard to write for some tasks, so machine learning uses examples instead."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens when the program makes a wrong guess during training?","c":["It deletes that example from the training data","A programmer writes a new rule to fix it","It changes its internal numbers by a tiny amount","It stops training and asks for more photos"],"a":2,"why":"The third paragraph says a wrong guess makes the program adjust its numbers a little."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader concludes that a photo sorting program understands cats. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["After many rounds, the program can often find patterns.","Machine learning takes a different approach.","It has only learned patterns in its examples.","People give a program thousands of examples."],"a":2,"why":"The last paragraph says the program does not understand cats; it only learned patterns."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that machine learning has limits?","c":["Missing examples in the training data can cause surprising errors.","For years, programmers wrote every rule by hand.","Photos in the training data are labeled with a word.","After many rounds, the program often finds patterns no person wrote."],"a":0,"why":"The text warns that gaps in the training data can lead to surprising errors."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the third paragraph, what does \"adjusts\" mean?","word":"adjusts","c":["Removes for good","Counts again","Copies exactly","Changes a little"],"a":3,"why":"The program changes its numbers by a tiny amount so the next guess is better."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which inference is best supported by the text?","c":["Engineers think new examples stop mattering once training ends.","A program trained only on indoor cat photos might struggle outdoors.","Programs that follow rules are always better than learning programs.","Every photo in the training data must show a cat."],"a":1,"why":"The text says leaving something out of the training data can cause errors."}]},{"id":"L09-food-bread-rise","level":9,"topic":"food","title":"The Quiet Work Inside a Bowl of Dough","text":["A lump of bread dough can double in size while it sits quietly on a kitchen counter. Nobody pumps air into it, so the growth can seem mysterious at first. The explanation is a tiny living thing called yeast.","Yeast is a fungus made of single cells, each far too small to see without a microscope. When yeast is mixed into dough, it feeds on sugars that come from the flour. As it feeds, it releases carbon dioxide gas along with a small amount of alcohol. This process is called fermentation, and bakers have relied on it for thousands of years.","Gas alone would simply escape, however. When wheat flour is mixed with water and kneaded, which means pressed and folded over and over, two of its proteins join to form gluten. Gluten is a stretchy network that behaves a bit like a web of tiny balloons, and it traps the gas bubbles so the whole dough slowly puffs up.","Baking finishes the job. In the hot oven, the trapped gas expands, taking up more space, and the dough often rises a little more. The heat eventually stops the yeast, most of the alcohol evaporates into the air, and the dough firms into bread. The small holes you see in a slice are the bubbles that the yeast made."],"vocab":[{"w":"fermentation","pos":"noun","def":"A process in which tiny living things like yeast break down sugar and give off gas.","ex":"This process is called fermentation."},{"w":"kneaded","pos":"verb","def":"Pressed and folded dough over and over with the hands.","ex":"The flour is mixed with water and kneaded."},{"w":"gluten","pos":"noun","def":"A stretchy material that forms when wheat flour and water are mixed and worked.","ex":"Gluten traps the gas bubbles so the dough puffs up."},{"w":"expands","pos":"verb","def":"Gets bigger and takes up more space.","ex":"In the hot oven, the trapped gas expands."},{"w":"evaporates","pos":"verb","def":"Changes from a liquid into a gas and goes into the air.","ex":"Most of the alcohol evaporates into the air."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to choose the best kind of flour at a grocery store","Why bread was a common food in many ancient places","How yeast and gluten make bread dough rise","How ovens heat food evenly from every side"],"a":2,"why":"Each paragraph explains a step in how yeast and gluten make dough grow into bread."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the third paragraph, what does \"kneaded\" mean?","word":"kneaded","c":["Baked in a hot oven","Pressed and folded over and over","Left alone on the counter to rest","Mixed with a spoonful of sugar"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains that kneaded means pressed and folded over and over."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, what keeps the gas bubbles inside the dough?","c":["Gluten forms a stretchy network that traps them","The baker pumps extra air into the dough","The alcohol makes the dough thick and heavy","The heat of the oven pushes the bubbles back in"],"a":0,"why":"The third paragraph says gluten traps the gas bubbles."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens to the trapped gas in the hot oven?","c":["It turns back into flour","It becomes food for the yeast again","It escapes before baking starts","It expands, so the dough often rises more"],"a":3,"why":"The last paragraph says the gas expands and the dough often rises a little more."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the yeast keeps working after the bread is baked. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Yeast feeds on sugars that come from the flour.","Bakers have relied on fermentation for thousands of years.","Gluten traps the gas bubbles.","The heat eventually stops the yeast."],"a":3,"why":"The last paragraph says the oven's heat stops the yeast."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["The Best Bread Recipe in the World","The Tiny Helpers That Raise Bread","A Short History of Kitchen Ovens","Why Flour Is Made From Wheat"],"a":1,"why":"The text is about how yeast, with help from gluten, makes bread rise."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"evaporates\" mean?","word":"evaporates","c":["Turns into a hard crust","Mixes back into the flour","Changes into a gas and drifts away","Sinks to the bottom of the pan"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the alcohol evaporates into the air, so it turns into a gas and leaves."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which question would a careful reader ask to check their understanding of the third paragraph?","c":["How does gluten keep the gas from escaping?","What color is yeast under a microscope?","How long have people owned kitchen counters?","Which country bakes the most bread each year?"],"a":0,"why":"The third paragraph is about how gluten traps the gas, so this question checks it."}]},{"id":"L10-money-compound-interest","level":10,"topic":"money","title":"Why Savings Can Grow on Their Own","text":["When people put money into a savings account, the bank does not simply lock it away in a vault. Banks lend much of that money to others, such as families buying homes, and they charge those borrowers a fee for using it. Part of what the bank earns is passed back to savers as interest, a payment for letting the bank use their money.","Interest is usually described as a percentage of the amount first saved, which is called the principal. Suppose someone deposits, or puts in, $100 in an account that pays 5% each year. After one year, the account earns $5, and the balance, which is the total in the account, becomes $105.","The second year is where something interesting happens. The bank now pays 5% on $105 rather than on the original $100, so the account earns $5.25 instead of $5. This is called compound interest, because the interest itself begins earning interest. The difference seems tiny at first, yet it grows larger every year. After ten years, the $100 would grow to about $163, while simple interest on the principal alone would reach only $150.","Compounding works in both directions, however. When people borrow money, the amount they owe can grow in the same way if they do not pay it back promptly, which means soon and without delay. For this reason, many money teachers encourage people to begin saving early and to repay loans quickly, since time often matters as much as the amount of money involved."],"vocab":[{"w":"principal","pos":"noun","def":"The amount of money first put into an account or first borrowed.","ex":"The amount first saved is called the principal."},{"w":"deposits","pos":"verb","def":"Puts money into a bank account.","ex":"Someone deposits $100 in an account."},{"w":"balance","pos":"noun","def":"The total amount of money in an account.","ex":"After one year, the balance becomes $105."},{"w":"compound","pos":"adjective","def":"Built up in layers, so that new interest is added on top of earlier interest.","ex":"This is called compound interest."},{"w":"promptly","pos":"adverb","def":"Soon and without waiting.","ex":"The amount owed can grow if people do not pay it back promptly."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["Banks keep all of their savers' money locked safely in a vault","Simple interest usually earns much more money than compound interest does","People should avoid saving money until they are much older","Interest that earns interest makes amounts grow faster over time"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains how compound interest builds on itself for savers and for borrowers."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the second paragraph, what does \"principal\" mean?","word":"principal","c":["The head of a school","The interest paid each year","The amount of money first saved","The bank that holds the account"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the amount first saved is called the principal."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does the account earn $5.25 in the second year instead of $5?","c":["The bank raised its rate to a higher percentage","Interest is now paid on $105, not on $100","The saver deposited another $5.25 that year","The bank stopped lending money to borrowers"],"a":1,"why":"The third paragraph says the bank now pays 5% on $105 rather than on $100."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the third paragraph mainly organized?","c":["It explains an idea using an example with numbers","It tells the history of banks in time order","It compares two banks to show which is safer","It lists rules that every saver must follow"],"a":0,"why":"It uses the $100 example to show how compound interest works."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks compounding can only help people. Which sentence shows this is not the whole story?","c":["The amount borrowers owe can grow in the same way.","After one year, the account earns $5.","Interest is usually described as a percentage of savings.","Banks lend much of that money to families and others."],"a":0,"why":"The last paragraph says debts can grow through compounding too."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that small differences add up over time?","c":["Someone deposits $100 in an account.","Banks charge borrowers a fee for using money.","Savers are paid interest for letting the bank use their money.","After ten years, $100 grows to about $163 instead of $150."],"a":3,"why":"The extra cents each year grow into a gap of about $13 after ten years."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"promptly\" mean?","word":"promptly","c":["Slowly","Soon","Partly","Rarely"],"a":1,"why":"The text says promptly means soon and without delay."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which inference is best supported by the text?","c":["Saving for a short time earns more than saving for a long time.","Only people who borrow money ever receive interest payments.","Starting to save sooner can lead to more money later on.","Interest rates are exactly 5% at every bank in the country."],"a":2,"why":"Because interest builds on itself each year, more years of saving means more growth."}]},{"id":"L10-stories-revision","level":10,"topic":"stories","title":"The Pages Readers Never See","text":["Readers usually see only the finished version of a book, with every sentence neatly in place. What they rarely see are the earlier drafts, the rough attempts that came first. Many writers describe their first draft as a way to discover what their story is really about.","The word revision comes from a Latin word meaning \"to look at again,\" and that is a good description of the work. When authors revise, they reread their draft as if they were strangers to it and then change it. They might cut a scene that slows the story down, move a surprise to a better place, or give a quiet character a stronger voice. Small changes matter too, such as replacing a vague word, one that is unclear, with a precise word that says exactly what the writer means.","Writers often get help with this process. An editor, a teacher or a trusted friend reads the draft and offers feedback, telling the writer which parts were confusing and which parts worked well. Good feedback does not rewrite the book for the author; it helps the writer notice what they could not see alone.","Some evidence of revision survives from long ago. The English novelist Jane Austen finished a draft of her novel Persuasion in 1816, then decided the ending was not right and rewrote its final chapters. Her handwritten pages, with words crossed out and replaced, are kept at the British Library, where they show that even celebrated authors rarely get it right the first time."],"vocab":[{"w":"drafts","pos":"noun","def":"Early versions of a piece of writing that will be changed later.","ex":"Readers rarely see the earlier drafts of a book."},{"w":"revise","pos":"verb","def":"To read something again and change it to make it better.","ex":"When authors revise, they reread their draft and then change it."},{"w":"vague","pos":"adjective","def":"Not clear or exact.","ex":"A writer might replace a vague word with a precise one."},{"w":"precise","pos":"adjective","def":"Exact and careful, saying just what is meant.","ex":"A precise word says exactly what the writer means."},{"w":"feedback","pos":"noun","def":"Comments that tell someone what worked and what could be better.","ex":"A trusted friend reads the draft and offers feedback."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["Most finished books are shaped by careful rounds of revision","Jane Austen was the first writer who ever revised a book","Editors usually rewrite most of an author's book for them","First drafts are the most valuable part of any book"],"a":0,"why":"The text explains what revision is, who helps with it, and how even famous authors do it."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the third paragraph, what does \"feedback\" mean?","word":"feedback","c":["A brand new ending for the story","A list of hard spelling words","Payment a writer gets for a book","Comments on what worked and what did not"],"a":3,"why":"The text says feedback tells the writer which parts were confusing and which parts worked."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the second paragraph organized?","c":["Events in one writer's life are told in time order","Two famous books are compared to each other","A word is defined, then examples of revising follow","A problem is described, and no answer is offered"],"a":2,"why":"It gives the meaning of revision and then lists things writers change when they revise."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, why do writers ask others to read their drafts?","c":["So that someone else will finish the book","To notice problems they could not see alone","To find out how much the book will cost","Because editors must approve every word"],"a":1,"why":"The third paragraph says feedback helps the writer notice what they could not see alone."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader concludes that great authors write perfect first drafts. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Readers usually see only the finished version of a book.","An editor or a friend might read the draft.","The word revision comes from a Latin word.","Even celebrated authors rarely get it right the first time."],"a":3,"why":"The last sentence says even celebrated authors rarely get it right the first time."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that revision is an old practice?","c":["Writers might cut a scene that slows the story down.","Good feedback helps a writer notice what they missed.","Austen rewrote the final chapters of Persuasion in 1816.","Readers rarely see the earlier drafts of a book."],"a":2,"why":"Austen's changes happened more than two hundred years ago."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the second paragraph, what does \"vague\" mean?","word":"vague","c":["Not clear or exact","Very long","Spelled wrong","Used too often"],"a":0,"why":"The text says a vague word is one that is unclear."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which question would a careful reader ask to check the main idea?","c":["How many pages long was the novel Persuasion?","How does rereading a draft help a writer improve it?","Which friend read Jane Austen's drafts first?","What is the Latin word for library?"],"a":1,"why":"The text is about how looking at writing again makes it better, so this question checks the main idea."}]},{"id":"L10-animals-beavers","level":10,"topic":"animals","title":"The Stream Builders","text":["Few animals change the land around them as much as the beaver does. Using sticks, mud and stones, a beaver family can build a dam across a stream, slowing the water until it spreads into a pond. Scientists often call beavers ecosystem engineers; an ecosystem is all the living things in a place along with their surroundings, and beavers reshape both.","The pond serves the beavers first. Its deep water lets them swim to and from their lodge, a dome of sticks with underwater entrances, while staying out of sight. In autumn, they store branches underwater near the lodge, so they have food to eat through the winter even when the surface of the pond freezes.","The pond helps many other creatures as well. Slow, shallow water at its edges becomes a wetland, an area of soggy ground and plants, where frogs, insects, ducks and young fish often thrive, growing and doing well. Because the water moves slowly, mud and soil sink to the bottom, which can leave the water downstream clearer. Ponds also soak water into the ground, so nearby land may stay green longer during dry seasons.","Beaver dams are not welcome everywhere, however. When a pond floods a road or a farm field, people may need to find ways to manage the water. In some places, wildlife workers install pipes, setting them in place through a dam so the pond cannot rise too high, which allows the beavers and their neighbors to stay."],"vocab":[{"w":"ecosystem","pos":"noun","def":"All the living things in a place, together with the land, water and air around them.","ex":"Beavers reshape the ecosystem around their pond."},{"w":"lodge","pos":"noun","def":"A beaver home, built as a dome of sticks with entrances under the water.","ex":"Beavers swim to and from their lodge."},{"w":"wetland","pos":"noun","def":"An area where the ground is soggy and covered with shallow water and plants.","ex":"The edges of the pond become a wetland."},{"w":"thrive","pos":"verb","def":"To grow strong and do well.","ex":"Frogs, insects, ducks and young fish often thrive there."},{"w":"install","pos":"verb","def":"To put something in place so it can be used.","ex":"Wildlife workers install pipes through a dam."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["Beavers build lodges that are warmer than any other animal home","Beaver dams change the land in ways that affect many living things","Beavers are a problem that people should always remove from streams","Ponds freeze solid every winter in places where beavers live"],"a":1,"why":"The text shows how a beaver dam shapes the land and the lives of many creatures."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the third paragraph, what does \"thrive\" mean?","word":"thrive","c":["Hide from danger","Leave for the winter","Grow and do well","Stay very still"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the animals thrive, growing and doing well."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, why does the water downstream of a pond often become clearer?","c":["Mud and soil sink in the slow water","Beavers carry the mud away to build lodges","Frogs and insects filter out the dirt","The ice in winter traps the soil"],"a":0,"why":"The third paragraph says slow water lets mud and soil sink to the bottom."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the text mainly organized?","c":["It tells one beaver's life story in order from birth","It compares beavers with several other building animals","It lists the steps for building a dam in a stream","It describes a dam, its effects, then a problem and a fix"],"a":3,"why":"The paragraphs move from the dam to its effects to flooding and the pipe solution."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks beavers go hungry when the pond freezes. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Beavers build dams from sticks, mud and stones.","Ducks and young fish often thrive in wetlands.","They store branches underwater for winter food.","Ponds soak water into the ground."],"a":2,"why":"The second paragraph says beavers store branches so they have food all winter."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that beaver ponds help other animals?","c":["Frogs, ducks and young fish often thrive at the pond's edges.","A lodge is a dome of sticks with entrances hidden under the water.","A pond may flood a road or a farm field.","Beavers use sticks, mud and stones to build."],"a":0,"why":"This detail names other animals that do well because of the pond."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"install\" mean?","word":"install","c":["Take apart","Pay for","Look for","Put in place"],"a":3,"why":"The text says workers install pipes by setting them in place through a dam."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which inference is best supported by the text?","c":["Beavers build dams mainly to help frogs and ducks.","People and beavers can sometimes share the same area.","Every beaver dam should be removed to protect roads.","Wetlands can only form where there are no beavers."],"a":1,"why":"Pipes through dams let people manage flooding while the beavers stay."}]},{"id":"M01-food-picnic-fruit","subject":"math","level":1,"topic":"food","title":"Fruit for the Picnic","text":["Maya and her dad make a fruit snack for the class picnic. First, they wash 8 red apples. Then they wash 6 green apples. They put all the apples in one big bowl. Maya wants to know the total.","Next, Dad cuts 12 orange slices. Maya eats 3 of the slices.","At the picnic, 5 friends each take 1 apple from the bowl. Maya likes to count, so she counts the apples that are left. She wants enough fruit for everyone."],"vocab":[{"w":"total","pos":"noun","def":"How many there are when you put all the groups together.","ex":"Maya wants to know the total number of apples."},{"w":"slices","pos":"noun","def":"Thin, flat pieces cut from something bigger.","ex":"Dad cuts 12 orange slices."},{"w":"count","pos":"verb","def":"To say numbers in order to find how many there are.","ex":"Maya likes to count the apples."},{"w":"left","pos":"adjective","def":"Still there after some are taken away.","ex":"Maya counts the apples that are left."},{"w":"enough","pos":"adjective","def":"As many as you need.","ex":"She wants enough fruit for everyone."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"Which group of apples has more?","c":["The green apples","The red apples","Both groups are the same","The apples friends took"],"a":1,"why":"There are 8 red apples and 6 green apples, and 8 is more than 6."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many apples do Maya and Dad wash in total?","c":["2 apples","15 apples","14 apples","13 apples"],"a":2,"why":"8 red apples + 6 green apples = 14 apples."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How many apples are in the bowl after 5 friends each take 1?","c":["9 apples","19 apples","3 apples","1 apple"],"a":0,"why":"8 + 6 = 14 apples, and 14 apples take away 5 apples is 9 apples."},{"skill":"problem","q":"After the friends take apples and Maya eats some slices, how many apples and orange slices are left in all?","c":["9 pieces of fruit","26 pieces of fruit","23 pieces of fruit","18 pieces of fruit"],"a":3,"why":"There are 9 apples and 12 take away 3 is 9 slices, so 9 + 9 = 18 pieces of fruit."},{"skill":"check","q":"Maya says 19 apples are left. Why can that not be right?","c":["19 is an odd number of apples","The friends took 19 apples away","Only 14 apples were washed","Dad cut 19 orange slices"],"a":2,"why":"Maya and Dad washed only 14 apples, so there cannot be more than 14 apples left."},{"skill":"number","q":"Dad cuts 12 orange slices. What does the 1 in 12 stand for?","c":["1 ten","1 one","1 slice","1 hundred"],"a":0,"why":"12 is 1 ten and 2 ones, so the 1 means 1 ten."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many orange slices are left after Maya eats 3?","c":["15 slices","8 slices","10 slices","9 slices"],"a":3,"why":"12 slices take away 3 slices is 9 slices."},{"skill":"check","q":"To find the orange slices left, Maya writes 12 + 3 = 15. What went wrong?","c":["She should start with 14 slices, not 12","She added instead of taking away","She should add 5 slices, not 3","She counted the apples by mistake"],"a":1,"why":"Maya ate 3 slices, so she should take 3 away from 12, which gives 9."}]},{"id":"M02-animals-pond-count","subject":"math","level":2,"topic":"animals","title":"Counting at the Pond","text":["Jamal's class visits a nature park with a big pond. A guide named Rosa helps them count the animals they see. First, the class counts 34 ducks on the pond and 25 geese on the grass.","Then 12 of the ducks fly away to a pond across the park. The geese stay on the grass.","Next, the class finds turtles resting on logs. There are 4 logs, and each log has 5 turtles on it. Rosa says turtles like to warm up in the sun.","Back at school, the class makes a chart of every animal they saw. Jamal wants to compare the numbers to find the most common animal."],"vocab":[{"w":"guide","pos":"noun","def":"A person who shows a group around a place and helps them learn.","ex":"A guide named Rosa helps the class."},{"w":"resting","pos":"verb","def":"Staying still to relax.","ex":"The turtles are resting on logs."},{"w":"chart","pos":"noun","def":"A picture or table that shows numbers in a neat way.","ex":"The class makes a chart of the animals."},{"w":"compare","pos":"verb","def":"To look at two or more things to see how they are the same or different.","ex":"Jamal wants to compare the numbers."},{"w":"common","pos":"adjective","def":"Seen a lot, or found in large numbers.","ex":"Ducks were the most common animal."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"The class counts 34 ducks. What does the 3 in 34 stand for?","c":["3 ones","3 hundreds","3 tens","3 ducks"],"a":2,"why":"34 is 3 tens and 4 ones, so the 3 means 3 tens, or 30."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many ducks and geese does the class count at first?","c":["59 birds","9 birds","58 birds","69 birds"],"a":0,"why":"34 ducks + 25 geese = 59 birds."},{"skill":"problem","q":"After 12 ducks fly away, how many ducks and geese are left in all?","c":["22 birds","71 birds","59 birds","47 birds"],"a":3,"why":"34 ducks take away 12 is 22 ducks, and 22 + 25 geese = 47 birds."},{"skill":"problem","q":"After 12 ducks fly away, how many more ducks are there than turtles?","c":["14 more ducks","2 more ducks","9 more ducks","12 more ducks"],"a":1,"why":"34 take away 12 is 22 ducks, 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20 turtles, and 22 is 2 more than 20."},{"skill":"check","q":"Jamal says there are 9 turtles because 4 + 5 = 9. What mistake did he make?","c":["He needed 5 four times, not 4 + 5","He forgot to count the geese on the grass","He should take 4 away from 5","He counted the ducks instead"],"a":0,"why":"Each of the 4 logs has 5 turtles, so he needs 5 + 5 + 5 + 5, which is 20 turtles."},{"skill":"number","q":"Which animal did the class count the most of at the start?","c":["Geese, with 25","Turtles, with 20","Geese and ducks tied","Ducks, with 34"],"a":3,"why":"34 is more than 25 and more than 20, so ducks were the most common."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many ducks are left after 12 fly away?","c":["46 ducks","22 ducks","12 ducks","32 ducks"],"a":1,"why":"34 ducks take away 12 ducks is 22 ducks."},{"skill":"check","q":"About how many ducks and geese were there at first? Pick the best estimate.","c":["About 10","About 100","About 60","About 30"],"a":2,"why":"34 is close to 30 and 25 is close to 30, so the total is about 60, and the real total is 59."}]},{"id":"M03-ocean-aquarium-feeding","subject":"math","level":3,"topic":"ocean","title":"Feeding Day at the Aquarium","text":["Priya volunteers at a small aquarium near the beach. On Saturday, she helps the keeper, Mr. Okafor, feed the fish. The largest tank has 6 schools of striped fish. Each school has 8 fish. One fourth of all the striped fish have yellow stripes.","Mr. Okafor shows Priya how to share food equally. She puts 36 pellets into a cup for the 9 crabs in another tank. Every crab should get the same number of pellets.","Last, they visit the touch pool, where visitors can gently touch sea stars. The pool is shaped like a rectangle. It is 7 feet long and 5 feet wide. Priya wants to find its area so she can tell her class."],"vocab":[{"w":"volunteers","pos":"verb","def":"Does a job to help out without getting paid.","ex":"Priya volunteers at the aquarium on Saturdays."},{"w":"schools","pos":"noun","def":"Groups of fish that swim together.","ex":"The tank has 6 schools of striped fish."},{"w":"equally","pos":"adverb","def":"In a way that gives everyone the same amount.","ex":"Priya shares the food equally among the crabs."},{"w":"rectangle","pos":"noun","def":"A flat shape with 4 straight sides and 4 square corners.","ex":"The touch pool is shaped like a rectangle."},{"w":"area","pos":"noun","def":"How much flat space a shape covers, counted in squares.","ex":"Priya wants to find the area of the pool."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"Which of these matches the striped fish in the largest tank?","c":["6 groups of 8 fish","6 groups of 9 fish","8 groups of 8 fish","6 + 8 fish in one group"],"a":0,"why":"There are 6 schools with 8 fish in each, so that is 6 groups of 8 fish."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many striped fish are in the largest tank?","c":["14 fish","42 fish","56 fish","48 fish"],"a":3,"why":"6 schools times 8 fish is 48 fish."},{"skill":"problem","q":"Priya walks once around the edge of the touch pool. How many feet does she walk?","c":["12 feet","24 feet","35 feet","19 feet"],"a":1,"why":"A rectangle has 4 sides, so 7 + 5 + 7 + 5 = 24 feet."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How many striped fish have yellow stripes?","c":["2 fish","44 fish","12 fish","4 fish"],"a":2,"why":"6 times 8 is 48 striped fish, and one fourth of 48 is 48 divided by 4, which is 12 fish."},{"skill":"check","q":"Priya says the area of the touch pool is 12 square feet. What mistake did she make?","c":["She used the number of crab pellets by mistake","She added 7 + 5 instead of multiplying","She should have divided 7 feet by 5 feet","She counted the sea stars instead"],"a":1,"why":"Area is length times width, so the area is 7 times 5, which is 35 square feet, not 7 + 5."},{"skill":"number","q":"The text says one fourth of the striped fish have yellow stripes. What does one fourth mean?","c":["Take 4 fish out of the whole tank","1 fish from each of the 4 schools","4 equal parts, then take 1 part","4 parts of any size, then take 1"],"a":2,"why":"One fourth means the whole is split into 4 equal parts and you take 1 of them."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many pellets does each crab get?","c":["4 pellets","27 pellets","45 pellets","3 pellets"],"a":0,"why":"36 pellets divided by 9 crabs is 4 pellets for each crab."},{"skill":"check","q":"Priya guesses that 6 times 8 is about 100. Is her guess reasonable?","c":["Yes, because 8 is close to 10","Yes, because 6 + 8 is about 14","No, the answer should be about 10","No, even 6 times 10 is only 60"],"a":3,"why":"6 times 10 is 60, and 6 times 8 is even less, so 100 is far too big."}]},{"id":"M04-space-model-rockets","subject":"math","level":4,"topic":"space","title":"Launch Day for the Rocket Club","text":["The rocket club at Hillside School is getting ready for launch day. The club has 16 members, and each member builds 3 model rockets. The rockets will fly from a field behind the school.","Each rocket needs 1 small engine. A box of engines costs $12 and holds 6 engines. The club leader, Ms. Reyes, buys just enough boxes for every rocket.","On launch day, the highest rocket flies 325 feet. The lowest rocket flies 198 feet. Ms. Reyes asks the members to find the difference between the two heights.","By the end of the day, 3 fourths of the rockets have been launched. The rest will fly next week."],"vocab":[{"w":"members","pos":"noun","def":"The people who belong to a club or group.","ex":"The rocket club has 16 members."},{"w":"launch","pos":"noun","def":"The moment a rocket is sent up into the air.","ex":"The club is getting ready for launch day."},{"w":"engine","pos":"noun","def":"The part of a machine that gives it the power to move.","ex":"Each rocket needs 1 small engine."},{"w":"difference","pos":"noun","def":"How much bigger or smaller one number is than another.","ex":"Find the difference between the two heights."},{"w":"fourths","pos":"noun","def":"Parts made when a whole is split into 4 equal pieces.","ex":"3 fourths of the rockets have been launched."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"The highest rocket flies 325 feet. What is the value of the 3 in 325?","c":["3 feet","30 feet","3,000 feet","300 feet"],"a":3,"why":"The 3 is in the hundreds place, so it means 300 feet."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many model rockets does the club build?","c":["19 rockets","48 rockets","42 rockets","38 rockets"],"a":1,"why":"16 members times 3 rockets is 48 rockets."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How much does Ms. Reyes spend on boxes of engines?","c":["$96","$576","$72","$108"],"a":0,"why":"48 engines divided by 6 is 8 boxes, and 8 boxes times $12 is $96."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How many rockets will fly next week?","c":["36 rockets","45 rockets","12 rockets","16 rockets"],"a":2,"why":"3 fourths of 48 is 36 rockets launched, and 48 take away 36 leaves 12 rockets."},{"skill":"check","q":"A member says the highest rocket flew 127 feet higher than the lowest. How can you check this answer?","c":["Add 325 + 198 and look for 127","Take 198 away from 127 and look for 325","Add 127 + 198 and look for 325","Multiply 127 by 198 and look for 325"],"a":2,"why":"Adding undoes subtracting, and 127 + 198 = 325, so the answer checks out."},{"skill":"number","q":"What fraction of the rockets will fly next week?","c":["1 fourth","3 fourths","1 third","4 thirds"],"a":0,"why":"3 fourths have flown, so 1 fourth is left to fly."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How much higher did the highest rocket fly than the lowest rocket?","c":["523 feet","137 feet","273 feet","127 feet"],"a":3,"why":"325 feet minus 198 feet is 127 feet."},{"skill":"check","q":"Which estimate best shows that 16 times 3 = 48 is reasonable?","c":["10 times 3 is 30, so 48 is too big","15 times 3 is 45, so 48 is close","16 + 3 is 19, so 48 is too big","20 times 3 is 600, so 48 is too small"],"a":1,"why":"16 is close to 15, and 15 times 3 is 45, which is close to 48."}]},{"id":"M05-sports-relay-race","subject":"math","level":5,"topic":"sports","title":"The Relay Team's Big Race","text":["Amara's school track team runs the 4 by 100 meter relay at the city meet. Four runners each run 100 meters, and they pass a baton from one runner to the next.","Amara runs first in 14.6 seconds. Theo runs second in 15.2 seconds. Lin runs third in 13.9 seconds, and Kofi finishes the race in 14.3 seconds.","The team's goal was to finish in under 60 seconds. Before the meet, Coach Diaz had the team practice for 3 fourths of an hour on Monday and 2 thirds of an hour on Tuesday. She wants to know if all that practice paid off."],"vocab":[{"w":"relay","pos":"noun","def":"A race where team members take turns running parts of the distance.","ex":"The team runs the relay at the city meet."},{"w":"baton","pos":"noun","def":"A short stick that relay runners pass from one to the next.","ex":"Amara passes the baton to Theo."},{"w":"meters","pos":"noun","def":"Units for measuring length or distance.","ex":"Each runner runs 100 meters."},{"w":"goal","pos":"noun","def":"Something you are trying to reach or get done.","ex":"The team's goal was to finish in under 60 seconds."},{"w":"practice","pos":"verb","def":"To do something again and again to get better at it.","ex":"The team practiced on Monday and Tuesday."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"Which runner had the fastest time?","c":["Theo, with 15.2 seconds","Lin, with 13.9 seconds","Amara, with 14.6 seconds","Kofi, with 14.3 seconds"],"a":1,"why":"13.9 seconds is the smallest time, and the smallest time is the fastest."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How much faster was Lin than Theo?","c":["2.7 seconds","0.3 seconds","29.1 seconds","1.3 seconds"],"a":3,"why":"15.2 seconds minus 13.9 seconds is 1.3 seconds."},{"skill":"problem","q":"What was the team's total time, and did they meet their goal?","c":["57.0 seconds, so yes","59.0 seconds, so yes","58.0 seconds, so yes","62.0 seconds, so no"],"a":2,"why":"14.6 + 15.2 + 13.9 + 14.3 = 58.0 seconds, which is under 60 seconds."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How many minutes did the team practice on Monday and Tuesday together?","c":["85 minutes","5 minutes","105 minutes","75 minutes"],"a":0,"why":"3 fourths of 60 minutes is 45 minutes, 2 thirds of 60 minutes is 40 minutes, and 45 + 40 = 85 minutes."},{"skill":"check","q":"Kofi says the team's average time for each runner is about 20 seconds. Is that reasonable?","c":["No, each time is close to 15 seconds","Yes, because 4 runners ran the race","Yes, because 60 seconds divided by 3 is 20","No, it should be about 58 seconds"],"a":0,"why":"Every runner took between 13.9 and 15.2 seconds, so the average must be near 15 seconds, not 20."},{"skill":"number","q":"Which list shows the times from fastest to slowest?","c":["14.3, 13.9, 14.6, 15.2","15.2, 14.6, 14.3, 13.9","13.9, 14.3, 14.6, 15.2","13.9, 14.6, 14.3, 15.2"],"a":2,"why":"13.9 is less than 14.3, which is less than 14.6, which is less than 15.2."},{"skill":"operations","q":"The team's total time is 58.0 seconds. What is the average time for each of the 4 runners?","c":["15.5 seconds","14.5 seconds","232 seconds","14.0 seconds"],"a":1,"why":"58.0 seconds divided by 4 runners is 14.5 seconds."},{"skill":"check","q":"Theo adds the 4 times and gets 5.80 seconds. Which mistake did he make?","c":["He forgot to add Kofi's time at the end","He added Lin's time twice by mistake","He subtracted the times instead of adding","His decimal point is in the wrong place"],"a":3,"why":"The real total is 58.0 seconds, so 5.80 has the decimal point one place too far to the left."}]},{"id":"M06-money-bracelet-sale","subject":"math","level":6,"topic":"money","title":"The Bracelet Booth","text":["Rafael and his cousin Noor sell handmade bracelets at a weekend craft fair. Before the fair, they spend $24 on beads and string for their supplies. They decide to sell each bracelet for $3.","On Saturday, they sell 18 bracelets. On Sunday, they sell bracelets at a steady rate of 4 bracelets per hour for 3 hours. Noor notices that for every 3 bracelets they sell, 2 are blue.","After the fair, the cousins figure out their profit, which is the money they took in minus the money they spent. They plan to save 25 percent of the profit for next year's supplies. They will split the rest of the profit equally."],"vocab":[{"w":"supplies","pos":"noun","def":"The things you need to make or do something.","ex":"They spend $24 on supplies."},{"w":"rate","pos":"noun","def":"How many of something happen in each unit of time.","ex":"They sell bracelets at a rate of 4 per hour."},{"w":"steady","pos":"adjective","def":"Staying the same, without speeding up or slowing down.","ex":"They sell at a steady rate on Sunday."},{"w":"profit","pos":"noun","def":"The money left after you take away what you spent.","ex":"The cousins figure out their profit."},{"w":"percent","pos":"noun","def":"A part out of 100.","ex":"They save 25 percent of the profit."},{"w":"equally","pos":"adverb","def":"In a way that gives each person the same amount.","ex":"They split the rest equally."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"Which ratio compares blue bracelets to all bracelets sold?","c":["3 to 2","1 to 3","2 to 3","2 to 1"],"a":2,"why":"For every 3 bracelets sold, 2 are blue, so the ratio of blue to all is 2 to 3."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many bracelets do they sell on Sunday?","c":["7 bracelets","12 bracelets","4 bracelets","16 bracelets"],"a":1,"why":"4 bracelets per hour times 3 hours is 12 bracelets."},{"skill":"problem","q":"What is their profit for the whole weekend?","c":["$90","$30","$54","$66"],"a":3,"why":"They sell 18 + 12 = 30 bracelets for $3 each, which is $90, and $90 minus $24 is $66."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How much money will each cousin get after they save 25 percent of the profit?","c":["$24.75","$33.00","$49.50","$16.50"],"a":0,"why":"25 percent of $66 is $16.50, so $49.50 is left, and $49.50 divided by 2 is $24.75."},{"skill":"check","q":"Rafael says 25 percent of $66 is $25. Which is the best way to see he is wrong?","c":["25 percent always means exactly $25","25 percent is close to half of $66","The profit is $90, so 25 percent is $25","1 fourth of $66 is less than $17"],"a":3,"why":"25 percent is 1 fourth, and 1 fourth of $66 is $16.50, which is far less than $25."},{"skill":"number","q":"They sell 30 bracelets in all. How many of them are blue?","c":["10 bracelets","20 bracelets","15 bracelets","2 bracelets"],"a":1,"why":"2 out of every 3 are blue, 30 divided by 3 is 10 groups, and 10 groups times 2 is 20 blue bracelets."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How much money do they take in from selling all 30 bracelets?","c":["$90","$66","$114","$54"],"a":0,"why":"30 bracelets times $3 is $90."},{"skill":"check","q":"Noor thinks their profit should be more than $90. Is she right?","c":["Yes, because they sold 30 bracelets in all","Yes, profit adds the $24 they spent","No, profit takes away the $24 spent","No, profit is only Saturday's sales"],"a":2,"why":"Profit is the money taken in minus the money spent, so it must be less than $90."}]},{"id":"M07-games-puzzle-points","subject":"math","level":7,"topic":"games","title":"Points Above and Below Zero","text":["At the library's game night, Kai and Lucia play a puzzle game on a tablet. Each solved puzzle earns 15 points. Each hint costs 6 points, so a score can drop below zero and become a negative number.","Kai starts the second round with a score of -12 because he used too many hints in the first round. In the second round, he solves 3 puzzles without any hints. Lucia solves 8 puzzles and uses 4 hints in the same round.","The game also tracks accuracy. Lucia answered 20 puzzle questions and got 17 of them right. The top score at the library this month is 120 points, and Lucia wants to know what percent of that score she has reached."],"vocab":[{"w":"hint","pos":"noun","def":"A small clue that helps you find an answer.","ex":"Each hint costs 6 points."},{"w":"negative","pos":"adjective","def":"Less than zero.","ex":"A score of -12 is a negative number."},{"w":"round","pos":"noun","def":"One part of a game that is played in turns or stages.","ex":"Kai starts the second round with -12 points."},{"w":"accuracy","pos":"noun","def":"How often you are correct, compared with how many tries you make.","ex":"The game tracks each player's accuracy."},{"w":"percent","pos":"noun","def":"A part out of 100.","ex":"Lucia wants to know what percent of the top score she reached."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"Which score is lower, -12 or -6?","c":["-12 is lower, since it is farther below 0","-6 is lower, since 6 is less than 12","They are equal, since both are below 0","-6 is lower, since it is closer to 0 than -12"],"a":0,"why":"On a number line, -12 is farther to the left of 0 than -6, so -12 is lower."},{"skill":"operations","q":"What is Kai's score after he solves 3 puzzles?","c":["57 points","45 points","33 points","-57 points"],"a":2,"why":"3 puzzles times 15 is 45 points, and -12 + 45 = 33 points."},{"skill":"problem","q":"What is Lucia's score for the second round?","c":["144 points","96 points","120 points","114 points"],"a":1,"why":"8 puzzles times 15 is 120 points, 4 hints times 6 is 24 points, and 120 minus 24 is 96 points."},{"skill":"problem","q":"Lucia's round score is 96 points. What percent of the top score of 120 points has she reached?","c":["96 percent","24 percent","125 percent","80 percent"],"a":3,"why":"96 divided by 120 is 0.8, and 0.8 is the same as 80 percent."},{"skill":"check","q":"Kai says his score of 33 and Lucia's score of 96 add up to 63 points. Which mistake did he make?","c":["He added 8 puzzles instead of 3","He forgot to count Lucia's hints","He used 15 points for each hint","He treated his 33 points as -33"],"a":3,"why":"-33 + 96 = 63, but Kai has 33 points, so the real total is 33 + 96 = 129 points."},{"skill":"number","q":"Lucia got 17 of 20 puzzle questions right. Which shows her accuracy?","c":["17 percent","0.17","85 percent","20 percent"],"a":2,"why":"17 out of 20 is the same as 85 out of 100, which is 85 percent."},{"skill":"operations","q":"What is the ratio of Lucia's hints to her solved puzzles, in simplest form?","c":["1 to 2","2 to 1","4 to 12","1 to 4"],"a":0,"why":"4 hints to 8 puzzles is 4 to 8, and dividing both by 4 gives 1 to 2."},{"skill":"check","q":"A player has -12 points and then uses 2 more hints. A friend says the new score is 0. Is that reasonable?","c":["Yes, 2 hints of 6 points make up the 12","No, it falls further, to -24 points","Yes, a score can never drop below 0","No, the new score should be -6 points"],"a":1,"why":"Each hint takes away 6 points, so -12 minus 12 is -24 points, not 0."}]},{"id":"M08-vehicles-electric-bus","subject":"math","level":8,"topic":"vehicles","title":"The Electric Bus on Route 9","text":["Our town is testing a new electric bus on Route 9. At 7 in the morning, its battery is charged to 100 percent. The driver, Mr. Haddad, records the battery level after each hour of driving.","The battery level drops at a steady rate of 12 percent per hour. Mr. Haddad writes this as an equation: L = 100 minus 12h. In the equation, L is the battery percent and h is the number of hours since 7 in the morning.","The bus must return to the station to charge before the battery falls below 20 percent. The station is 4 miles east and 3 miles north of the last stop, and a straight road connects them. On a full charge, the bus can travel about 150 miles."],"vocab":[{"w":"records","pos":"verb","def":"Writes down information so it can be used later.","ex":"Mr. Haddad records the battery level each hour."},{"w":"steady","pos":"adjective","def":"Staying the same over time, without speeding up or slowing down.","ex":"The battery drops at a steady rate."},{"w":"rate","pos":"noun","def":"How much something changes in each unit of time.","ex":"The battery drops at a rate of 12 percent per hour."},{"w":"equation","pos":"noun","def":"A math sentence that says two amounts are equal.","ex":"L = 100 minus 12h is an equation."},{"w":"connects","pos":"verb","def":"Joins two things or places together.","ex":"A straight road connects the stop and the station."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"In the equation L = 100 minus 12h, what does the 12 tell you?","c":["The battery starts the day at 12 percent","The bus drives for 12 hours","The bus makes 12 stops each hour","The battery loses 12 percent per hour"],"a":3,"why":"The 12 is multiplied by the hours, so the battery goes down by 12 percent for every hour of driving."},{"skill":"operations","q":"What is the battery level after 5 hours of driving?","c":["40 percent","60 percent","88 percent","52 percent"],"a":0,"why":"100 minus 12 times 5 is 100 minus 60, which is 40 percent."},{"skill":"problem","q":"Mr. Haddad checks the battery only at whole hours. What is the last whole hour when the battery is still at 20 percent or more?","c":["After 8 hours","After 7 hours","After 6 hours","After 5 hours"],"a":2,"why":"After 6 hours the level is 100 minus 72, which is 28 percent, but after 7 hours it is 16 percent, which is too low."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How long is the straight road from the last stop to the station?","c":["7 miles","5 miles","25 miles","12 miles"],"a":1,"why":"The road is the long side of a right triangle, and 3 times 3 + 4 times 4 = 25, so the road is the square root of 25, which is 5 miles."},{"skill":"check","q":"To find when the battery reaches 40 percent, Ana writes: Step 1, 12h = 100 + 40. Step 2, 12h = 140. Step 3, h is about 11.7. Where is the mistake?","c":["Step 2, because 100 + 40 is not 140","Step 1, it should be 100 minus 40","Step 3, she should multiply 140 by 12","There is no mistake in her work at all"],"a":1,"why":"From 100 minus 12h = 40 you get 12h = 100 minus 40, which is 60, so h = 5 hours."},{"skill":"number","q":"Which describes the graph of the battery level over time?","c":["A straight line going up by 12 each hour","A curve that drops faster and faster","A flat line that stays at 100 percent","A straight line going down 12 each hour"],"a":3,"why":"The level drops by the same 12 percent every hour, so the graph is a straight line that slopes down."},{"skill":"operations","q":"A full charge lasts about 150 miles. About how many miles can the bus travel on 40 percent of a charge?","c":["110 miles","40 miles","60 miles","90 miles"],"a":2,"why":"40 percent of 150 miles is 0.4 times 150, which is 60 miles."},{"skill":"check","q":"Mr. Haddad estimates the battery will be near 50 percent at 11 in the morning. Is this reasonable?","c":["Yes, after 4 hours it is 52 percent","No, it will be empty by then","No, it will be at 88 percent","Yes, after 11 hours it is 50 percent"],"a":0,"why":"11 in the morning is 4 hours after 7, and 100 minus 48 is 52 percent, which is close to 50."}]},{"id":"M09-music-concert-tickets","subject":"math","level":9,"topic":"music","title":"Tickets for the Spring Concert","text":["The school orchestra is holding a spring concert to raise money for new instruments. Student tickets cost $4 each, and adult tickets cost $9 each. The music teacher, Ms. Abara, hopes to raise at least $1,500.","By the night of the concert, the orchestra has sold 250 tickets in all, and the ticket money adds up to $1,650. The treasurer, a student named Wren, wants to figure out how many of each kind of ticket were sold.","The orchestra also plans the program carefully. The five pieces in the program last 6, 9, 4, 12, and 9 minutes. Between each pair of pieces, the musicians take a 2 minute pause to retune their instruments."],"vocab":[{"w":"raise","pos":"verb","def":"To collect money for a goal or a cause.","ex":"The concert will raise money for new instruments."},{"w":"treasurer","pos":"noun","def":"The person in a group who keeps track of the money.","ex":"Wren is the treasurer for the orchestra."},{"w":"program","pos":"noun","def":"The list of pieces that will be played at a show, in order.","ex":"The program has five pieces."},{"w":"pause","pos":"noun","def":"A short stop before starting again.","ex":"The musicians take a 2 minute pause."},{"w":"retune","pos":"verb","def":"To adjust an instrument again so its notes sound right.","ex":"They pause to retune their instruments."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"Let s be the number of student tickets and a be the number of adult tickets. Which equation matches the total number of tickets?","c":["s + a = 1,650","s + a = 250","4s + 9a = 250","s = a + 250"],"a":1,"why":"Every ticket is either a student ticket or an adult ticket, and 250 tickets were sold, so s + a = 250."},{"skill":"operations","q":"If all 250 tickets had been student tickets, how much money would that be?","c":["$2,250","$254","$1,000","$1,650"],"a":2,"why":"250 tickets times $4 is $1,000."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How many adult tickets were sold?","c":["130 adult tickets","120 adult tickets","183 adult tickets","72 adult tickets"],"a":0,"why":"All student tickets would bring $1,000, and each adult ticket adds $5 more, so the extra $650 divided by $5 is 130 adult tickets."},{"skill":"problem","q":"How much of the ticket money came from student tickets?","c":["$1,170","$520","$1,000","$480"],"a":3,"why":"250 minus 130 is 120 student tickets, and 120 times $4 is $480."},{"skill":"check","q":"Lee says 200 adult tickets were sold. Which quick check shows that is not reasonable?","c":["200 times $9 is already over $1,650","200 is less than the 250 tickets sold","Adult tickets cost more than student tickets","200 is not a multiple of 9, so it fails"],"a":0,"why":"200 adult tickets would bring in $1,800 by themselves, which is more than the $1,650 total."},{"skill":"number","q":"What is the median length of the five pieces?","c":["4 minutes","9 minutes","8 minutes","12 minutes"],"a":1,"why":"In order the lengths are 4, 6, 9, 9 and 12 minutes, so the middle value is 9 minutes."},{"skill":"operations","q":"What is the mean length of the five pieces?","c":["9 minutes","10 minutes","40 minutes","8 minutes"],"a":3,"why":"6 + 9 + 4 + 12 + 9 = 40 minutes, and 40 divided by 5 is 8 minutes."},{"skill":"check","q":"Wren says the music and the pauses together last 48 minutes. Is that reasonable?","c":["No, there are 5 pauses, so it is 50","No, the music alone lasts 48 minutes","Yes, 40 + 4 times 2 is 48 minutes","No, it should be 40 + 2, or 42 minutes"],"a":2,"why":"There are 4 pauses between 5 pieces, so 40 minutes of music + 4 times 2 minutes of pauses is 48 minutes."}]},{"id":"M10-tech-study-app","subject":"math","level":10,"topic":"tech","title":"How Fast Can a Study App Grow?","text":["A group of high school students builds a study app for their school. The app has 50 users in the week it launches. Each week after that, the number of users doubles, because students share the app with their friends.","The team writes the growth as U = 50 times 2 to the power w, where U is the number of users and w is the number of weeks after the launch. Growth like this often slows down, though, once most of the people who want the app already have it.","To keep costs low, the team picks a server plan that charges $5 a month plus $0.02 for each user.","To reward users, the app shows a random badge each time someone finishes a quiz. There are 10 badges in all: 4 are stars, 3 are rockets, 2 are planets, and 1 is a rare comet. Every badge has the same chance of being shown."],"vocab":[{"w":"doubles","pos":"verb","def":"Becomes 2 times as big.","ex":"The number of users doubles each week."},{"w":"growth","pos":"noun","def":"The way something gets bigger over time.","ex":"The team writes the growth as an equation."},{"w":"server","pos":"noun","def":"A computer that stores an app's data and sends it to users.","ex":"The server plan charges $5 a month."},{"w":"random","pos":"adjective","def":"Picked by chance, with no plan or pattern.","ex":"The app shows a random badge."},{"w":"rare","pos":"adjective","def":"Not found or seen very often.","ex":"Only 1 of the 10 badges is the rare comet."}],"questions":[{"skill":"number","q":"Which is the same as 2 to the power 4?","c":["2 times 4","4 times 4 times 4 times 4","2 times 2 times 2 times 2","2 + 2 + 2 + 2"],"a":2,"why":"2 to the power 4 means 4 twos multiplied together, which is 16."},{"skill":"operations","q":"How many users does the app have 4 weeks after the launch?","c":["400 users","250 users","1,600 users","800 users"],"a":3,"why":"50 times 2 to the power 4 is 50 times 16, which is 800 users."},{"skill":"problem","q":"What is the monthly server cost 4 weeks after the launch?","c":["$16","$21","$13","$805"],"a":1,"why":"After 4 weeks there are 800 users, 800 times $0.02 is $16, and $16 + $5 is $21."},{"skill":"problem","q":"The team can spend at most $45 a month on the server. What is the greatest number of users the plan can cover?","c":["2,000 users","2,250 users","1,600 users","2,500 users"],"a":0,"why":"$45 minus $5 is $40, and $40 divided by $0.02 is 2,000 users."},{"skill":"check","q":"A student uses the equation to predict over 50 million users after 20 weeks. What is the best response?","c":["The math is wrong, because 2 times 20 is only 40","The math fits, but a school has far fewer people","The math is wrong, since 50 times 20 is 1,000","It is certain, because doubling patterns never stop"],"a":1,"why":"50 times 2 to the power 20 is about 52 million, but the text says growth like this often slows once most people have the app."},{"skill":"number","q":"What is the probability that a random badge is the rare comet?","c":["1 out of 4","1 out of 9","10 out of 10","1 out of 10"],"a":3,"why":"1 of the 10 badges is a comet, so the probability is 1 out of 10."},{"skill":"operations","q":"What is the probability that a random badge is a star or a planet?","c":["60 percent","8 percent","40 percent","6 percent"],"a":0,"why":"4 stars + 2 planets is 6 of the 10 badges, and 6 out of 10 is 60 percent."},{"skill":"check","q":"Sami finishes 2 quizzes and says the chance of getting the comet both times is 2 out of 10. What went wrong?","c":["She should have used 1 out of 4 badges","Each quiz should be 1 out of 5 chances","She added when she should multiply","She forgot to count the 3 rocket badges"],"a":2,"why":"Each quiz has a 1 out of 10 chance, so both times is 1 tenth times 1 tenth, which is 1 out of 100."}]},{"id":"S01-animals-frog-life","subject":"science","level":1,"topic":"animals","title":"A Tadpole's Big Change","text":["A frog starts life as a tiny egg. The egg is in a pond. Soon a baby frog hatches, or comes out of the egg. This baby is called a tadpole.","A tadpole has a long tail. It swims like a fish. It breathes under water with gills.","Then the tadpole grows back legs. Next it grows front legs. Its tail gets smaller and smaller. It grows lungs to breathe air. At last, the frog hops out of the pond."],"vocab":[{"w":"tadpole","pos":"noun","def":"A baby frog that lives in water and has a tail.","ex":"The tadpole swims like a fish."},{"w":"hatches","pos":"verb","def":"Comes out of an egg.","ex":"A baby frog hatches from the egg."},{"w":"gills","pos":"noun","def":"Body parts that let an animal breathe under water.","ex":"A tadpole breathes with gills."},{"w":"lungs","pos":"noun","def":"Body parts that let an animal breathe air.","ex":"The frog grows lungs to breathe air."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["What frogs like to eat","Why ponds have fish","How a frog grows from an egg","Where frogs go to sleep at night"],"a":2,"why":"Each part of the text tells one step in how an egg becomes a frog."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"hatches\" mean?","word":"hatches","c":["Comes out of an egg","Swims very fast","Grows a long new tail","Hops onto land"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the baby frog hatches, or comes out of the egg."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What does a tadpole grow first?","c":["Its front legs","Its back legs","Its lungs","A new tail"],"a":1,"why":"The text says the tadpole grows back legs, and next it grows front legs."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens right after the tadpole grows front legs?","c":["It hatches from an egg","It swims like a fish","It lays eggs in the pond","Its tail gets smaller"],"a":3,"why":"The sentence after the front legs says its tail gets smaller and smaller."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a tadpole breathes air. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["It breathes under water with gills.","At last, the frog hops out of the pond.","Then the tadpole grows back legs.","A tadpole has a long tail."],"a":0,"why":"The text says a tadpole breathes under water with gills."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["A Day at the Beach","Fish That Live in Ponds","From Egg to Frog","The Fastest Frog"],"a":2,"why":"The text follows a frog from an egg to a frog that hops on land."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"What are \"gills\"?","word":"gills","c":["Legs for hopping on land","Eyes for seeing in the dark","Tails for swimming fast","Parts to breathe in water"],"a":3,"why":"The text says a tadpole breathes under water with gills."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which sentence explains how the frog can live out of the pond?","c":["A tadpole has a long tail.","It grows lungs to breathe air.","It swims like a fish.","This baby is called a tadpole."],"a":1,"why":"Lungs let the frog breathe air, so it can leave the water."}]},{"id":"S02-nature-seed-travel","subject":"science","level":2,"topic":"nature","title":"How Seeds Get Around","text":["Plants cannot walk, but their seeds can travel far from home. Seeds need space to grow. A seed that lands under its parent plant gets less sunlight.","Some seeds ride the wind. A dandelion seed has a fluffy top. It works like a tiny parachute, so the seed falls slowly. A light wind, or breeze, carries it far away. Maple seeds have a thin wing. They spin as they fall.","Other seeds hitch a ride. They get a free trip on an animal. A burr is a seed case with tiny hooks. The hooks catch on an animal's fur. Later the burr drops off in a new place."],"vocab":[{"w":"parachute","pos":"noun","def":"Something that catches the air so a thing falls slowly.","ex":"The fluffy top works like a tiny parachute."},{"w":"breeze","pos":"noun","def":"A light wind.","ex":"A breeze carries the seed far away."},{"w":"hitch","pos":"verb","def":"To get a free ride on something that is going your way.","ex":"Some seeds hitch a ride on animals."},{"w":"burr","pos":"noun","def":"A seed case covered with tiny hooks.","ex":"The burr stuck to the dog's fur."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How seeds travel to new places","How tall maple trees can grow","Why some animals have thick fur","What dandelions smell like"],"a":0,"why":"The text tells how seeds ride the wind or ride on animals to new places."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"breeze\" mean?","word":"breeze","c":["A fluffy seed","A heavy rain","A tiny hook","A light wind"],"a":3,"why":"The text says a light wind, or breeze, carries the seed."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why is it good for a seed to travel away from its parent plant?","c":["It can find more animals to ride","It can get more sunlight to grow","It can spin faster in the wind","It can grow a fluffy top"],"a":1,"why":"The text says a seed that lands under its parent gets less sunlight."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens after a burr catches on an animal's fur?","c":["The burr grows a set of new hooks","The animal stops to eat the burr","The burr drops off in a new place","The wind lifts the burr up high"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the burr later drops off in a new place."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks all seeds travel by wind. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["A dandelion seed has a fluffy top.","Maple seeds have a thin wing.","They get a free trip on an animal.","Some seeds ride the wind."],"a":2,"why":"Some seeds get a free trip on an animal, not on the wind."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["The Biggest Maple Tree Ever","Animals With Soft Fur","A Rainy Day in Spring","Seeds That Ride and Fly"],"a":3,"why":"The whole text is about seeds riding animals and flying on the wind."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does a \"parachute\" do for the seed?","word":"parachute","c":["Helps it fall slowly","Helps it hook onto fur","Keeps it warm at night","Gives it water to drink"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the fluffy top works like a parachute, so the seed falls slowly."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure what a burr is. Which sentence helps most?","c":["Later the burr drops off in a new place.","A burr is a seed case with tiny hooks.","Other seeds hitch a ride.","Plants cannot walk, but their seeds can travel far from home."],"a":1,"why":"This sentence tells exactly what a burr is."}]},{"id":"S03-dinosaurs-feathers","subject":"science","level":3,"topic":"dinosaurs","title":"Dinosaurs in Fluffy Coats","text":["Long ago, some dinosaurs had feathers. For many years, scientists only found dinosaur bones. But bones do not show what covered the skin.","Then, in 1996, scientists in China found a special fossil. A fossil is a part or print of a living thing from long ago, saved in rock. This small dinosaur is called Sinosauropteryx (SY-no-sor-OP-ter-ix). Fine mud at the bottom of an old lake had covered it. The mud kept a print, or pressed mark, of its fuzzy feathers.","These feathers were short and simple, like fluff. They were not made for flying. They may have helped keep the dinosaur warm. Scientists also studied tiny shapes inside the feathers. These shapes showed that the tail had reddish brown and white stripes, or bands of color."],"vocab":[{"w":"fossil","pos":"noun","def":"A part or print of a living thing from long ago, saved in rock.","ex":"Scientists found a dinosaur fossil in China."},{"w":"print","pos":"noun","def":"A mark pressed into something soft.","ex":"The mud kept a print of the feathers."},{"w":"fuzzy","pos":"adjective","def":"Covered in soft, short bits, like fluff.","ex":"The dinosaur had fuzzy feathers."},{"w":"stripes","pos":"noun","def":"Long, thin bands of color.","ex":"The tail had reddish brown and white stripes."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How dinosaur bones slowly turn into stone","Why many birds fly south in the winter","How scientists dig for fossils in China","How we know some dinosaurs had feathers"],"a":3,"why":"The text tells how a fossil showed that some dinosaurs had feathers."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"fossil\"?","word":"fossil","c":["Soft mud from the bottom of an old lake","Part or print of old life, saved in rock","A short, fluffy feather used to stay warm","A scientist who studies rocks and bones"],"a":1,"why":"The text says a fossil is a part or print of a living thing from long ago, saved in rock."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why did scientists not know about dinosaur feathers for many years?","c":["Bones do not show what covered the skin","Dinosaurs only lived in the land of China","Feathers were too heavy to keep in mud","No one had ever looked inside a lake"],"a":0,"why":"The text says scientists only found bones, and bones do not show what covered the skin."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What helped keep a print of the feathers?","c":["Wind that blew across the lake","Bright sunlight on the bones","Fine mud that covered the dinosaur","Ice that froze over the whole old lake"],"a":2,"why":"The text says fine mud covered the dinosaur and kept a print of its feathers."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks these feathers helped the dinosaur fly. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Long ago, some dinosaurs had feathers.","They were not made for flying.","But bones do not show what covered the skin.","Scientists also studied tiny shapes inside the feathers."],"a":1,"why":"The text says the feathers were not made for flying."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that some dinosaurs had feathers?","c":["But bones do not show what covered the skin.","Fine mud at the bottom of an old lake had covered it.","For many years, scientists only found dinosaur bones.","The mud kept a print of its fuzzy feathers."],"a":3,"why":"The print in the mud shows the feathers that the dinosaur really had."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what are \"stripes\"?","word":"stripes","c":["Small bones in a tail","Tiny shapes inside a feather","Bands of color","Soft bits of fluff"],"a":2,"why":"The text says stripes are bands of color."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure what color the feathers were. Where should they look?","c":["The last two sentences of the text","The first sentence of the text","The sentence about the old lake","The sentence that names the dinosaur"],"a":0,"why":"The last two sentences tell about the tiny shapes and the colored stripes on the tail."}]},{"id":"S04-ocean-salty-sea","subject":"science","level":4,"topic":"ocean","title":"Where the Ocean Gets Its Salt","text":["Ocean water is very salty, but the water in most rivers tastes fresh. So where does the ocean get its salt? The answer starts on land, with rain and rock.","Rainwater is slightly acidic, which means it can slowly eat away at rock. As rain falls, it wears off tiny bits of rock. This slow breaking down is called weathering. The rain picks up minerals, the natural materials in rock, including the bits that form salt. Streams and rivers carry them to the sea.","River water holds a little salt, but too little to taste. When the water reaches the ocean, the sun warms it. Some of the water turns into vapor, a gas that rises into the air. This change is called evaporation. The salt cannot rise with the water, so it stays behind. Over a very long time, salt built up in the sea. Today, about 3.5 percent of the weight of seawater is salt. Some salt also comes from openings in the sea floor."],"vocab":[{"w":"acidic","pos":"adjective","def":"Able to slowly eat away at hard things like rock.","ex":"Rainwater is slightly acidic."},{"w":"weathering","pos":"noun","def":"The slow breaking down of rock by rain, wind and ice.","ex":"Weathering wears tiny bits off big rocks."},{"w":"minerals","pos":"noun","def":"Natural materials that make up rock.","ex":"Rivers carry minerals to the sea."},{"w":"vapor","pos":"noun","def":"A gas made when a liquid such as water warms up.","ex":"Water vapor rises into the air."},{"w":"evaporation","pos":"noun","def":"The change of a liquid into a gas.","ex":"Evaporation leaves the salt behind in the sea."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["Why rivers are cleaner than lakes","How the ocean became salty","How rain clouds form over land","Why fish need salty water"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains where the ocean's salt comes from and why it stays."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"weathering\" mean?","word":"weathering","c":["A sudden change in the day's weather","Water rising into the air","The slow breaking down of rock","Salt building up in the sea"],"a":2,"why":"The text says this slow breaking down is called weathering."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What carries minerals from the land to the sea?","c":["Streams and rivers","Strong winds and clouds","Fish swimming upstream","Melting ice and snow"],"a":0,"why":"The text says streams and rivers carry the minerals to the sea."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens to the salt when ocean water evaporates?","c":["It rises into the air with the water","It turns into rain and falls on land","It sinks into the rocks below","It stays behind in the sea"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the salt cannot rise with the water, so it stays behind."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks river water has no salt at all. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["As rain falls, it wears off tiny bits of rock.","This change is called evaporation.","Over a very long time, salt built up in the sea.","River water holds a little salt."],"a":3,"why":"The text says river water holds a little salt, but too little to taste."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["From Rocks to the Sea","Why Fish Like Salt","Rainy Days at the Lake","How Clouds Are Made"],"a":0,"why":"The text follows salt from rocks on land all the way to the sea."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is \"vapor\"?","word":"vapor","c":["A kind of salty rock","Water as a gas that rises","A stream that runs to the sea","An opening in the sea floor"],"a":1,"why":"The text says vapor is a gas that rises into the air."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders how rain can wear away rock. Which sentence helps most?","c":["Streams and rivers carry them to the sea.","This change is called evaporation.","Rainwater is slightly acidic.","Some salt comes from openings in the sea floor."],"a":2,"why":"Acidic rain can slowly eat away at rock, so this sentence answers the question."}]},{"id":"S05-space-red-mars","subject":"science","level":5,"topic":"space","title":"The Planet With a Dusty Coat","text":["On a clear night, Mars can look like a small reddish dot in the sky. People have long called it the Red Planet. But why is it red?","The rocks and dust on Mars hold a lot of iron. Over a very long time, the iron reacted, or joined, with oxygen. This change made iron oxide, the same kind of material as rust. An old bike chain left out in the rain turns reddish brown for a similar reason. On Mars, a layer of rusty dust covers much of the ground.","The dust is very fine, which means each grain is tiny, like powder. Wind can lift it easily. Some dust storms grow large enough to cover almost the whole planet. Dust also floats in the thin air and gives the daytime sky a tan color.","Below the surface, or top layer of ground, many rocks are not red at all. Rovers are robot vehicles that drive across Mars. One rover drilled into a rock. The rock powder inside was gray. So Mars is red mostly on the outside, like a planet wearing a dusty coat."],"vocab":[{"w":"reacted","pos":"verb","def":"Joined with something else and changed into something new.","ex":"The iron reacted with oxygen."},{"w":"fine","pos":"adjective","def":"Made of very tiny bits.","ex":"The dust on Mars is very fine."},{"w":"surface","pos":"noun","def":"The top or outside layer of something.","ex":"Red dust covers the surface of Mars."},{"w":"rovers","pos":"noun","def":"Robot vehicles that drive across another world.","ex":"Rovers drive across Mars and study rocks."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How rovers drive across Mars","Why dust storms happen on Earth","Why Mars has a red color","How to see Mars on a clear night"],"a":2,"why":"The text explains that rusty dust gives Mars its red color."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"reacted\" mean?","word":"reacted","c":["Floated up into the thin air","Joined and changed","Was drilled into by a rover","Fell to the ground like rain"],"a":1,"why":"The text says the iron reacted, or joined, with oxygen and made something new."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What caused the iron on Mars to become iron oxide?","c":["Wind lifted the dust","A rover drilled into it","Rain fell on an old bike chain","The iron joined with oxygen"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the iron joined with oxygen, and this change made iron oxide."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why can wind lift the dust on Mars so easily?","c":["The grains are tiny, like powder","The dust is made of gray rock","The air on Mars is thick and heavy","The dust is wet from rain"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the dust is very fine, with tiny grains like powder."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks Mars is red all the way through. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["The rock powder inside was gray.","Wind can lift it easily.","People have long called it the Red Planet.","On Mars, a layer of rusty dust covers much of the ground."],"a":0,"why":"Gray powder from inside a rock shows that Mars is not red all the way through."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that Mars is red because of rusty dust?","c":["Rovers are robot vehicles that drive across Mars.","On Mars, a layer of rusty dust covers much of the ground.","On a clear night, Mars can look like a small reddish dot.","Some dust storms grow large enough to cover almost the whole planet."],"a":1,"why":"A layer of rusty dust over the ground is what gives Mars its red look."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"fine\" mean in \"very fine\" dust?","word":"fine","c":["Good or just okay","Bright red in color","Heavy, wet and sticky","Made of tiny grains"],"a":3,"why":"The text says fine means each grain is tiny, like powder."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which question does the text answer?","c":["How long does it take to fly to Mars?","How many rovers are on Mars today?","Why is the daytime sky on Mars tan?","How cold does Mars get at night?"],"a":2,"why":"The text says dust floating in the thin air gives the daytime sky a tan color."}]},{"id":"S06-food-popcorn-pop","subject":"science","level":6,"topic":"food","title":"What Makes Popcorn Pop","text":["A popcorn kernel is a small, hard seed. Yet when it is heated, it bursts into a fluffy snack many times bigger. The secret is a tiny amount of water sealed inside each kernel.","The outside of the kernel is a tough shell called the hull. The hull is strong and tightly sealed, so water cannot easily get out, or escape. Inside the hull is starch mixed with that bit of water. Starch is the filling material also found in rice and bread.","When the kernel is heated, the water turns into steam. Steam needs far more room than water, but the hull keeps it trapped. Pressure, the push of the trapped steam against the hull, builds higher and higher. At the same time, the heat softens the starch into a thick, gooey paste.","At about 180 degrees Celsius, the hull finally splits open. The steam rushes out and puffs the soft starch into a white foam. The foam cools and hardens in a fraction of a second, giving popcorn its crunchy shape. Kernels that are too dry often fail to pop, because they do not have enough water to make steam."],"vocab":[{"w":"kernel","pos":"noun","def":"A small, hard seed of corn or another grain.","ex":"Each popcorn kernel holds a little water."},{"w":"hull","pos":"noun","def":"The tough outer shell of a seed.","ex":"The hull splits open when the kernel pops."},{"w":"escape","pos":"verb","def":"To get out of a place.","ex":"The hull keeps the water from escaping."},{"w":"starch","pos":"noun","def":"A filling material found in foods such as rice, bread and corn.","ex":"The starch puffs into a white foam."},{"w":"pressure","pos":"noun","def":"A steady push against something.","ex":"Pressure builds inside the kernel."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How heat and steam make popcorn pop","Why rice and bread are filling foods","How farmers grow corn in hot places","Why some snacks are crunchy and salty"],"a":0,"why":"The text explains how heated water turns to steam and bursts the kernel open."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"pressure\" mean?","word":"pressure","c":["The heat coming from a pan","A tough outer shell","A push of trapped steam","A soft and gooey paste"],"a":2,"why":"The text says pressure is the push of the trapped steam against the hull."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens right after the hull splits open?","c":["The water turns into steam","The starch softens into a gooey paste","The kernel becomes too dry","Steam puffs the starch into foam"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the steam rushes out and puffs the soft starch into a white foam."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does pressure build up inside the kernel?","c":["The starch hardens into a foam","The hull traps the steam inside","The kernel loses all of its water","The foam cools in a fraction of a second"],"a":1,"why":"Steam needs more room than water, but the hull keeps it trapped."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks any kernel will pop as long as it is heated. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["The secret is a tiny amount of water sealed inside each kernel.","When the kernel is heated, the water turns into steam.","Kernels that are too dry often fail to pop.","The outside of the kernel is a tough shell called the hull."],"a":2,"why":"The text says kernels that are too dry often fail to pop."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["Steam Under Pressure","Growing Corn in a Garden","The Story of Bread","Salty Snacks Around the World"],"a":0,"why":"The text shows how trapped steam and pressure make a kernel burst."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"escape\" mean?","word":"escape","c":["Turn into steam","Get out","Get hot","Split open"],"a":1,"why":"The text says water cannot easily get out, or escape."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is confused about what is inside the hull. Which sentence helps most?","c":["The outside of the kernel is a tough shell called the hull.","At about 180 degrees Celsius, the hull finally splits open.","The foam cools and hardens in a fraction of a second.","Inside the hull is starch mixed with that bit of water."],"a":3,"why":"This sentence names what is inside the hull: starch and a bit of water."}]},{"id":"S07-tech-touchscreen","subject":"science","level":7,"topic":"tech","title":"How a Screen Feels a Finger","text":["A touchscreen on a phone or tablet can tell exactly where a finger lands, even though nothing on the glass moves. Most of these screens work by sensing electricity, not pressure. This explains a winter puzzle: tapping with an ordinary glove often does nothing.","Under the top layer of glass is a grid, a crisscross pattern of rows and columns made of thin lines. The lines are made of a material that conducts electricity, which means electricity can flow through it. They are so thin and clear that the eye cannot see them. The screen keeps a small electric charge, a tiny store of electricity, on this grid and checks it constantly, without stopping.","The human body also conducts electricity, because it contains a lot of salty water. When a fingertip comes close to the glass, it pulls away a little of the charge near that spot. A chip inside the device compares every part of the grid many times each second. It finds the row and column where the charge changed, and that gives the exact location of the finger. The chip can track two fingers at once, which is how a pinch can zoom in on a picture.","A wool or cotton glove is an insulator, a material that blocks electricity, so the screen cannot sense the finger inside. Some winter gloves have fingertips woven with thread that conducts electricity, and those gloves work on touchscreens."],"vocab":[{"w":"grid","pos":"noun","def":"A pattern of lines that cross to make rows and columns.","ex":"A grid of thin lines sits under the glass."},{"w":"conducts","pos":"verb","def":"Lets electricity or heat flow through it.","ex":"The human body conducts electricity."},{"w":"charge","pos":"noun","def":"An amount of electricity stored in something.","ex":"A finger pulls away some of the charge."},{"w":"constantly","pos":"adverb","def":"All the time, without stopping.","ex":"The screen checks the grid constantly."},{"w":"insulator","pos":"noun","def":"A material that blocks electricity or heat.","ex":"A wool glove is an insulator."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is the author's main point?","c":["Touchscreens break easily when people press too hard","Gloves keep hands warm by blocking electricity","Phones work best when they are kept warm","Touchscreens sense a finger through electricity"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains that most touchscreens find a finger by sensing changes in electricity."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"conducts\" mean?","word":"conducts","c":["Lets electricity flow through","Leads a group of musicians in a band","Blocks electricity","Stores a tiny charge"],"a":0,"why":"The text says a material that conducts electricity lets electricity flow through it."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens when a fingertip comes close to the glass?","c":["The glass bends a little under the finger's weight","The glove blocks the electricity","It pulls away some charge near that spot","The chip turns off the grid"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the fingertip pulls away a little of the charge near that spot."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does an ordinary glove often fail on a touchscreen?","c":["The glove presses too softly on the glass","The glove blocks electricity","The glove is too cold for the chip","The glove covers too many rows"],"a":1,"why":"A wool or cotton glove is an insulator, so the screen cannot sense the finger inside."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks a touchscreen works by feeling how hard a finger presses. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["They are so thin and clear that the eye cannot see them.","Most of these screens work by sensing electricity, not pressure.","When a fingertip comes close to the glass, it pulls away a little of the charge.","A wool or cotton glove is an insulator."],"a":1,"why":"The text says most of these screens sense electricity, not pressure."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that the human body helps a touchscreen work?","c":["The lines are so thin and clear that the eye cannot see them.","Some winter gloves have fingertips woven with special thread.","Most of these screens work by sensing electricity, not pressure.","The human body also conducts electricity."],"a":3,"why":"Because the body conducts electricity, a fingertip can change the charge on the grid."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In this text, what does \"charge\" mean?","word":"charge","c":["A tiny store of electricity","The price of something","A quick run toward something","A pattern of rows"],"a":0,"why":"The text says a charge is a tiny store of electricity."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which question does the third paragraph answer?","c":["What is a touchscreen made of besides glass?","Why do gloves keep hands warm?","How does the chip find where a finger is?","Who invented the first touchscreen?"],"a":2,"why":"The third paragraph explains how the chip finds the row and column where the charge changed."}]},{"id":"S08-vehicles-regenerative-brakes","subject":"science","level":8,"topic":"vehicles","title":"Brakes That Make Electricity","text":["Every moving car carries energy of motion, which scientists call kinetic energy. To slow down, the car has to get rid of some of that energy. Energy cannot simply vanish, so the real question is where it goes.","In most cars, the answer is heat. When a driver presses the brake pedal, pads squeeze against a metal disc that turns with the wheel. The rubbing between them, called friction, slows the wheel and changes kinetic energy into heat. That heat spreads into the air and cannot be used again.","Electric cars and hybrid cars can do something cleverer. Their electric motor uses power from a battery to turn the wheels, but the motor can also work in reverse as a generator, a machine that turns motion into electricity. When the driver slows down, the turning wheels spin the motor, which sends electricity back into the battery. Making that electricity takes effort, so the wheels slow down in the process. This is called regenerative braking, because it regenerates, or creates again, some of the energy used to speed up.","The system has limits. It cannot capture, or catch and store, all of the energy, and it does less at very low speeds or in a sudden stop. So electric cars still carry ordinary friction brakes. Even so, saving a little energy at every stop can add up, especially in city driving with many stops and starts."],"vocab":[{"w":"kinetic","pos":"adjective","def":"Having to do with motion.","ex":"A rolling ball has kinetic energy."},{"w":"friction","pos":"noun","def":"The rubbing of one thing against another, which slows motion and makes heat.","ex":"Friction between the pads and the disc slows the wheel."},{"w":"generator","pos":"noun","def":"A machine that turns motion into electricity.","ex":"The motor can work as a generator."},{"w":"regenerates","pos":"verb","def":"Creates or makes something again.","ex":"The system regenerates some of the energy."},{"w":"capture","pos":"verb","def":"To catch and keep something.","ex":"The battery can capture some of the energy."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is the author's main claim?","c":["Electric cars no longer need any friction brakes at all","Some cars can turn braking energy into electricity","Friction brakes are the newest kind of brakes","Driving in a city wastes the most fuel"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains how regenerative braking turns some braking energy back into electricity."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"friction\" mean?","word":"friction","c":["A kind of battery","Energy of motion","A machine that makes electricity","Rubbing that slows things down"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the rubbing between the pads and the disc is called friction."},{"skill":"structure","q":"In most cars, what happens to the kinetic energy when the driver brakes?","c":["It turns into heat","It goes into the battery","It turns into fuel","It speeds up the wheel"],"a":0,"why":"The text says friction changes kinetic energy into heat."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the third paragraph organized?","c":["Events are told in the order they happened over many years","Two kinds of electric cars are compared","It explains the steps of a process and why the wheels slow","A list of problems is given with no solutions"],"a":2,"why":"The paragraph walks through how the motor becomes a generator and why that slows the wheels."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks electric cars need no friction brakes. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Energy cannot simply vanish.","The system has limits.","Electric cars and hybrid cars can do something cleverer.","So electric cars still carry ordinary friction brakes."],"a":3,"why":"The text says electric cars still carry ordinary friction brakes."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the claim that regenerative braking saves energy?","c":["When a driver presses the brake pedal, pads squeeze against a metal disc.","The motor sends electricity back into the battery.","Every moving car carries energy of motion.","That heat spreads into the air and cannot be used again."],"a":1,"why":"Sending electricity back into the battery stores energy that would otherwise be lost."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"regenerates\" mean?","word":"regenerates","c":["Slows down","Wastes as heat","Creates again","Squeezes together"],"a":2,"why":"The text says it regenerates, or creates again, some of the energy."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders why regenerative braking helps most in a city. Which sentence helps most?","c":["Saving a little energy at every stop can add up.","That heat spreads into the air and cannot be used again.","Their electric motor uses power from a battery to turn the wheels.","It does less at very low speeds or in a sudden stop."],"a":0,"why":"City driving has many stops, and the text says a little energy saved at each stop adds up."}]},{"id":"S09-sports-bending-kick","subject":"science","level":9,"topic":"sports","title":"The Science of a Bending Kick","text":["In soccer, a free kick sometimes travels around a wall of defenders and then swerves, or turns sharply to one side, toward the goal. To a viewer, the ball can seem to change its mind in midair. The path is not magic, however; it comes from spin and from the way a spinning ball interacts with the air around it.","When a player strikes, or kicks, the ball off center, the ball spins as it flies. A spinning ball drags a thin layer of air along with its surface. On one side, that layer moves with the air rushing past the ball, and on the other side it moves against it. As a result, the air leaving the back of the ball is pushed toward one side. Because every push produces an equal push in return, the ball is nudged, or gently pushed, the opposite way. This sideways force is called the Magnus effect.","The effect is named for Heinrich Gustav Magnus, a German scientist who studied it in 1852, although Isaac Newton had noticed curving tennis balls nearly two centuries earlier. Topspin makes a tennis ball dip sharply, and spin lets a baseball pitcher throw a curveball.","How much a ball bends depends on several factors, or things that shape the result, such as how fast it spins, how fast it moves, and how smooth its surface is. Researchers have also found that the curve can grow tighter near the end of a long kick, as the ball slows down."],"vocab":[{"w":"swerves","pos":"verb","def":"Turns sharply to one side while moving.","ex":"The ball swerves toward the goal."},{"w":"interacts","pos":"verb","def":"Acts on something and is acted on by it in return.","ex":"A spinning ball interacts with the air around it."},{"w":"strikes","pos":"verb","def":"Hits or kicks something.","ex":"The player strikes the ball off center."},{"w":"nudged","pos":"verb","def":"Gently pushed.","ex":"The ball is nudged to one side."},{"w":"factors","pos":"noun","def":"Things that help shape a result.","ex":"Spin and speed are two factors in how a ball curves."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["Free kicks are the hardest skill for any soccer player to learn","Isaac Newton was the first person to play a sport with a curving ball","Spin causes a sideways push from the air that makes a ball curve","Smooth balls always curve more than rough balls in every sport"],"a":2,"why":"The text explains how spin and the air create the Magnus effect that bends a ball's path."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"nudged\" mean?","word":"nudged","c":["Gently pushed","Kicked very hard","Spun quickly","Slowed down"],"a":0,"why":"The text says the ball is nudged, or gently pushed, the opposite way."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, what happens because the air leaving the ball is pushed to one side?","c":["The ball begins to spin faster and faster","The ball slows down and drops at once","The surface of the ball becomes rough","The ball is pushed the opposite way"],"a":3,"why":"Every push has an equal push in return, so the ball is nudged the opposite way."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the second paragraph organized?","c":["It compares soccer with tennis","It explains a chain of causes and effects","It tells a story in time order over many years","It lists problems with no solutions"],"a":1,"why":"Each step leads to the next: spin drags air, the air is pushed aside, and the ball is pushed back."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the Magnus effect only matters in soccer. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Topspin makes a tennis ball dip sharply.","This sideways force is called the Magnus effect.","A spinning ball drags a thin layer of air along with its surface.","To a viewer, the ball can seem to change its mind in midair."],"a":0,"why":"The text shows spin also changes the path of a tennis ball and a baseball."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that spin is what makes the ball curve?","c":["The effect is named for Heinrich Gustav Magnus.","To a viewer, the ball can seem to change its mind in midair.","A spinning ball drags a thin layer of air along with its surface.","In soccer, a free kick sometimes travels around a wall of defenders."],"a":2,"why":"The air dragged by the spinning surface starts the chain that pushes the ball sideways."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"factors\" mean?","word":"factors","c":["Numbers that are multiplied","Things that shape a result","Players on a team","Parts of a soccer ball"],"a":1,"why":"The text says factors are things that shape the result, such as spin and speed."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which inference is best supported by the text?","c":["A ball kicked with no spin at all would curve the most","Newton and Magnus worked together to explain soccer kicks","The Magnus effect only works on balls in very cold, thin air","A ball struck right through its center would curve less"],"a":3,"why":"The text links an off center kick to spin, and spin to the curve, so a center kick would curve less."}]},{"id":"S10-nature-lichen-partners","subject":"science","level":10,"topic":"nature","title":"Partners on a Bare Rock","text":["On bare rocks, tree bark and old wooden fences, a careful observer can often find flat, crusty patches of gray, green, orange or yellow. These patches are lichens. Although a lichen may look like one plant, it is not a plant at all. It is a partnership between very different living things.","The main partner is a fungus, which forms the lichen's body and shelters it from drying out. Inside that body live tiny algae or certain bacteria that can photosynthesize, meaning they use sunlight to make food from air and water. The fungus cannot make its own food, so it takes a share of the sugars its partners produce. In return, they get a protected home. A close relationship like this, in which both partners benefit, is called symbiosis.","For more than a century, scientists described lichens as a pair. In 2016, however, researchers reported that many lichens also contain a yeast, a single-celled fungus, living in their outer layer. Scientists are still studying what the yeast does, and the discovery suggests that some lichens may be more complex partnerships than anyone had assumed.","This teamwork allows lichens to survive where few other living things can, from hot deserts to frozen mountaintops. Many grow only a few millimeters each year, and some patches are probably hundreds of years old. Because many lichens absorb, or soak up, water and nutrients straight from the air, they are sensitive to air pollution, which means it harms them easily. Scientists often survey lichens, carefully counting and studying them, to estimate how clean the air is in a forest or a city."],"vocab":[{"w":"photosynthesize","pos":"verb","def":"To use sunlight to make food from air and water.","ex":"Algae photosynthesize to make sugar."},{"w":"symbiosis","pos":"noun","def":"A close relationship between two living things that helps both of them.","ex":"A lichen is an example of symbiosis."},{"w":"absorb","pos":"verb","def":"To soak up or take in.","ex":"Lichens absorb water from the air."},{"w":"sensitive","pos":"adjective","def":"Easily harmed or changed by something.","ex":"Lichens are sensitive to air pollution."},{"w":"survey","pos":"verb","def":"To carefully count and study something.","ex":"Scientists survey lichens in a forest."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["Lichens are small, slow plants that grow mostly on old wooden fences","Lichens harm rocks and trees, so scientists try to remove them","Yeast is the most important partner in every kind of lichen","A lichen is a partnership of living things with surprising abilities"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains the lichen partnership, where it can survive and what it reveals about air."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"symbiosis\" mean?","word":"symbiosis","c":["A kind of yeast that lives in the outer layer","The way lichens break rock down into soil","A close bond that helps both partners","The process of making food from sunlight"],"a":2,"why":"The text says symbiosis is a close relationship in which both partners benefit."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does the fungus take some of the sugars its partners make?","c":["It needs the sugars to break down rock","It cannot make its own food","It wants to keep the algae from growing","It gives the sugars to the yeast"],"a":1,"why":"The text says the fungus cannot make its own food, so it takes a share of the sugars."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, why are lichens sensitive to air pollution?","c":["They take in water and nutrients from the air","They grow by only a few millimeters every single year","They live mostly in hot deserts","They contain a yeast in their outer layer"],"a":0,"why":"The text links soaking up water and nutrients straight from the air to being sensitive to pollution."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader assumes that a lichen is a kind of plant. Which sentence corrects this idea?","c":["These patches are lichens.","Although a lichen may look like one plant, it is not a plant at all.","Scientists often survey lichens to estimate how clean the air is in a forest or a city.","The main partner is a fungus."],"a":1,"why":"The text states plainly that a lichen is not a plant at all."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that scientists are still learning about lichens?","c":["The main partner is a fungus, which forms the lichen's body.","Many grow only a few millimeters each year.","These patches are lichens.","Scientists are still studying what the yeast does."],"a":3,"why":"The yeast was reported in 2016, and its role is still being studied."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"survey\" mean?","word":"survey","c":["Carefully count and study","Ask many people a set of questions","Clean and protect","Grow and spread"],"a":0,"why":"The text says to survey lichens is to carefully count and study them."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which inference is best supported by the text?","c":["Lichens grow fast enough to cover a city in only a few years","The yeast in lichens is the partner that makes all of the food","A place with very few lichens may have dirtier air","Lichens cannot live anywhere that is colder than a city"],"a":2,"why":"Lichens are easily harmed by air pollution, so few lichens can be a sign of dirtier air."}]},{"id":"H01-people-community-helpers","subject":"social","level":1,"topic":"people","title":"Helpers All Over Town","text":["A town needs many helpers. Each helper has a job. The jobs keep the town safe and clean.","A crossing guard stands by the street. The guard holds up a stop sign. Then the cars stop, and children cross. A mail carrier brings letters to each home. A librarian helps people find good books.","Some helpers work at night. Nurses care for sick people. Workers fix the lights on the street. While you sleep, helpers are still at work."],"vocab":[{"w":"guard","pos":"noun","def":"A person who watches over others to keep them safe.","ex":"The guard holds up a stop sign."},{"w":"carrier","pos":"noun","def":"A person who carries things and brings them to people.","ex":"A mail carrier brings letters to each home."},{"w":"librarian","pos":"noun","def":"A person who works in a library and helps people find books.","ex":"A librarian helps people find good books."},{"w":"nurses","pos":"noun","def":"People whose job is to care for sick people.","ex":"Nurses care for sick people."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to read a stop sign","People who help a town","Where letters come from","Why streets have lights"],"a":1,"why":"The text tells about many helpers and the jobs they do in a town."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does a \"librarian\" do?","word":"librarian","c":["Helps people find good books","Brings letters to each home","Fixes the lights on the street","Cares for sick people at night"],"a":0,"why":"The text says a librarian helps people find good books."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens right after the guard holds up a stop sign?","c":["The children go home","The mail comes","The cars stop","The lights go out"],"a":2,"why":"The text says that then the cars stop, and children cross."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why does a town need helpers?","c":["To sell books to people","To drive all the cars","To make the streets busy","To keep the town safe"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the jobs keep the town safe and clean."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks all helpers stop work at night. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["A town needs many helpers.","Some helpers work at night.","Each helper has a job.","A librarian helps people find good books."],"a":1,"why":"The text says some helpers work at night."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["Our Town's Helpers","A Day at the Sunny Beach","How to Mail a Letter","The Biggest Hospital"],"a":0,"why":"The whole text is about the helpers in a town."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does a mail \"carrier\" do?","word":"carrier","c":["Reads books to little children","Stops the cars","Fixes the lights","Brings letters to homes"],"a":3,"why":"The text says a mail carrier brings letters to each home."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which sentence tells what nurses do?","c":["Workers fix the lights on the street.","A mail carrier brings letters to each home.","Nurses care for sick people.","A town needs many helpers."],"a":2,"why":"This sentence tells that nurses care for sick people."}]},{"id":"H02-food-farm-to-table","subject":"social","level":2,"topic":"food","title":"An Apple's Long Trip","text":["An apple starts on a tree at a farm. In the fall, the apples are ripe. That means they are ready to eat. Farm workers pick them by hand. This picking time is called the harvest.","Next, the apples go to a packing house. There, workers wash the apples. Then machines sort them into groups by size. The apples are packed in boxes.","Then a truck carries the boxes to a store. Many trucks are cold inside. The cold keeps the apples fresh and crisp. At last, a family buys the apples to eat."],"vocab":[{"w":"ripe","pos":"adjective","def":"Fully grown and ready to eat.","ex":"In the fall, the apples are ripe."},{"w":"harvest","pos":"noun","def":"The time when crops are picked or gathered.","ex":"This picking time is called the harvest."},{"w":"sort","pos":"verb","def":"To put things into groups.","ex":"Machines sort the apples into groups by size."},{"w":"fresh","pos":"adjective","def":"New and still good to eat, not old or spoiled.","ex":"The cold keeps the apples fresh and crisp."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to bake an apple pie at home","Why apple trees grow so tall","How apples get to the store","Where the big trucks go at night"],"a":2,"why":"The text follows apples from the tree to the packing house to the store."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is the \"harvest\"?","word":"harvest","c":["A kind of truck","The time when apples are picked","A box for apples","A big machine that washes the fruit"],"a":1,"why":"The text says the picking time is called the harvest."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What happens right after the apples are washed?","c":["Machines sort them by size","A family buys them at the store","Workers pick them from the tree","They go back to the farm"],"a":0,"why":"The text says workers wash the apples, then machines sort them."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why are many trucks cold inside?","c":["To make the apples grow","To help the truck go fast","To wash the apples","To keep the apples fresh"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the cold keeps the apples fresh and crisp."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the apples go straight from the tree to the store. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["An apple starts on a tree at a farm.","Farm workers pick them by hand.","At last, a family buys the apples to eat.","Next, the apples go to a packing house."],"a":3,"why":"The apples stop at a packing house before they go to the store."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which title fits this text best?","c":["A Day at the Beach","How Big Trucks Are Built","From Tree to Store","The Tallest Apple Tree"],"a":2,"why":"The text tells how apples travel from a tree to a store."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"sort\" mean?","word":"sort","c":["Put into groups","Cut into pieces","Wash with water","Pick from a tree"],"a":0,"why":"The text says machines sort the apples into groups by size."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which sentence tells when the apples are ready to eat?","c":["Then a truck carries the boxes to a store.","In the fall, the apples are ripe.","The apples are packed in boxes.","There, workers wash the apples."],"a":1,"why":"Ripe means ready to eat, and the text says this happens in the fall."}]},{"id":"H03-vehicles-travel-long-ago","subject":"social","level":3,"topic":"vehicles","title":"Getting Around Long Ago","text":["Long ago, there were no cars, trains or planes. Most people walked wherever they needed to go. A trip to the next town could take a whole day.","People found ways to travel faster. Near rivers and lakes, they carved boats from big logs. They dug out the middle of a log to make a dugout canoe. They pushed the canoe with paddles, short poles with a flat end.","About 5,500 years ago, people in Mesopotamia were among the first to use the wheel. Mesopotamia was a land in what is now Iraq. Wheels made it easier to move heavy loads in carts. People also trained animals, such as oxen and horses, to pull carts. Horses could go farther in a day than people on foot."],"vocab":[{"w":"carved","pos":"verb","def":"Cut wood or stone into a shape.","ex":"They carved boats from big logs."},{"w":"canoe","pos":"noun","def":"A narrow boat that people move with paddles.","ex":"They dug out the middle of a log to make a dugout canoe."},{"w":"paddles","pos":"noun","def":"Short poles with a flat end, used to push a boat through water.","ex":"They pushed the canoe with paddles."},{"w":"loads","pos":"noun","def":"Things that are carried, often heavy ones.","ex":"Wheels made it easier to move heavy loads in carts."},{"w":"trained","pos":"verb","def":"Taught a person or an animal how to do something.","ex":"People trained animals to pull carts."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["Ways people traveled long ago","How workers build a modern car today","Why rivers always flow to the sea","How horses find food"],"a":0,"why":"The text tells about walking, canoes, wheels and animals that helped people travel long ago."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"canoe\"?","word":"canoe","c":["A cart with two wheels","A kind of strong horse","A road made of logs","A boat made from a log"],"a":3,"why":"The text says people dug out the middle of a log to make a dugout canoe."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why did people use wheels?","c":["To dig out the middle of logs","To move heavy loads more easily","To cross deep lakes and rivers quickly","To make flat paddles for boats"],"a":1,"why":"The text says wheels made it easier to move heavy loads in carts."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How did people make a dugout canoe?","c":["They tied many long sticks together","They bent a sheet of metal","They dug out the middle of a log","They wove grass into a mat"],"a":2,"why":"The text says they dug out the middle of a log."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks people long ago could only walk. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["Most people walked wherever they needed to go.","A trip to the next town could take a whole day.","People found ways to travel faster.","Mesopotamia was a land in what is now Iraq."],"a":2,"why":"The text says people found ways to travel faster, such as canoes, carts and horses."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that people found faster ways to travel?","c":["There were no cars, trains or planes long ago","Horses could go farther in a day","Mesopotamia was in what is now Iraq","A trip to the next town could take a day"],"a":1,"why":"Horses going farther than people on foot shows a faster way to travel."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"carved\" mean?","word":"carved","c":["Painted with bright colors","Tied together with rope","Carried across a lake","Cut into a shape"],"a":3,"why":"People carved logs into boats, so carved means cut into a shape."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure where Mesopotamia was. Which sentence helps?","c":["Mesopotamia was a land in what is now Iraq.","Most people walked wherever they needed to go.","People found ways to travel faster.","Wheels made it easier to move heavy loads in carts."],"a":0,"why":"This sentence says Mesopotamia was in what is now Iraq."}]},{"id":"H04-money-trading-goods","subject":"social","level":4,"topic":"money","title":"Trading Before Coins","text":["Long before coins, people traded goods with each other. A farmer with extra grain might swap it for a clay pot. This kind of trade is called barter. No money changes hands in a barter.","Barter had a problem. The farmer had to find a potter who wanted grain at that moment. If the potter did not need grain, there was no trade. Also, it was hard to decide how much grain one pot was worth.","To solve this, many people began to use objects that almost everyone accepted, or agreed to take in a trade. In parts of Africa and Asia, small shiny shells called cowries were used this way. In other places, people used salt or cattle. These objects worked as an early kind of money.","Coins came much later. The first known coins were made about 2,600 years ago in Lydia, a kingdom in what is now Turkey."],"vocab":[{"w":"barter","pos":"noun","def":"Trading goods for other goods without using money.","ex":"This kind of trade is called barter."},{"w":"swap","pos":"verb","def":"To give one thing and get another thing back.","ex":"A farmer might swap grain for a clay pot."},{"w":"worth","pos":"adjective","def":"Equal in value to something.","ex":"It was hard to decide how much grain one pot was worth."},{"w":"accepted","pos":"verb","def":"Agreed to take something.","ex":"People used objects that almost everyone accepted."},{"w":"cowries","pos":"noun","def":"Small shiny shells that were once used as money.","ex":"Small shiny shells called cowries were used as money."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How potters make clay pots by hand","Why people put salt on their food","How farmers grow grain in big fields","How people traded before coins"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains barter, its problem, and the objects people used before coins."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is \"barter\"?","word":"barter","c":["Paying for goods with metal coins","Making shells into jewelry","Trading goods without money","Counting cattle on a farm"],"a":2,"why":"The text says no money changes hands in a barter."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why was barter sometimes hard?","c":["Each person had to want what the other had","Clay pots often broke on the long trip home","Grain was much too heavy for people to carry","Shells were far too small for people to see"],"a":0,"why":"The text says if the potter did not need grain, there was no trade."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the second paragraph organized?","c":["It tells events in the order they happened","It explains a problem with barter","It compares two kinds of coins","It lists ways to grow grain"],"a":1,"why":"The paragraph starts with \"Barter had a problem\" and then explains it."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks shells were used as money everywhere. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["In other places, people used salt or cattle.","Barter had a problem.","Also, it was hard to decide how much grain one pot was worth.","The farmer had to find a potter who wanted grain at that moment."],"a":0,"why":"The text says other places used salt or cattle instead of shells."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that people used something like money before coins?","c":["Barter had a problem.","A farmer with extra grain might swap it for a clay pot.","No money changes hands in a barter.","These objects worked as an early kind of money."],"a":3,"why":"Shells, salt and cattle worked as an early kind of money before coins."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what are \"cowries\"?","word":"cowries","c":["Large clay pots for grain","Small shiny shells","Heavy bags of salt","Old coins made of metal"],"a":1,"why":"The text calls cowries small shiny shells."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which question does the last paragraph answer?","c":["What kinds of pots did potters in Lydia make?","Why did people in other places use salt and cattle?","Where were the first known coins made?","How do small shiny shells form in the sea?"],"a":2,"why":"The last paragraph says the first known coins were made in Lydia."}]},{"id":"H05-art-cave-paintings","subject":"social","level":5,"topic":"art","title":"Paintings Deep Underground","text":["In 1940, four teenagers in southern France explored a hole in the ground near the village of Montignac. Inside, they found a cave whose walls were covered with paintings of horses, deer and huge wild cattle. The cave is called Lascaux, and its art is about 17,000 years old.","The artists made paint from natural materials. They used minerals, which are natural materials found in rocks and soil. Minerals such as red and yellow ochre were ground into powder. Black came from dark minerals or from charcoal, the dark remains of burned wood. Scientists think the artists worked by the light of small lamps. These lamps burned animal fat.","After the cave opened to visitors, a problem appeared. The breath of so many people changed the air inside, and green algae began to grow on the walls. In 1963, the cave was closed to the public to protect the art. Today, visitors can tour a careful copy of the cave built nearby instead."],"vocab":[{"w":"explored","pos":"verb","def":"Looked around a place to learn about it.","ex":"Four teenagers explored a hole in the ground."},{"w":"minerals","pos":"noun","def":"Natural materials found in rocks and soil.","ex":"The artists used minerals to make paint."},{"w":"ochre","pos":"noun","def":"A red or yellow mineral that can be ground into paint.","ex":"Red and yellow ochre were ground into powder."},{"w":"charcoal","pos":"noun","def":"The dark remains of burned wood.","ex":"Black came from dark minerals or from charcoal."},{"w":"protect","pos":"verb","def":"To keep something safe from harm.","ex":"The cave was closed to protect the art."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How lamps were made from animal fat","A very old painted cave in France","Why wild cattle lived in southern France","How teenagers learn to paint today"],"a":1,"why":"The text tells how the Lascaux cave was found, how its art was made, and how it is kept safe."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is \"charcoal\"?","word":"charcoal","c":["A kind of red mineral","A small stone lamp that burns animal fat","A powder made from shells","The dark remains of burned wood"],"a":3,"why":"The text says charcoal is the dark remains of burned wood."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why was the cave closed to the public in 1963?","c":["The paintings were too dark to see","The teenagers wanted to keep it secret","Visitors' breath was harming the art","The village needed the cave for storage"],"a":2,"why":"The text says the breath of many visitors changed the air and algae grew on the walls."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the last paragraph organized?","c":["A problem, then a solution","A comparison of two caves","A list of steps for making paint","A description of one painting"],"a":0,"why":"It describes harm to the cave, then tells how people protected the art."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the artists bought their paint. Which sentence shows this is wrong?","c":["The cave is called Lascaux, and its art is about 17,000 years old.","In 1963, the cave was closed to the public to protect the art.","The artists made paint from natural materials.","Today, visitors can tour a careful copy of the cave built nearby instead."],"a":2,"why":"The text says the artists made their own paint from natural materials."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that the art is very old?","c":["Four teenagers explored a hole in the ground.","The art is about 17,000 years old.","Black came from charcoal.","Visitors can tour a careful copy."],"a":1,"why":"An age of about 17,000 years shows the art is very old."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the first paragraph, what does \"explored\" mean?","word":"explored","c":["Looked around to learn about it","Painted pictures on a stone wall","Closed a place to keep it safe","Ground rocks into a fine powder"],"a":0,"why":"The teenagers went into the hole and found the cave, so they looked around to learn about it."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader wonders how the artists could see inside a dark cave. Which sentence helps?","c":["Black came from dark minerals or from charcoal, the dark remains of burned wood.","The breath of so many people changed the air inside, and green algae began to grow on the walls.","The artists made paint from natural materials.","Scientists think the artists worked by the light of small lamps."],"a":3,"why":"The text says the artists likely worked by the light of small lamps."}]},{"id":"H06-music-instruments-places","subject":"social","level":6,"topic":"music","title":"Instruments Shaped by Place","text":["Musical instruments are often built from materials that people can find close to home. Looking at three instruments from different places shows how this works.","In northern Australia, Aboriginal musicians play the didgeridoo, a long wooden tube. Traditionally, it is made from a eucalyptus branch that termites have hollowed out, leaving it empty inside. Players use a skill called circular breathing. They breathe in through the nose while pushing stored air out through the mouth, so the low, buzzing sound never stops.","In West Africa, the kora is a kind of harp with 21 strings. Its body is a large gourd, a hard dried fruit, cut in half and covered with cowhide. Musicians called griots have played the kora for centuries while singing the histories of families and places.","The steelpan comes from Trinidad and Tobago, an island nation in the Caribbean. In the 1930s and 1940s, musicians there began hammering the ends of empty steel oil drums into new shapes. Each shaped area plays a different note when it is struck with a stick."],"vocab":[{"w":"hollowed","pos":"verb","def":"Made empty on the inside.","ex":"Termites have hollowed out the branch."},{"w":"gourd","pos":"noun","def":"A large fruit with a hard shell that can be dried and used as a container.","ex":"The body of the kora is a large gourd."},{"w":"cowhide","pos":"noun","def":"The skin of a cow, used like leather.","ex":"The gourd is covered with cowhide."},{"w":"griots","pos":"noun","def":"West African musicians and storytellers who keep the histories of families and places.","ex":"Griots sing the histories of families and places."},{"w":"struck","pos":"verb","def":"Hit with something.","ex":"Each area plays a note when it is struck with a stick."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is this text mostly about?","c":["How to build a steel drum at home","Why termites eat the inside of eucalyptus trees","Instruments shaped by the places they come from","Which musical instrument is the hardest one to learn"],"a":2,"why":"The text shows how three instruments are made from materials found where they come from."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"gourd\"?","word":"gourd","c":["A hard dried fruit","A kind of drum stick","A thin metal string","A long wooden tube"],"a":0,"why":"The text says a gourd is a hard dried fruit."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the text organized?","c":["It tells one musician's life in time order","It gives steps for making an instrument","It argues that one instrument is best","It looks at three instruments, one by one"],"a":3,"why":"After the first paragraph, each paragraph describes a different instrument and place."},{"skill":"structure","q":"Why can the didgeridoo's sound keep going without a break?","c":["The tube is very long","Players use circular breathing","Termites hollow out the branch","The wood is from a eucalyptus tree"],"a":1,"why":"Circular breathing lets players breathe in while still pushing air out, so the sound never stops."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the steelpan is thousands of years old. Which detail shows this is wrong?","c":["It comes from an island nation in the Caribbean","It was first made in the 1930s and 1940s","It is made from steel oil drums","Each area plays a different note"],"a":1,"why":"The text says musicians began making steelpans in the 1930s and 1940s."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that instruments are built from materials found nearby?","c":["Griots have sung the histories of families and places.","The kora is a kind of harp with 21 strings.","Didgeridoos come from branches hollowed by termites.","Steelpan players strike shaped areas to make notes."],"a":2,"why":"A branch from a local tree, hollowed by local termites, is a material found close to home."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what does \"hollowed out\" mean?","word":"hollowed","c":["Painted on the outside","Cut into small pieces","Made heavier and stronger","Made empty on the inside"],"a":3,"why":"The text says the termites leave the branch empty inside."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is not sure what griots do. Which answer matches the text?","c":["They sing histories and play the kora","They hammer old steel drums into shapes","They hollow out eucalyptus branches","They cover gourds with metal sheets"],"a":0,"why":"The text says griots play the kora while singing the histories of families and places."}]},{"id":"H07-people-reading-maps","subject":"social","level":7,"topic":"people","title":"How Maps Are Made and Read","text":["A map is a drawing of a place as it would look from above, shrunk down to fit on a page or a screen. To read one well, a person needs to understand a few tools that mapmakers include.","The first tool is the scale, which compares distance on the map to distance on the ground. On a town map, for example, one inch might stand for one mile. The second tool is the legend, sometimes called a key, which explains what each symbol means. A blue line might show a river, while a green patch might show a park. A third tool, the compass rose, shows directions, and on most maps north points toward the top.","For centuries, surveyors, the people who measure land, walked across fields with chains of a set length to record distances. Today, much of that work is done with photographs taken from airplanes and satellites, which can show huge areas in great detail.","Even modern maps face a problem that cannot be fully solved. Earth is round, so any flat map must stretch or squeeze some places. On one common world map, Greenland looks about as large as Africa, yet Africa is roughly 14 times bigger. Careful readers remember that every map makes choices about what to show and how."],"vocab":[{"w":"scale","pos":"noun","def":"The part of a map that shows how map distance compares to real distance.","ex":"One inch on the scale might stand for one mile."},{"w":"legend","pos":"noun","def":"A list on a map that explains what each symbol means.","ex":"The legend shows that a blue line is a river."},{"w":"symbol","pos":"noun","def":"A mark or picture that stands for something else.","ex":"A green patch is a symbol for a park."},{"w":"surveyors","pos":"noun","def":"People whose job is to measure land.","ex":"Surveyors walked across fields with chains."},{"w":"squeeze","pos":"verb","def":"To press something so it takes up less space.","ex":"A flat map must stretch or squeeze some places."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"What is the author's main point?","c":["Maps made long ago were always wrong about every distance","Satellites have now replaced every human mapmaker","Greenland is much larger than most people believe","Reading a map well means knowing its tools and limits"],"a":3,"why":"The text explains the tools on a map and warns that every flat map has limits."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In this text, what does \"legend\" mean?","word":"legend","c":["An old story about a hero from the past","A list that explains a map's symbols","A line that shows north","A photo taken from above"],"a":1,"why":"The text says the legend explains what each symbol on the map means."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, why must a flat map stretch or squeeze some places?","c":["Because Earth is round","Because satellites take blurry photos","Because mapmakers use chains","Because north is at the top"],"a":0,"why":"The text says Earth is round, so any flat map must stretch or squeeze some places."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is the second paragraph organized?","c":["It tells the history of one map in time order","It compares two countries by size","It explains map tools one at a time","It explains a problem and how to solve it"],"a":2,"why":"The paragraph describes the first, second and third tools in turn."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks the sizes of places on every world map are exact. Which detail shows this is wrong?","c":["Greenland looks as big as Africa on one map","One inch might stand for one mile on a town map","Surveyors once walked the land with chains","North points toward the top on most maps"],"a":0,"why":"Africa is about 14 times bigger than Greenland, yet one map shows them about the same size."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that mapmaking has changed over time?","c":["A legend is sometimes also called a key.","One inch might stand for one mile on a map.","Earth is round, so flat maps must stretch.","Photos from the air replaced chains."],"a":3,"why":"Surveyors once used chains, and now photos from airplanes and satellites do much of the work."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, who are \"surveyors\"?","word":"surveyors","c":["People who draw symbols for parks","People who fly airplanes","People who measure land","People who print world maps"],"a":2,"why":"The text calls surveyors the people who measure land."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader is confused about what a map scale does. Which part of the text should they reread?","c":["The last sentence of the text","The first sentence of paragraph two","The first sentence of paragraph three","The second sentence of the text"],"a":1,"why":"The first sentence of paragraph two explains that the scale compares map distance to ground distance."}]},{"id":"H08-sports-olympic-history","subject":"social","level":8,"topic":"sports","title":"From Olympia to the Whole World","text":["The Olympic Games began in ancient Greece, at a place called Olympia. According to Greek records, the first Games were held in 776 BCE as part of a religious festival. For many years, the only event was a footrace called the stadion, which covered about 192 meters.","The Games took place every four years, and the Greeks even used this four-year period, called an Olympiad, as a way to count time. Winners did not receive medals. Instead, each champion was crowned with a wreath, a ring of olive leaves, which was a simple prize that carried enormous honor.","The ancient Games continued for more than a thousand years before they ended. Then, in the late 1800s, a French educator named Pierre de Coubertin argued that sports could bring young people from different nations together. He gave speeches and gathered support, and his efforts led to the first modern Olympics, held in Athens in 1896.","Since then, the Games have grown and changed. Women first competed in 1900, the Winter Games began in 1924, and the Paralympic Games, for athletes with disabilities, started in 1960. Today, athletes from more than 200 nations and teams take part. Even so, before each Games, a flame is still lit at Olympia, a link to the place where it all began."],"vocab":[{"w":"Olympiad","pos":"noun","def":"The period of four years between one Olympic Games and the next.","ex":"The Greeks used the Olympiad to count time."},{"w":"wreath","pos":"noun","def":"A ring of leaves or flowers, sometimes worn on the head.","ex":"Each champion was crowned with a wreath of olive leaves."},{"w":"enormous","pos":"adjective","def":"Very large or very great.","ex":"The simple prize carried enormous honor."},{"w":"argued","pos":"verb","def":"Gave reasons to try to persuade others.","ex":"Coubertin argued that sports could bring nations together."},{"w":"efforts","pos":"noun","def":"Hard work done to reach a goal.","ex":"His efforts led to the first modern Olympics."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["The Games began long ago and have grown over time","Ancient champions earned more honor than athletes today","Running is the most important sport at every Olympics","Pierre de Coubertin was the fastest runner of his day"],"a":0,"why":"The text moves from the first ancient Games to the much larger Games of today."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In paragraph three, what does \"argued\" mean?","word":"argued","c":["Shouted angrily at someone","Lost a footrace by a very small amount","Gave reasons to persuade others","Wrote down a list of rules"],"a":2,"why":"Coubertin gave speeches and gathered support, so he gave reasons to persuade people."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How is most of the text organized?","c":["By comparing two sports","In time order, from ancient times to today","As a problem followed by a solution","As a list of rules that every athlete must follow"],"a":1,"why":"The paragraphs move from 776 BCE to 1896 and then to today."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, what led to the first modern Olympics?","c":["The discovery of an old stadion track","A new law passed in Athens","A wreath of olive leaves found buried at Olympia","Coubertin's efforts to bring nations together"],"a":3,"why":"The text says Coubertin's efforts led to the first modern Olympics in 1896."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader thinks women have always competed in the Olympics. Which detail shows this is wrong?","c":["The first Games were in 776 BCE","The stadion was about 192 meters","The Winter Games began in 1924","Women first competed in 1900"],"a":3,"why":"The text says women first competed in 1900, long after the Games began."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that the ancient Games were very important to the Greeks?","c":["They used Olympiads to count time","The stadion covered about 192 meters","Winners did not receive medals","Athens hosted the Games in 1896"],"a":0,"why":"Using the time between Games to count years shows how central the Games were."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the text, what is a \"wreath\"?","word":"wreath","c":["A shiny gold medal hung on a ribbon","A ring of leaves worn as a crown","A footrace of about 192 meters","A flame lit at Olympia"],"a":1,"why":"The text says a wreath is a ring of olive leaves used to crown a champion."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which inference is best supported by the text?","c":["The modern Games copy every ancient rule exactly","Only athletes from Greece may compete in the Games today","Organizers still honor the Games' ancient past","Medals were given out at the first ancient Games"],"a":2,"why":"A flame is still lit at Olympia before each Games, which links them to their beginning."}]},{"id":"H09-stories-spoken-tradition","subject":"social","level":9,"topic":"stories","title":"Stories Carried by Voice","text":["Long before most people could read, communities kept their knowledge alive by speaking it. Histories, family records, songs and lessons were passed from older people to younger ones, generation after generation. This practice, called oral tradition, still thrives in many parts of the world, where it remains strong and widely used.","Remembering a long story without writing it down might seem nearly impossible, but storytellers developed clever methods. Many stories use rhythm, repeated lines and familiar phrases, which make the words easier to recall, or bring back to mind. Some stories are sung or chanted, since a melody can act like a thread that holds the words in order.","In West Africa, storytellers and musicians called griots have kept the histories of families and kingdoms for centuries. Griots have told the story of Sundiata, a founder of the Mali Empire, for hundreds of years. In Australia, some researchers believe that certain Aboriginal stories about coastlines covered by rising seas preserve memories of real events. If so, those memories could be thousands of years old.","Oral stories are rarely fixed in the way a printed book is. A skilled teller may adjust details to suit a particular audience while keeping the heart of the story the same. For this reason, many scholars treat oral tradition not as a weaker kind of writing but as a flexible way of preserving what a community values."],"vocab":[{"w":"thrives","pos":"verb","def":"Grows strong and does well.","ex":"Oral tradition still thrives in many parts of the world."},{"w":"recall","pos":"verb","def":"To bring something back to mind; to remember.","ex":"Repeated lines make the words easier to recall."},{"w":"preserve","pos":"verb","def":"To keep something safe so it lasts.","ex":"Some stories may preserve memories of real events."},{"w":"adjust","pos":"verb","def":"To change something a little so it fits better.","ex":"A skilled teller may adjust details to suit an audience."},{"w":"flexible","pos":"adjective","def":"Able to change when needed.","ex":"Oral tradition is a flexible way of preserving what a community values."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["Written stories are always more accurate than spoken ones","Spoken stories have carried knowledge for a very long time","Griots are the only storytellers who still use songs","Aboriginal stories prove exactly when Australia's coasts flooded"],"a":1,"why":"The text explains how communities have kept knowledge alive by speaking it, then and now."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In paragraph two, what does \"recall\" mean?","word":"recall","c":["To bring back into memory","To write down on paper","To sing in a loud voice","To change a little at a time"],"a":0,"why":"The text explains recall as bringing words back to mind."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to paragraph two, why are some stories sung or chanted?","c":["Singing makes a story shorter","Audiences in every place prefer music to plain words","Chanting was required by law","A melody helps hold the words in order"],"a":3,"why":"The text says a melody can act like a thread that holds the words in order."},{"skill":"structure","q":"What is the main purpose of paragraph three?","c":["To explain how melodies are written down on paper","To argue that writing is a weaker form of storytelling","To give examples from two parts of the world","To list rules for telling a story well"],"a":2,"why":"The paragraph gives examples of oral tradition from West Africa and Australia."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader concludes that researchers have proven the Aboriginal stories describe real floods. Which phrase shows the reader has gone too far?","c":["some researchers believe","for centuries","the heart of the story","generation after generation"],"a":0,"why":"\"Some researchers believe\" shows this is an idea some experts hold, not a proven fact."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that oral tradition can last a long time?","c":["Some stories are sung or chanted.","A skilled teller may adjust details for an audience.","Many stories use repeated lines.","Griots have told Sundiata's story for centuries."],"a":3,"why":"A story told for hundreds of years shows that spoken stories can last a long time."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the last paragraph, what does \"flexible\" mean?","word":"flexible","c":["Weak and very easy to break apart","Old and mostly forgotten","Able to change when needed","Fixed in one exact form"],"a":2,"why":"Tellers can adjust details while keeping the heart of the story, so the tradition can change when needed."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which question would a reader need another source to answer?","c":["What is oral tradition?","How many griots work today?","Why do some stories use repeated lines?","What did Sundiata help to found?"],"a":1,"why":"The text never says how many griots work today, but it answers the other questions."}]},{"id":"H10-tech-libraries-and-mail","subject":"social","level":10,"topic":"tech","title":"Carrying Words Across Distance","text":["Information is only useful when it can reach the people who need it. For most of history, that was a serious challenge, because words could travel no faster than the person or animal carrying them. Two institutions, the library and the postal service, gradually changed how widely knowledge could spread.","Libraries were first designed to gather information in one place. The famous Library of Alexandria in Egypt, founded more than 2,000 years ago, tried to collect copies of every important text it could find. Ancient writers claimed it held hundreds of thousands of scrolls, although modern historians treat those numbers with caution. For centuries afterward, most large collections belonged to rulers, religious communities or universities, so ordinary readers rarely used them.","That began to shift as libraries opened their doors to more people. In 1731, Benjamin Franklin and a group of friends in Philadelphia pooled their money, combining it to buy books they could all borrow. By the late 1800s, many towns in Britain and the United States had founded free public libraries paid for by taxes.","Postal services, meanwhile, moved information outward. The ancient Persian Empire kept relay stations along its roads, where fresh riders and horses waited to carry royal messages onward. Much later, in 1840, Britain introduced the first adhesive postage stamp, and the price of a letter no longer depended on how far it traveled. Because sending mail became cheap and predictable, letters, newspapers and ideas circulated far more widely than before."],"vocab":[{"w":"institutions","pos":"noun","def":"Lasting organizations or systems that serve a society.","ex":"The library and the postal service are two institutions."},{"w":"caution","pos":"noun","def":"Care taken because something might not be true or safe.","ex":"Historians treat those numbers with caution."},{"w":"pooled","pos":"verb","def":"Put together so a group could share it.","ex":"Franklin and his friends pooled their money to buy books."},{"w":"relay","pos":"noun","def":"A system in which fresh people or animals take over to carry something onward.","ex":"Fresh riders waited at relay stations along the roads."},{"w":"predictable","pos":"adjective","def":"Able to be known ahead of time.","ex":"Sending mail became cheap and predictable."},{"w":"circulated","pos":"verb","def":"Moved from person to person or place to place.","ex":"Letters and newspapers circulated far more widely."}],"questions":[{"skill":"central","q":"Which statement best expresses the central idea?","c":["The Library of Alexandria held more scrolls than any library since","Benjamin Franklin invented both the library and the post office","Libraries and the mail slowly helped knowledge reach more people","Postage stamps were the most important invention in history"],"a":2,"why":"The text shows how libraries and postal services gradually spread knowledge more widely."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In the first paragraph, what does \"institutions\" mean?","word":"institutions","c":["Single books kept inside a family home","Laws that are passed by a ruler","Roads used by fast riders","Lasting systems that serve a society"],"a":3,"why":"The text names the library and the postal service as institutions, and both are lasting systems that serve people."},{"skill":"structure","q":"According to the text, what changed in Britain in 1840?","c":["A letter's price no longer depended on distance","Libraries began charging every reader to borrow books","Riders and horses carried all the mail","Newspapers stopped being printed"],"a":0,"why":"The text says that after the first adhesive stamp, a letter's price no longer depended on how far it traveled."},{"skill":"structure","q":"How does the author organize paragraphs two through four?","c":["By arguing for one side and then the other side","By following libraries first, then the mail","By listing inventions from the newest to the oldest","By describing one city across a single year"],"a":1,"why":"Paragraphs two and three describe libraries, and paragraph four turns to postal services."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"A reader assumes the Library of Alexandria definitely held hundreds of thousands of scrolls. Which phrase should make the reader more careful?","c":["the famous Library of Alexandria","treat those numbers with caution","copies of every important text","founded more than 2,000 years ago"],"a":1,"why":"The text says modern historians treat those numbers with caution, so the claim may not be exact."},{"skill":"central","q":"Which detail best supports the idea that libraries became open to more people?","c":["Most large collections belonged to rulers.","The Library of Alexandria tried to collect every text.","Towns founded free libraries paid for by taxes.","Fresh riders and horses waited at relay stations."],"a":2,"why":"Free public libraries paid for by taxes let many more people borrow books."},{"skill":"vocab","q":"In paragraph three, what does \"pooled\" mean?","word":"pooled","c":["Spent all at once","Hid in a safe place","Lent to a stranger","Put together"],"a":3,"why":"The text says they pooled their money by combining it to buy books."},{"skill":"monitor","q":"Which inference is best supported by the text?","c":["Cheaper mail helped people learn news from distant places","Ancient Persian riders delivered letters for ordinary families","Franklin's library was free for anyone in the world","Universities stopped keeping books after 1731"],"a":0,"why":"The text says cheap mail let letters, newspapers and ideas circulate far more widely."}]}];
